hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4801","And what Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Yala ibn Umayyah used to sit for an hour in the mosque intending itikaf thereby. So our answer to him in that was that this hadith is not connected to Yala, for Ata only narrates the hadiths of Yala from his father, and we do not know for him any hearing from Yala. And it is rationally understood that whoever sits in the mosque is not thereby a mutakif; were it so, everyone in the mosque would be a mutakif. But in our view - and Allah knows best - what is meant thereby is devotion to the mosque by sitting in it, so he called himself thereby a mutakif. And that itikaf is not the itikaf differed over, whether it is with fasting or without fasting. And Allah Almighty said: Equal is the resident therein and the visitor. That was not about the itikaf we mentioned; it was only about the equality of people in it, that none of them is more entitled to it than another. And Allah we ask for success."
"4802","4160 - So we found Ali ibn Mabad and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq both had narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, Bahz ibn Hakim narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I did not come to you until I swore by the number of these - and he joined the fingers of his two hands - that I would not come to you nor come to your religion. And I have come to you a man who understands nothing except what Allah and His Messenger taught me. And I ask you by the Face of Allah: with what did our Lord Almighty send you to us? He said: With Islam. I said: And what is the sign of Islam? He said: That you say: I have submitted my face to Allah, and withdrawn, and you establish prayer, and give zakah; every Muslim is inviolable to every Muslim, two brothers, two helpers. Allah does not accept from a polytheist who commits shirk after his Islam any deed, unless he leaves the polytheists for the Muslims. How I hold you by your waist-wrappers away from the Fire. Verily my Lord will call me - or is my shepherd - Ibn Marzuq doubted, and Ali said in his hadith: Verily my Lord will call me - without doubt - and will say: Have you conveyed to My servants? So I will say: O Lord, I have conveyed to them, so let your present convey to your absent. Then you will be called with your mouths muzzled with muzzles. Then the first thing that will speak for one of you will be his thigh and his palm. Then I looked at the Prophet of Allah when he struck his thigh with his hand. He said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, is this our religion? He said: This is my religion. Abu Jafar said: Thus Ibrahim said, and Ali said in his hadith: This is your religion, and wherever you do good it will suffice you."
"4803","4161 - And we found Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Harb had narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Sahl ibn Ibrahim al-Araj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, Shibl ibn Abbad al-Makki narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Qazaah narrating to Amr ibn Dinar, from Hakim ibn Muawiyah, from his father, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Muhammad, I swore by the number of my fingers that I would not follow you nor follow your religion. So I adjure you: what has Allah Almighty sent you with? He said: Islam: testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and you establish prayer, and give zakah, two brothers, two helpers. Allah does not accept from anyone a repentance who commits shirk after his Islam. He said: I said: What is the right of one of our wives upon him? He said: He feeds her when he eats and clothes her when he clothes himself, and does not strike the face, nor revile, nor abandon except in the house. He said: And he pointed with his hand toward Syria, and said: Here, to here, you will be gathered riding and walking and upon your faces on the Day of Resurrection, with muzzles upon your mouths. You complete seventy nations; you are the best of them and the noblest of them to Allah Almighty. And the first thing that will speak for one of you will be his thigh. So in this hadith is Muawiyah ibn Haydah informing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he swore by the number of his fingers that he would not come to him and would not come to his religion, and informing along with that that he understands nothing except what Allah Almighty and His Messenger taught him. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not command him any expiation for those oaths of his in which he had broken his oath. So that indicated that there was no expiation upon him in them, and that his swearing in them in his state of shirk is as no swearing. And when that is so in his swearing, it is more so in his vow. And that was also strengthened by what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it."
"4804","4162 - As al-Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Kab al-Halabi narrated to us, Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Abu Harmalah, meaning Abd al-Rahman, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A vow is only what Allah Face is sought with. And we have understood that the polytheist did not seek with his vow in his shirk the Face of Allah the Exalted, so that indicated that there is no meaning to his vow. And that was also strengthened by what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4805","4163 - Of what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas. 4164 - And what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, from Malik. 4165 - And what Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Abd al-Malik al-Ayli, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever vows to obey Allah, let him obey Him; and whoever vows to disobey Allah, let him not disobey Him."
"4806","4166 - And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Talhah ibn Abd al-Malik, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So that indicated that whoever vows what is not obedience to Allah the Exalted, what he vowed is not obligatory upon him. Then an objector said: Then what is the meaning of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to Umar in Islam: Fulfill your vow which was from you in the pre-Islamic period? So our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty success and help, was that it may be upon the meaning of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding him to fulfill to Allah Almighty an obedience by which he obeys Him in Islam in place of the vow which was not obedience, so that what comes from him be a good deed he does in place of what he vowed, of what had he done it in his state of shirk would not have been so. And with Allah is success."
"4807","648 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the shaking of the Throne for the death of Sad ibn Muadh. 4167 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, meaning al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Throne shook for the death of Sad ibn Muadh."
"4808","4168 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Masud narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan and from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4809","4169 - And Muhammad ibn Ali narrated to us, Hawdhah ibn Khalifah narrated to us, Awf narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4810","4170 - And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from a woman of the Ansar called Asma bint Yazid ibn al-Sakan, she said: When the bier of Sad ibn Muadh was brought out, his mother wept and cried out. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Will your tear not dry and your grief not depart, for your son is the first for whom Allah Almighty laughed, and for whom the Throne shook. Abu Jafar said: So in these reports is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing people of the shaking of the Throne for the death of Sad ibn Muadh, but there is not in them his clarifying to them which of the thrones that Throne is. So we considered in that."
"4811","So we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail al-Nahdi narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, Ata ibn al-Saib narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him - and he did not mention in it the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - he said: The Throne shook out of love for Allah meeting of Sad. He said: Then they said: And what is the Throne? He said: Glory be to Allah, its wooden beams - or its crosspieces - have certainly split apart, and it is upon our necks and shoulders. And the last who came out of his grave was the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: Sad was squeezed in his grave with a squeeze, so I asked Allah the Exalted to lighten it for him. And he recited: And he raised his parents upon the throne. He said: The couch."
"4812","4171 - And we found Fahd ibn Sulayman had narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, Abd al-Salam and Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Throne shook out of love for Allah meeting of Sad. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith as Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is that that Throne is the couch upon which Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, was carried."
"4813","4172 - And we found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah had narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from his father, from his grandfather, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came from a journey and alighted at Dhu al-Hulayfah, the children would go out and inform them about their families. And Usayd ibn Hudayr was informed of the death of his wife, so he wept. So it was said to him: Do you weep? So he said: And why should I not weep when I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The wooden beams of the Throne shook for the death of Sad ibn Muadh. Aishah said: And when Sad died, Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, wept until I distinguished the weeping of Abu Bakr from the weeping of Umar, and the weeping of Umar from the weeping of Abu Bakr. Abu Jafar said: So in these two hadiths is that the Throne meant in the first hadiths is the couch upon which Sad ibn Muadh was carried. So we considered in that, and whether whoever said that was opposed in what he said of it or not."
"4814","4173 - So we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd ibn Sulayman both had narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Muadh ibn Rifaa, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Jibril came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Who is this righteous servant who has died, for whom the gates of heaven were opened and for whom the Throne moved? He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, and behold it was Sad ibn Muadh. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat at his grave while he was being buried. And while he was sitting, he said: Glory be to Allah, twice, so the people glorified Allah. Then he said: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, so the people magnified Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For this righteous servant, Allah made it severe upon him in his grave, until this was when relief was granted to him. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is what indicated that the Throne mentioned in the first hadiths is not the couch upon which Sad was carried; for in this hadith is Jibril asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the righteous servant who died for whom the gates of heaven were opened and for whom the Throne moved, and the going out of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at that to know who he is until he knew that he was Sad ibn Muadh. So in that is what indicated that the Throne mentioned in the first hadiths is other than the couch upon which Sad was carried, since Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, had not yet been carried upon the couch upon which he was carried to his grave at that time; rather he was carried upon it after that."
"4815","4174 - And we found Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Muhammad ibn Isa al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Tammar and Man ibn Isa and Abd al-Aziz ibn Imran narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Salih, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Amir ibn Sad, from his father, that Umar said to the mother of Sad ibn Muadh while she was weeping over him: Watch what you say, O mother of Sad. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave her, O Umar; every wailing woman is belied except the mother of Sad; whatever good she says, she will not lie. Then he was carried and placed in his grave, and the color of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed. So the Muslims said: O Messenger of Allah, you used to outpace us, meaning in speed. He said: I feared that the angels would precede us to washing him, as they preceded us to washing Hanzalah ibn Abi Amir. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we saw your color change when you sat at the grave. He said: Sad was compressed in the grave with a compression, and were anyone to be exempted from it, Sad would have been exempted from it. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seventy thousand angels descended to the earth to witness Sad; they had never descended to it before. And all the people of heaven rejoiced at him, and the Throne shook for him. Salih, meaning Ibn Muhammad, said: My father said: A man said to Sad ibn Ibrahim: The Arabs call the couch al-arsh, and it only means the couch of Sad ibn Muadh. So Sad said: The couch of Sad ibn Muadh did not reach that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would mention it. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing, after burying Sad, of the shaking of the Throne for him. So it is possible that that Throne is the Throne which Ibn Umar and Usayd ibn al-Hudayr spoke of, and possible that it is other than it. Then an objector said: How can it be as Ibn Umar and Usayd ibn Hudayr said, when that is only information about a couch that has no soul, and from the like of which the shaking they mentioned of it cannot come. So our answer to him in that was that the couch, if it is as Ibn Umar and Usayd said, then it is possible that Allah Almighty made it understand, after Sad was carried upon it, his place with Allah Almighty and his rank with Him, so it became of the people of knowledge and cognizance thereby, and shook for him as Ibn Umar and Usayd mentioned of its shaking - just as Allah Almighty inspired the wooden trunk to which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to address the people before he took the pulpit; then when he took the pulpit and moved to it from it, there came from it the yearning narrated in that, as we shall mention hereafter in this book of ours, if Allah Almighty wills. And that was a great sign of the signs of prophethood, and a momentous favor by which Allah favored His Messenger, and a great honor by which He honored him. And He inspired whomever He inspired of the grandeur of his place with Him what He inspired him of what is mentioned in this hadith. And it has been narrated that the Throne which shook for the death of Sad ibn Muadh was other than the couch upon which he was carried, and that it was the Throne of the Most Merciful, Almighty."
"4816","4175 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu al-Walid Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Yusuf ibn al-Majishun narrated to us, he said: I heard my father - or: my father narrated to me - from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, from his grandmother Rumaythah."
"4817","And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn al-Majishun narrated to us, from his father, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, from his grandmother Rumaythah, she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and had I wished to kiss the seal between his shoulders from my nearness I could have done so - while he was saying when Sad ibn Muadh died: The Throne of the Most Merciful, Almighty, indeed shook for him. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing the people that the Throne narrated of in its shaking for the death of Sad is the Throne of the Most Merciful, Almighty. And we found the Aws, when they boasted against the Khazraj, boasted against them thereby, and mentioned in their boasting against them that the Throne which shook for the death of their companion is the Throne of the Most Merciful, Almighty."
"4818","As Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Hasan ibn Zabalal al-Madini narrated to us, Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The two tribes, al-Aws and al-Khazraj, boasted. So the first said: Of us is the one washed by the angels, Hanzalah ibn al-Rahib, and of us is he for whom the Throne of the Most Merciful shook, and of us is he whom the wasps protected, Asim ibn Thabit ibn al-Aqlah, and of us is he whose testimony was allowed as the testimony of two men. And the Khazrajis said: Of us are four who collected the Quran, and none other collected it: Ubayy ibn Kab and Muadh ibn Jabal and Abu Zayd and Zayd ibn Thabit. Abu Jafar said: And it is possible that the two thrones together, mentioned in this hadith and in the two hadiths of Ibn Umar and Usayd ibn Hudayr, that was from them both together, and Allah knows best - except that we affirm what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that, and believe in it. And the people of language used to hold that shaking is restfulness and joy, as it is said: So-and-so, when asked, shook, i.e., he looked forward to that and rejoiced at it. So Allah the Exalted inspired the two thrones of the place of Sad with Him, so there came from them both what came of what is mentioned in these hadiths - except that some of them held that that shaking attributed to the Throne was only from the angels who carry it and surround it, and that was attributed to the Throne though they are the ones meant thereby. And they make that like His Almighty saying: So neither heaven nor earth wept over them, meaning: neither the people of heaven nor the people of earth wept over them; and as He said in what He related to us from whom He related of His servants, his saying: And ask the town in which we were and the caravan in which we came; and as the Prophet, peace be upon him, said about Uhud: This is a mountain that loves us and we love it."
"4819","As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Amr, the freedman of al-Muttalib, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that, with the meaning: Its people love us, meaning the Ansar, and we love them. And Allah knows best what His Messenger meant by what he said of that, of what we have related in this chapter and of what besides it of what our knowledge fell short of. And with Allah is success."
"4820","649 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying about what came from the trunk to which he used to address the people, when he moved from it to the pulpit he took to address upon it. 4176 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from al-Tufayl ibn Ubayy ibn Kab, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray toward a trunk when the mosque was a trellis of palm branches, and he used to address toward that trunk. So a man of the Ansar said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we make for you a pulpit upon which you stand on Friday so that the people see you and you make them hear your address? He said: Yes. So he made for him three steps, and they are those upon the pulpit. So when the pulpit was made and placed in the place in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed it, and when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to stand to the pulpit, he passed to it. Then when he passed beyond the trunk to which he used to address, the trunk cried out or bellowed until it cracked and split. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended when he heard the sound of the trunk, and wiped it with his hand until it calmed, and returned to the pulpit. And when he prayed, he used to pray toward it. Then when the mosque was demolished and altered, Ubayy ibn Kab took that trunk, and it was with him in his house until it decayed and the termites ate it and it returned to dust."
"4821","4177 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah al-Basri narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to address toward a trunk. Then when the pulpit was taken, he moved to it, so the trunk yearned until he came to it and embraced it, so it calmed. So he said: Had I not embraced it, it would have yearned until the Day of Resurrection."
"4822","4178 - And we found al-Rabi al-Muradi had narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4823","4179 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Umar ibn Yunus al-Yamami narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Abi Talhah narrated to us, Anas narrated to us that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to stand on Friday and lean his back against a trunk erected in the mosque, addressing the people. So a Byzantine came to him and said: Shall I make for you something upon which you sit as though standing? So he made for him a pulpit with two steps, and he sits upon the third. So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat upon that pulpit, the trunk bellowed like the bellowing of a bull until the mosque shook with its bellowing, out of grief for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended to it and embraced it while it was bellowing. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) embraced it, it fell silent. Then he said: By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, had I not embraced it, it would not have ceased thus until the Day of Resurrection, grieving for the Messenger of Allah. So the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding it, and it was buried."
"4824","4180 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said informed me, Abdullah ibn Hafs ibn Anas informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: There was a trunk to which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would stand. Then when the pulpit was placed, we heard for the trunk like the sounds of pregnant camels in their tenth month, until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended to it and placed his hand upon it."
"4825","4180m - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said informed us, Abdullah ibn Hafs ibn Anas informed me that he heard Jabir saying, then he mentioned the like of it."
"4826","4181 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Hafs ibn Anas informed me that he heard Jabir saying, then he mentioned the like of it."
"4827","4182 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar informed me, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Hafs ibn Anas informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah, then he mentioned the like of it. So Yazid and Husayn agreed upon the name of the man mentioned in this hadith, whose lineage is traced to Hafs ibn Anas, that it is Ubaydullah, while Ibn Abi Dawud opposed them in his hadith, saying Abdullah."
"4828","4183 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Jabir ibn Abdillah al-Ansari - and he did not mention anyone between them - that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to address toward a trunk before the pulpit was made. Then when the pulpit was made, that trunk yearned until we heard its yearning. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and placed his hand upon it until it calmed."
"4829","4184 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Kathir narrated to us, al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4830","4185 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Kamil al-Fudayl ibn al-Husayn al-Jahdari narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Kathir informed us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4831","4186 - And Musab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: My father Ibrahim ibn Hamzah narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Muslim narrated to us, from his uncle Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab, from one who heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying, then he mentioned the like of it."
"4832","4187 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4833","4188 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Umar ibn Ali narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to address toward a wooden beam with a shade over it. So his companions said to him: Were we to make for you a trellis or something like it, so you sit toward it as though standing. So the pulpit was made, and he addressed the people upon it. So the wooden beam yearned with the yearning of a she-camel that has lost her young. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood to it and embraced it, so it fell silent. And they used to say: Had he not embraced it, it would not have fallen silent until the Day of Resurrection."
"4834","4189 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Umar ibn Ali narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Abi Karib, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4835","4190 - And Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Israil, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Karib, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4836","4191 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Karib, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4837","4192 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4838","4193 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ayman narrated to us, he said: I heard my father, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to stand on Friday toward a tree or a palm. So a woman of the Ansar - or a man - said to him: O Messenger of Allah, shall we not make for you a pulpit. He said: If you wish. So they made a pulpit for him. Then when Friday came, he went to the pulpit, so the palm cried out with the crying of a child. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended and drew it to himself; it was moaning with the moaning of a child being quieted. It was weeping for what it used to hear of remembrance beside it."
"4839","4194 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Said ibn Aws al-Nahwi narrated to us, al-Salt ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to address toward a trunk in Madinah. Then he moved to the pulpit when it was made for him, so the trunk yearned with the yearning of a she-camel. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended to the trunk and embraced it until it calmed."
"4840","4195 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard my father, he said: I heard Abu Nadrah, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to stand beside a tree or a trunk or a piece of wood or something, addressing, leaning upon it. He said: Then thereafter a pulpit was taken, so he took to standing upon it. So that which he used to stand beside yearned with a yearning heard by the people of the mosque. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to it, and he either said: he wiped it, or said: he touched it, or as he said."
"4841","4196 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Lahiah informed us, Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated to me that he heard Abbas ibn Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi informing from his father that he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed, he would stand toward a wooden beam with two prongs that was in the mosque. Then when the people grew many, it was said to him: O Messenger of Allah, were you to make a pulpit overlooking the people, for they have grown many. He said: I do not mind. And there was in Madinah a carpenter called Maymun. He said: So he sent to the carpenter, and he went and I went with him until we came to al-Khaniqayn, and we cut palm wood from it, and he made it. So by Allah, it was only that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat upon it and spoke, and the wooden beam missed him and bellowed as a bull bellows, with a moaning for it. He said: So Abbas took to stretching his hands to conceal the yearning of the beam, until the people took fright and weeping grew much from what they saw in it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Glory be to Allah, do you not see this beam; pull it out and place it under the pulpit in the ground. So they pulled it out and buried it under the pulpit. So in these reports is what is mentioned therein of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to address toward, when he moved from it to the pulpit, of yearning toward him, and of what besides that mentioned in these reports, of what Allah originated in it until He made the people hear from it what He made them hear of what comes from possessors of souls among the children of Adam and others. And in these reports about the trunk is what is mentioned therein of its burial by the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and of Ubayy taking it and that it remained with him until it became dust, and of mention of the place in which it was buried, that it is under the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And that is not a contradiction, for it is permissible that Ubayy taking it was after it was buried, so that it be with him in a state more protective for it than burial, and he was not prevented from that for this meaning, so it remained with him until it decayed and became dust. And Allah knows best the reality of what was in that - except that in these reports is that Allah the Exalted originated in that trunk what He originated therein of what the people witnessed of it, of what was unimagined from its like until Allah Almighty originated it in it, and made it a sign of the signs of the prophethood of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his virtue, to be an alerting to people to know his place with Him, Majestic and Mighty. And likewise what came from it in Hira when it moved while he was upon it, and others of his companions besides him, among them Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and of his saying to it when it quaked with them: Be still, Hira, for upon you is only a prophet or a truthful one or a martyr. And we shall mention that chapter and what was narrated in it hereafter in this book of ours, if Allah Almighty wills. So like that is what was narrated about the couch of Sad, of its shaking, upon what whoever narrated it therein narrated - it is likewise for the like of this meaning. And Allah, Glorious and Exalted, knows best."
"4842","650 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: When the prayer is established, do not stand until you see me. 4197 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Hisham al-Dastuwai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah narrated to him, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the call for prayer is made, do not stand until you see me. So we considered this hadith and found Hisham did not hear it from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir; rather he narrated it from him, from his writing of it to him."
"4843","4198 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi narrated to us, Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from Hisham, he said: Yahya wrote to me narrating, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from Abu Qatadah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the prayer is established, do not stand until you see me. Abu Jafar said: Then we considered whether anyone besides Hisham narrated this hadith from Yahya, by other than this route by which Hisham narrated it from him."
"4844","4199 - So we found Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub and Hajjaj al-Sawwaf, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith."
"4845","4200 - And we found Ibn Abi Maryam had narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan, and he is al-Thawri, narrated to us, from Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4846","4201 - And we found Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil had narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us. 4202 - And we found al-Muzani had narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, then they both said together: from Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this hadith."
"4847","4203 - And we found Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Talq ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Shayban, meaning al-Nahwi, narrated to us. And we found Abu al-Darda Hashim ibn Muhammad al-Ansari had narrated to us, he said: Adam Abu Iyas narrated to us, Shayban Abu Muawiyah narrated to us - Abu Umayyah said in his hadith: from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, and Abu al-Darda said in his hadith: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir informed us - from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4848","4204 - And we found Sulayman ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Then we considered whether this hadith was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by other than this route."
"4849","4205 - So we found Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghazwan informed us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Jarir ibn Hazim, from Thabit, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it or its like. Abu Jafar said: So if this hadith is preserved, it has come from Abu Qatadah and from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And the hadith of Abu Qatadah had come from a group, among them Yahya. So we reflected upon this hadith and found the standing of people for prayer before the presence of their imam is of no need to them, for they do not enter prayer except with the entering of their imam into it or after his entering into it. So that standing of theirs was superfluous, so they were forbidden it. And in that is what indicated that people do not enter prayer before their imam enters it. And the like of that is what has been narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in it."
"4850","As Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Abu Khalid al-Walibi, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us while the prayer had been established and we were standing awaiting him, so he said: Why do I see you samidin. Abu Jafar said: And sumud in the view of the people of language is diversion. Thus Abu Ubaydah said, as Wallad narrated to us, he said: al-Musadiri narrated from him; and likewise al-Farra said. So they were forbidden to be diverted. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"4851","651 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated about the imam in the prayer which was the last of the prayers of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in which he prayed sitting while Abu Bakr prayed in it standing and the people prayed standing: who was the imam in it of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. 4206 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became heavy, Bilal came to announce prayer to him. So he said: Bring Abu Bakr, let him pray with the people. She said: So I said: O Messenger of Allah, were you to command Umar to pray with them, for Abu Bakr is a tender-hearted man, and whenever he stands in your place, he will not make the people hear. He said: Command Abu Bakr, let him pray with the people. So they commanded Abu Bakr, and he prayed with the people. Then when he entered prayer, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) found lightness in himself, so he stood supported between two men, his feet dragging on the ground. Then when Abu Bakr heard his movement, he went to step back, so he gestured to him to pray as you are. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came until he sat to the left of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying with the people, while Abu Bakr was following the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing, and the people were following the prayer of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4852","4207 - Fahd narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, Musa ibn Abi Aishah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, and he is Ibn Utbah, he said: I entered upon Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: Will you not narrate to me about the illness of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She said: Yes. The people were gathered in the mosque awaiting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the last night prayer. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to Abu Bakr to pray with the people, and he used to pray with them those days. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) found lightness in himself, so he went out supported between two men for the noon prayer, while Abu Bakr was praying with the people. Then when Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, saw him, he went to step back, so he gestured to him not to step back, and said to them both: Seat me beside him. So they seated him beside Abu Bakr. So Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, took to praying, standing, with the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while the people prayed with the prayer of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting. Ubaydullah said: So I entered upon Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and presented her hadith to him, and he did not deny anything of that. Abu Jafar said: So in these two hadiths is that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was praying with the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And in the first of them is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying with the people, while Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was following him, standing, and the people praying, meaning with the prayer of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. And in the second of them is that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, took to praying with them, standing, praying with the prayer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while the people prayed with the prayer of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting. And in this second hadith is the agreement of Ibn Abbas with Aishah upon what is in it. And when what was narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in that counterbalanced, it was lifted, and what was narrated from Ibn Abbas in it was established. Then we considered whether anything besides these two hadiths was narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in that or not."
"4853","4208 - So we found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Shabbah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Nuaym ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Wail, from Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in his illness in which he died behind Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sitting."
"4854","4209 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: I heard Shubah mentioning, from Nuaym ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Wail, from Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed with the people while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the row. So in these two hadiths is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in that prayer praying with the prayer of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, as one led in it. And we considered the statement of Ibn Abbas and Aishah: Abu Bakr was praying with the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So we found that is possible to mean by their saying that that he was praying with the prayer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the extent of his capacity, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), upon it, for the illness that was in him; for his capacity for prayer in it is not like the capacity of others for it among those with no illness like his illness that was in him. And of his sunnah, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which he commanded the imams of people, is to measure people in their prayer by the prayer of their weakest."
"4855","4210 - As al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Said ibn Abi Hind, from Mutarrif ibn Abdullah, he said: I heard Uthman ibn Abi al-As saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to lead people and to measure them by their weakest, for among them is the old, the sick, the weak, and the one with a need. So the prayer of Abu Bakr with the prayer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is only his measuring it and his praying with people the like of it, and his leaving exceeding by his capacity in it to what is above that. So that was his praying with his prayer, by nothing besides that. And this was the worthiest of what this meaning is carried upon; for the people in that prayer had no imam in it except one imam, not two imams. And when in it is that Abu Bakr was the imam with people in it, other than the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it is necessary that he be the imam in it for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also. And the hadith of Masruq from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, verified that, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in that prayer behind Abu Bakr. And the hadith of Musa ibn Abi Aishah has been narrated by other than the route of Zaidah, with a meaning additional to what is in the hadith of Zaidah."
"4856","4211 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to me, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi Aishah, he said: I heard Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah narrating from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Abu Bakr to pray with the people. She said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in front of Abu Bakr, praying sitting, while Abu Bakr prayed with the people, and the people behind Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. So in this hadith is what some people claimed indicated to him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in that prayer an imam, and that Abu Bakr was in it one led; for in it is that he was in front of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. So our answer to him in that was that there is no indication for him thereby upon what he mentioned it indicated to him, since of the people of knowledge are those who say: it is permissible for the one led to pray in front of the imam as he prays behind him. And of those who said that of them is Malik ibn Anas. And when that is so, there is not in what is in this hadith of that meaning a proof for him upon what he mentioned, besides that there has been narrated in what is besides these hadiths what verified that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in that prayer one led."
"4857","4212 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr al-Azdi narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell ill, so he said: Command Abu Bakr, let him pray with the people. So Aishah said: Abu Bakr is a tender man. So he said: Command Abu Bakr, let him pray with the people, for you are the companions of Yusuf. He said: So Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, led in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So this one arguing by what we mentioned, his arguing thereby, said: There is no proof for you in this hadith of Abu Musa, for it is permissible that this imamate of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, is only meant thereby the prayers in which he led people in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before the prayer in which this dispute occurred about the imam in it with people, who it was of him and of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. So our answer to him in that was that in the hadith of Abu Musa is of their addressing the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressing them the like of his addressing them and their addressing him in the hadith of al-Aswad from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her - besides that we have found of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides Ibn Abbas and Aishah and Abu Musa, and he is Anas ibn Malik, verified that the imam in that prayer was Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4858","4213 - As Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ruayni narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, Humayd narrated to me, Thabit al-Bunani narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed behind Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, in a single garment, a cloak, differing between its two ends. So it was the last prayer he prayed."
"4859","4214 - And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Ali ibn Hujr informed us, Ismail ibn Jafar informed us, Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The last prayer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the people, he prayed it in a single garment, wrapped, behind Abu Bakr - and he did not mention in its chain Thabit. And how is it permissible for one to be an imam for others in a prayer which that other entered before him, when his entering into it was an entering that obliges upon him in his forgetfulness in it of prostration what, were he one led, would not oblige him, and his entering into it as an imam obliges upon him of recitation in it, in the view of whoever holds that he was the imam in it, what does not oblige him in it when he is one led; for the imam in his view and in the view of others recites in the first two of that prayer, in every rakah of them, the Opening of the Book and a surah, while when he is one led, it is incumbent upon him in his view to recite in each single rakah of them the Opening of the Book without a surah. And how is it permissible to exit from a prayer whose ruling is this to another prayer whose ruling is the opposite of this ruling, without a takbir he newly begins for it. And how is that thought of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, when of the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which he taught him and others of his companions besides him is not to precede their imams in bowing nor in prostration in their prayer which they pray with them, and to be following them in that, not opposing them in it. Then if an objector says: But the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a prayer other than this prayer of his prayers commenced takbir with people, then mentioned that he was in a state of major impurity, so gestured to them to remain in their places until he went and washed, then returned and prayed with them. So our answer to him in that was that this is a hadith narrated from three of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with them - and they are Anas ibn Malik and Abu Hurayrah and Abu Bakrah. Of them is whoever mentioned in his hadith that what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it, of his mentioning impurity, was before he commenced takbir for it; and when he had not commenced takbir for it, those behind him are more deserving not to have commenced takbir for it. And in that is what necessitates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he returned having washed, began prayer with them anew. And of them is whoever mentioned in his hadith that his mentioning that was after he had commenced takbir and entered prayer. And it is possible that the people had commenced takbir, and possible that they had not commenced takbir, so they had not entered prayer until the Prophet came from his washing and prayed with them with a takbir he began anew and with a takbir they began anew. So it is established thereby that there is no proof in this hadith for whoever argues thereby for the people entering prayer before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entering it. And we have mentioned this chapter with its chains and with the differences in it in what preceded from us in this book of ours. And in what we mentioned in this chapter is what prevents the one led entering prayer before another entering it, then returning led by that other whose entering that prayer was after his entering it. And Allah the Exalted we ask for success."
"4860","652 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated about the leaving of the one praying behind Muadh ibn Jabal to the prayer of himself: was it with a newly begun takbir or by building upon his entering with Muadh. 4215 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, and Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: Muadh ibn Jabal used to pray with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the night prayer - or he said: the darkness prayer - then return and pray it with his people in Banu Salimah. So the Prophet, peace be upon him, delayed the night prayer - or he said: the darkness prayer - one night, so Muadh prayed with him, then returned and led his people, reciting Surah al-Baqarah. So a man withdrew from behind him. So it was said to him: Have you become a hypocrite? He said: No, but I shall come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and inform him. So he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said to him: O Messenger of Allah, you delayed the last night prayer, and Muadh prayed with you, then returned and led us, opening with Surah al-Baqarah. So when I saw that, I stepped back and prayed. And we are but people of water-camels, working with our hands. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to Muadh and said: Are you a trial, O Muadh, are you a trial, O Muadh! Recite such-and-such surah and such-and-such surah."
"4861","4216 - al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, and Bakkar narrated to us, Ibrahim narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir the like of it, adding that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Recite: Glorify the Name of your Lord, the Most High, and: By the night when it covers, and: By the heaven and the night-comer, and the like of them. Sufyan said: So I said to Amr ibn Dinar: Abu al-Zubayr says: And he said: Recite by: Glorify the Name of your Lord, the Most High, and: By the night when it covers, and: By the heaven and the night-comer. He said: So Amr said: It is this or the like of this. Then an objector said: This indicates that this man exited from the prayer of Muadh to the prayer of himself without beginning a takbir anew. And in this chapter is also another hadith from Jabir, besides the hadith of Amr and Abu al-Zubayr from him."
"4862","4217 - And it is what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Dawud, the companion of al-Tayalisah, narrated to us, Talib ibn Habib ibn Amr ibn Sahl al-Ansari narrated to us, called Ibn al-Daji, the bedfellow of Hamzah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir, from Jabir, he said: Hazm ibn Ubayy ibn Kab passed by Muadh ibn Jabal while he was praying the sunset prayer with his people, opening with a long surah, while Hazm had a water-camel with him. So he stepped back and prayed, praying well, then came to his water-camel. Then he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. So they said: O Messenger of Allah, he is of the most righteous of those he is of. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Muadh: Do not be a trial - he said it three times - for there prays behind you the old and the weak and the one with a need and the ailing. So what this objector said, of what we mentioned from him, has no reality with it; for it is possible that that man entered the prayer of himself with a takbir he began anew for himself. And Allah knows best the reality of what the matter was upon in that. So this objector said: But there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his praying with people the fear prayer on the day of Dhat al-Riqa, what indicated the like of this also."
"4863","4218 - And he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him. And what al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Yazid ibn Ruman, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from one who prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Dhat al-Riqa the fear prayer, that a group lined with him, and a group facing the enemy. So he prayed with those with him a rakah, then remained standing, and they completed for themselves. Then they left and lined facing the enemy, and the other group came. So he prayed with them the rakah that remained of his prayer, then remained sitting, and they completed for themselves, then he gave salutation with them."
"4864","4219 - And what Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mumin al-Marwazi narrated to us, Abdan ibn Uthman ibn Jabalah narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Shubah, from Yahya ibn Said and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Salih ibn Khawwat, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah - Shubah said: Abd al-Rahman raised it, while Yahya ibn Said did not raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, and a row stood behind him, and a row facing the enemy. So he prayed with those behind him a rakah and two prostrations, then stood until they prayed a rakah to their rakah. Then these went to the place of the others, and the others came to the place of these. So he prayed a rakah and two prostrations, then sat until they prayed another rakah, then gave salutation upon them. He said: So in this hadith is that the first group which had entered with the Prophet, peace be upon him, at the beginning of his prayer had exited from following him to the prayer of themselves, praying it before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed. So in that is what indicated the permissibility of the one led exiting from the prayer of his imam to the prayer of himself. So our answer to him in that was that this prayer which has been narrated in this hadith, that it was on the day of Dhat al-Riqa with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with his witnessing what was from the people in it, and of his leaving rebuke in that upon them, has been narrated that it was on that day contrary to what is in this hadith."
"4865","4220 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu Salamah Musa ibn Ismail al-Minqari narrated to us, Aban ibn Yazid al-Attar narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhat al-Riqa. So the prayer was established, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with a group two rakahs, and they stepped back, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the other group. So for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) there were four rakahs, and for the people two rakahs. And this is contrary to what is in the hadith of Yazid ibn Ruman and al-Qasim ibn Muhammad from Salih ibn Khawwat. And when the two narrations in that counterbalanced, they were both lifted; and when they were both lifted, there is no proof in either of them for whoever argues thereby against his opponent, since his opponent may argue against him by the other of them. And when that is so, there is not in anything of what we narrated in this chapter what indicates that it is for anyone to exit from the prayer of his imam to the prayer of himself without a takbir he begins anew for it. And with Allah is success."
"4866","653 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the reason for which Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed in his hajj with people at Mina four. 4221 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Rabi ibn Tariq al-Hilali narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi al-Mawsili narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Abi Dhubab narrated to us, from his father, from Uthman ibn Affan, that he prayed with the people of Mina four rakahs. Then when he gave salutation, he turned to them and said: I took a wife in Makkah, and I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever takes a wife in a town, he is of its people, so let him pray four. So for that I prayed four."
"4867","4222 - Ismail ibn Hamdawayh narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah, the freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, from Ibn Abi Dhubab, from his father, from Uthman ibn Affan, that he prayed with the people of Mina four, so the people disapproved that of him. So he said: O people, when I came to Makkah, I took a wife in it, and I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When a man takes a wife in a town, let him pray the prayer of a resident. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is what indicated what Abu Hanifah and his companions and al-Shafii say, that the imam, when he is of the people of Makkah, and whoever of the pilgrims is of its people, do not shorten prayer at Mina; for they are in a travel short of the travel in whose like prayer is shortened. And Ata and Mujahid preceded them in this statement, and they are the two imams of people in hajj."
"4868","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Ata and Mujahid, they both said: There is no shortening upon the people of Makkah in hajj. And analogy necessitates this also; for the shortening of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, of prayer with people at Mina in their hajj is not devoid of one of three meanings: that the travel they were in is of what prayer is shortened in its like, or that it was for the hajj they were in, or that it was for the homeland they were in - there is no aspect for that in it besides these three aspects we mentioned. So we considered that: was that shortening for the homeland? So we found all the people of knowledge do not differ that whoever is not a pilgrim does not shorten prayer in that homeland. So we understood thereby that the shortening which was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his two companions in that prayer was not for the homeland. Then we returned to that shortening: was it for the hajj? So we found them all do not differ that the pilgrim of the people of Mina do not shorten that prayer at Mina. So we understood thereby that that prior shortening was not for the hajj they were in. And when these two meanings were negated and exited from being the shortening in that prayer for either of them, and there remained only the other aspect, namely travel, we understood thereby that the shortening which was in that prayer was for travel, not for what is besides it. And likewise Malik, may Allah have mercy on him, used to say about the pilgrim of the people of Mina: they do not shorten prayer in it, while the people of Makkah and the people of Arafah shorten prayer in it, and the people of Mina shorten prayer at Arafah. And when it is negated that prayer was shortened by whoever shortened it for other than travel, there is thereby negated the statement of whoever says that other than the traveler shortens it at Mina, pilgrim or not. Then we considered al-Harith ibn Abi Dhubab, to whom this hadith returns: is there in his age what indicates that what he narrated from Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, in it is hearing."
"4869","So we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yaqub ibn Utbah, from Yazid ibn Hurmuz, from al-Harith ibn Abi Dhubab al-Dawsi, he said: When it was the year of ar-Ramadah, Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, took charity until, when people lived from the next year, he sent to them collectors, and sent me among them. So he said: Take from them the two iqals: the iqal we delayed from them and the iqal that became due upon them. Then divide upon them one of the two iqals, then bring down to me the other. He said: So I collected. So we understood thereby that in his age is above what we sought in it; for when he was of the governors of Umar, he was in the time of Uthman above many of those who narrated from Uthman in ages. And Allah we ask for success."
"4870","654 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Abdullah ibn Umar about his wife whom he divorced while she was menstruating, to take her back, then when she is pure, divorce her while she is pure or pregnant. 4223 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, the freedman of the family of Talhah, from Salim, narrating, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: It was said to the Prophet, peace be upon him: Ibn Umar divorced his wife while she was menstruating. He said: Let him take her back; then when she is pure, let him divorce her while she is pure or pregnant."
"4871","4224 - And Fahd narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, and Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, they said: Waki narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, the freedman of the family of Talhah, from Salim, from Ibn Umar the like of it. So this hadith was of what a group of the people of knowledge argued with, of those whose school is that the pregnant woman does not menstruate, for their school: when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Ibn Umar to divorce her after he takes her back while she is pure or pregnant, that indicated that there is no menstruation in pregnancy; for had there been menstruation in it, he would not have commanded him to divorce her in a state in which she might be menstruating. And in his commanding him to divorce her in that state, without saying to him: not menstruating, is what indicated that there is no menstruation in pregnancy. And those who opposed them in that said: This speech you mentioned in this hadith is impossible; for he did not divorce her while she was pure, then he mentioned the place of purity in which is the place of that divorce, then said: or pregnant - and the pregnant is a place for divorce. So necessity did not call to mention pregnancy; for what is intended by the sunnah divorce is purity. And when pregnancy has no menstruation in it, it is purity, and speech with it is superfluous, and mention of the purity before it would dispense with mentioning it. And far be it from Allah Almighty that there be in the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what is not needed and what has no benefit in it. So of our answer to this speaker, on behalf of those whom he addressed with this address, was that in this speech attributed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the utmost benefit. That is that the pure woman is not divorced in her purity except that she be not had intercourse with in it, while the pregnant may be divorced in her pregnancy whether she was had intercourse with in it or not; for intercourse with the pure is an intercourse from which pregnancy may come, while intercourse with the pregnant is an intercourse from which no pregnancy comes. So the ruling of purity with which there is no pregnancy and the ruling of purity by pregnancy differ in this meaning we mentioned, in what they vary in and differ in. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Ibn Umar to divorce her pure, a purity in which he had not had intercourse with her. And though it was not conveyed to us in this hadith, it has been conveyed to us in another."
"4872","4225 - As Fahd narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Abu al-Malih al-Raqqi narrated to us, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he divorced his wife during her menstruation. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to take her back until she is pure; then when she is pure, if he wishes he keeps her, and if he wishes he divorces before he has intercourse. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) negated intercourse from the purity in which he commanded him with divorce in it, and commanded him that his divorcing her be while she is pure, not had intercourse with. And he did not negate intercourse from the pregnant; for intercourse with the pregnant does not prevent divorcing her according to the sunnah. So it became clear, by Allah praise and favor, that what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what is mentioned from him in the hadith of Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, is of the utmost benefit. And of what also indicates that the pregnant does not menstruate is what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what preceded from us in this book of ours, in his commanding, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about captives that no pregnant of them be had intercourse with until she gives birth, and that no non-pregnant of them be had intercourse with until she menstruates. So it was understood from him, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), thereby that he meant that menstruation, when it is known thereby that there is no pregnancy, the intercourse which was not lawful were there pregnancy becomes lawful. And because had menstruation not negated pregnancy, menstruation and purity would both be with one meaning. But it is contrary to that; for when there is menstruation, it is known that there is no pregnancy with it. So this is a sound proof that menstruation does not occur with pregnancy. Then an objector said: But it has been narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the pregnant menstruates, and he mentioned in that."
"4873","What al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, and what Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, my father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abdillah ibn al-Ashajj, from Umm Alqamah, the freedwoman of Aishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was asked about the pregnant who sees blood. So she said: She does not pray. So our answer to him in that was that this hadith has been narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, as he mentioned, yet contrary to this statement has been narrated from her in another hadith, and it is."
"4874","What Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Said, meaning Ibn Abi Arubah, from Matar, meaning al-Warraq, from Ata, from Aishah, about the pregnant who sees blood, he said: She does not leave prayer."
"4875","And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, Matar al-Warraq narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about the pregnant who sees blood, she said: She washes and prays."
"4876","And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Hibban ibn Hilal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Musa narrated to us, from Ata, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The pregnant does not menstruate; so when she sees blood, let her wash and pray. So this was in our view from Aishah worthier than what we mentioned from her of what opposes that, for the eminence of Ata and his place in knowledge, and because the place of Umm Alqamah in knowledge is not so. Then an objector said: But Amrah narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in that what agrees with what Umm Alqamah narrated from her in it. So our answer to him in that was that we did not find that from Amrah sound; rather we found it from the narration of the people of the house, from Yahya ibn Said, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, without mention of Amrah in it."
"4877","As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Aishah, that she said: The pregnant, when she sees blood, let her refrain from prayer, for it is menstruation."
"4878","And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, he said: I narrated to Yahya ibn Said the hadith of Matar al-Warraq, meaning what we mentioned in this chapter, so he denied it and said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: She does not pray. Then this statement has also been said - I mean in that the pregnant does not leave prayer upon seeing blood - by Ata ibn Abi Rabah and al-Hasan al-Basri."
"4879","As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Jami ibn Abi Rashid, he said: I heard Ata, asked about the pregnant who sees blood, he said: She performs ablution and prays."
"4880","And as Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Yunus informed us, from al-Hasan, about the pregnant who sees blood, he said: She is in the position of the mustahadah; she washes every day one washing, from noon to noon."
"4881","And as Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Jami ibn Abi Rashid, from Ata, he said: She is in the position of the mustahadah; she washes every day, from noon to noon."
"4882","So this is the statement in our view, for what we argued for it by the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and analogy. And it is the statement of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, as Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, Yaqub narrated to us, from Abu Hanifah, about the pregnant who sees blood, he said: She prays. And no disagreement between him and any of his companions has been related in it. And Allah we ask for success."
"4883","655 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who has intercourse with his wife while she is menstruating. 4226 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Bakr ibn Khalaf narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Shubah, al-Hakam narrated to us, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about one who comes to his wife while she is menstruating, he said: He gives in charity a dinar or half a dinar. So we examined this hadith in its chain to know its reality, how it is."
"4884","So we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, al-Hakam informed me, from Abd al-Hamid, the governor of Kufah, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas - and he did not raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - about one who comes to his wife while she is menstruating, he said: He gives in charity a dinar or half a dinar. Shubah said: al-Hakam doubted. So we established thereby that al-Hakam had not narrated this hadith to Shubah from Miqsam as hearing from him, and that he only took it from Abd al-Hamid, from Miqsam, so he concealed it thereby. Then we considered whether anyone besides Shubah narrated this hadith from al-Hakam or not."
"4885","So we found Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh had narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both - and he did not mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - about one who comes to his wife while she is menstruating, he said: He gives in charity a dinar, and if he does not find, then half a dinar. So in this hadith is the agreement of Abu Awanah with Shubah in what Yazid ibn Zuray narrated from him, and the agreement of Hajjaj in what he narrated from Shubah, of stopping this hadith upon Ibn Abbas."
"4886","And we found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Ajlah, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both - and he did not mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - about one who falls upon his wife while she is menstruating, he said: He gives in charity half a dinar. So al-Ajlah also agreed with Abu Awanah in the chain of this hadith from al-Hakam. Then we considered whether anyone besides al-Hakam narrated it from Miqsam."
"4887","4227 - So we found Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded a man who had intercourse with his wife while she was menstruating to give in charity a dinar or half a dinar. Then we considered whether Qatadah narrated to Said this hadith from Miqsam by his hearing it from him or by what is besides that."
"4888","4228 - So we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Ubadah ibn Suhayb narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abd al-Hamid, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a man had intercourse with his wife while she was menstruating, so he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he commanded him to give in charity a dinar or half a dinar. So we established thereby that Qatadah only narrated this hadith to Said from Miqsam by concealment, not by his hearing it from him. Then we considered whether Qatadah heard it from Abd al-Hamid or not."
"4889","4229 - So we found al-Hajjaj ibn Imran ibn al-Fadl al-Mazini al-Basri Abu Abdillah had narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hammad ibn al-Jad narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Hamid, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about one who comes to his wife while she is menstruating, he said: He gives in charity a dinar or half a dinar. So we established thereby that Qatadah did not hear it from Abd al-Hamid, for he only narrated it from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Hamid. And Allah knows best whether he heard it from al-Hakam or not. Then we considered whether anyone besides Abd al-Hamid narrated it from Miqsam."
"4890","4230 - So we found al-Hasan ibn Abdillah ibn Mansur al-Balisi had narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, and we found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us - al-Hasan said: Sharik narrated to us, and Fahd said: Sharik informed us - from Khusayf, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about one who falls upon his wife while she is menstruating, he said: He gives in charity half a dinar."
"4891","And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Khusayf al-Jazari informed us, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both - and he did not mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - about one who has intercourse with his wife while she is menstruating, he said: He gives in charity a dinar or half a dinar. Abu Jafar said: So the hadith of Khusayf, this, was of what we did not find any disturbance in its chain, but there occurred in it between Hammad and Sharik in its text of difference what we mentioned in their two narrations: Sharik raised it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while Hammad agreed upon Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both. Then we considered whether anyone besides those we mentioned narrated it from Miqsam."
"4892","4231 - So we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Mihran al-Dabbagh narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both - Sufyan said: I think - from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When a man comes to his wife while she is menstruating, in fresh flowing blood, he gives in charity a dinar; and if it is yellowness, then half a dinar."
"4893","4232 - And we found Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn Ayan al-Baghdadi had narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, Abu Jafar al-Razi informed us, from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. So this hadith was narrated by Ibn Uyaynah from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, and he is acceptable in narration, while Abu Jafar al-Razi narrated it from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, and he is obscure in his narration. And both narrated it from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas. And Ibn Uyaynah doubted whether Abd al-Karim raised it to him or not, while Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah did not doubt that it is raised. Then we considered whether anyone besides Miqsam narrated it from Ibn Abbas."
"4894","4233 - So we found al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi had narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Tamim narrated to me that he heard Ali ibn Budhaymah al-Jazari saying: I heard Said ibn Jubayr saying: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: A man informed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he came to his wife while she was menstruating, so he commanded him to free a soul. Ibn Abbas said: And the value of a soul on that day was a dinar. So as though this hadith returned to Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Tamim, and he is not like whoever narrated this hadith besides him of those we mentioned in what preceded from us in this chapter. And we uncovered the states of this Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, and found al-Bukhari mentioned that he is a man of the people of Syria, and that he narrates rejected hadiths, and that he had come to Kufah, so more than one of its people wrote from him, attributing him to Jabir, saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, while they deem him Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, though he is not him."
"4895","4234 - And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata al-Attar, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about one who has intercourse with his wife while she is menstruating, he said: He gives in charity a dinar, and if he does not find, then half a dinar. And this Ata was in the view of the people of knowledge of chains Abu Yazid ibn Ata - except that al-Bukhari attributed him to cloth, not to perfume. And it is possible that he was a perfumer and a cloth-merchant, so some attributed him to cloth and some attributed him to perfume."
"4896","4235 - And we found al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi had narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata al-Attar, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Then we considered whether anyone besides the Iraqis also narrated this hadith from Abd al-Hamid or not."
"4897","4236 - So we found al-Hasan ibn Abdillah ibn Mansur al-Balisi had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yazid ibn Abi Malik, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Zayd, meaning Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab - and he did not go beyond him with it. He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab had a wife who disliked intercourse, so when he wanted her, she would plead menstruation. So he thought it is not as she says, so he fell upon her, and behold she was menstruating. So he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he commanded him to give in charity two-fifths of a dinar. So in this hadith, of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to give in charity is less than what is in the first hadiths. And the first hadiths were worthier in our view than this hadith, for the firmness of their narrators and for their exceeding in amount Yazid ibn Abi Malik. Then we considered whether anyone also narrated this hadith from Miqsam besides those we mentioned."
"4898","4237 - So we found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Bakr, meaning Ibn Ayyash, narrated to us, from Ibn Ata, meaning Yaqub, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about one who falls upon his wife while she is menstruating: He gives in charity a dinar or half a dinar. Then we reflected upon this hadith and found it, when established, that what is in it is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding whoever falls into the cause mentioned in it with charity of the amount mentioned in it. So we understood thereby that that charity he commanded him with is a nearness to Allah Almighty. So it is possible that it be an expiation for what was from him, and possible that it be a nearness, not because it is an expiation - just as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded charity at the eclipse of the sun, to be a nearness, not an expiation."
"4899","4238 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sun and the moon are two signs of the signs of Allah, Blessed and Exalted; they do not eclipse for the death of anyone nor for his life. So when you see that, supplicate Allah Almighty, magnify Allah, and give charity. And it is the worthier of the two possibilities. Then an objector said: And why was what you interpreted in that charity, of nearness, worthier than expiation? So our answer to him in that was that we found the expiations Allah Almighty commanded with in His Book and upon the tongue of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): of them is what He mixed fasting with others in, and it is the verse of recompense for game. So Allah Almighty said: Then a recompense like what he killed of livestock. And the like of it also is the verse of ransom in the pilgrim shaving his head from hurt, and it is His saying: Then whoever of you is ill or has hurt in his head, then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice. And of them is what He commanded in His Book in expiations of oaths upon lacking a neck and clothing and feeding, and it is His Almighty saying: Then whoever does not find, then fasting three days. So that was fasting transferred upon lacking, as a substitute for what is before it of what is not fasting. And the like of that is what Allah Almighty made of fasting as a substitute for the expiation for mistaken killing, by His saying: Then whoever does not find, then fasting two consecutive months. And the like of that is the expiation of zihar for whoever does not find a neck, by fasting two consecutive months; then if he cannot, he feeds sixty poor. And the like of that is what He clarified to us upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who has intercourse in his fasting deliberately, of what is like that. So what Allah Almighty made an expiation, He mixed it with fasting or made for it a substitute of fasting. While what He commanded the one having intercourse in the state of menstruation with, He did not mix with fasting, nor make fasting a substitute for it upon lacking, as He commanded charity upon finding. So we understood thereby that what He commanded, of our mention, for intercourse in menstruation, was charity of nearness, not charity of expiation. Then an objector said: But we have seen the pilgrim having intercourse in his pilgrimage, so upon him is blood, without fasting with it and without fasting as a substitute for it upon lacking it. So what do you deny that likewise the charity the Prophet, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), commanded with in intercourse in menstruation be an expiation with no substitute for it. So our answer to him in that was that what he mentioned, of blood in intercourse without a substitute for it of fasting and without intermixing of fasting with it, is only said by the Kufans. And they have in that opponents of the people of knowledge besides them. Of them is Malik ibn Anas, may Allah have mercy on him: he used to say about intercourse in pilgrimage that in it is a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice, like the obligatory in shaving the head in pilgrimage from hurt. And of them is al-Shafii, may Allah have mercy on him: he used to say that it necessitates blood in this, then the blood is valued, and its price is spent after inability for blood, as the like of it is spent in recompense for game struck by the pilgrim in his pilgrimage. And what Malik ibn Anas said in that was in our view the worthiest of what was said in it; for pilgrimage forbade intercourse and forbade shaving the head and forbade clothing. And whoever did anything of that without need to it is sinful, while whoever did it with need to it is not sinful. And the expiations obligatory in that are upon the act, not what is besides it - except that when they are by occurrence upon need, there is no sin with them, while when they are upon other than need, then with them is sin. So expiation was obligatory for the act, not for what is besides it. And killing game is violating a sanctity other than bodies, while shaving hair is violating the sanctity of the body. So some causes of the body are more like some causes of the body than the game which is not of the causes of the body. And when what we mentioned is so, there is not in what this arguer argued with against us a proof for him in what he argued with against us. Then we considered whether anyone before these differers in this meaning, of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), preceded them."
"4900","Then we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: A woman came to him and said: I went out with my husband, and we entered ihram for umrah. So I circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then he fell upon me before I shortened. So he said: Intense desire. So the woman was shy and stood. So he said: Upon the woman is a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice: fasting three days, or feeding three poor, or sacrificing a sacrifice. So she said: Which of that is best? He said: Sacrifice. She said: Which sacrifice is best? He said: Slaughter a cow or sacrifice a camel. She said: Which of that is best? He said: Sacrifice a camel. So what we narrated from Ibn Abbas in that agreed with what we mentioned from Malik exactly. So it is the worthiest of statements in our view in this chapter, and to it Ahmad ibn Abi Imran used to go. And with Allah is success."
"4901","656 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what is obligatory upon one who leaves Friday deliberately. 4239 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Qudamah ibn Wabarah, from Samurah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever leaves Friday without excuse, let him give in charity a dinar; and if he does not find, then half a dinar. And what we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter indicates the meaning in this hadith which we mentioned in this chapter, in this hadith: whoever leaves Friday without excuse. Then an objector said: This hadith indicates that there is nothing upon him for excuse. So our answer to him in that was that if what this man was commanded with is an expiation, there does not fall from him what he was commanded with in that, by excuse nor by other than excuse - just as the expiation in shaving the head in pilgrimage did not fall by excuse, rather it was obligatory in excuse and in other than excuse. And if what is in this hadith is meant thereby other than expiation, then in that is what indicated that it is charity of righteousness. And in that is what indicated that charity in intercourse in menstruation is likewise, and that it is charity of righteousness, not charity of expiation; for had it been an expiation, it would not have fallen in the state of excuse, just as expiation did not fall by excuse in shaving the head in pilgrimage from hurt. And with Allah is success."
"4902","657 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the warrior who steals from spoils, of his killing and of burning his baggage. 4240 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Salamah Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Salih ibn Muhammad - Abu Jafar said: and he is Ibn Zaidah - he said: We entered the land of Byzantium with Maslamah ibn Abd al-Malik, and a man stole from spoils. So Maslamah sent to Salim, saying: My father narrated to me, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whomever you find has stolen from spoils, strike his neck and burn his goods. And in his goods was, I think he said, a codex. So he asked Salim, so he said: Sell it and give its price in charity."
"4903","4241 - And Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Salih ibn Muhammad ibn Zaidah, he said: I was with Maslamah ibn Abd al-Malik, and with him was Salim ibn Abdillah ibn Umar. So a man who had stolen from spoils was brought, so Salim narrated to him, from his father, from Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever steals from spoils, burn his goods and strike him. So Maslamah gathered his goods and burned them, except a codex that was in them."
"4904","4242 - And Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Muhammad informed me, he said: I was with Maslamah ibn Abd al-Malik in battle, and he found a man who had stolen from spoils. So he called Salim ibn Abdullah and asked him about that. So he said: My father narrated to me, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever you find has stolen from spoils, strike him and burn his goods. So he found in his baggage a codex, so Salim was asked about that, so he said: Sell it and give its price in charity."
"4905","4243 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, then he mentioned it with its chain and text. Abu Jafar said: So Musa ibn Ismail and Nuaym ibn Hammad differed upon al-Darawardi in the chain of this hadith: Musa did not mention in it, between the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Ibn Umar, his father Umar, while Nuaym mentioned him in its chain. And they differed in what is done with him after burning his baggage: Musa said in his hadith: And strike his neck; while Nuaym said in his hadith: And strike him. And the worthier of the two hadiths in our view in this chapter is what Musa narrated of it; for it is what is in the hands of people from al-Darawardi, from other than their two hadiths. And when that is so, and in his hadith is the command to strike his neck and burn his goods for the stealing from spoils that was from him - though we never heard of this in other than this hadith, nor found anyone of the jurists of the cities upon it besides Makhul, for we found from him in that."
"4906","What Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan ibn Uyaynah saying: Yazid ibn Yazid - meaning Ibn Jabir al-Azdi - narrated to us, from Makhul and others, they said: When stealing from spoils is found in the baggage of the warrior, his goods are burned. And though the school of the companions of the people of hadith about this Salih ibn Muhammad is weakening his narration, without dropping it from them, we considered this hadith of his: do we find in the Book of Allah Almighty or in the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what opposes it or not? So we found Allah Almighty said in His Book: As for the thief, male and female, cut their hands as recompense for what they earned. So Allah Almighty informed that what He commanded in them both, of cutting their hands, is recompense for what was from them both. And in that is what indicated that there is no recompense for them both in what was from them both besides cutting their hands. And that was upon their stealing what is property of others, in which they have no share. While the stealer from spoils steals something in which he has a share. So it was understood that when one finds not upon whoever was from him what is mentioned in the verse that there is not in it burning of his baggage, then when he has in it a share, it is more deserving that there not be obligatory upon him in his stealing from spoils of it burning of his baggage. So it was negated by what we mentioned that there be upon him in his stealing burning of his baggage. And we found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has been narrated from him, of what we mentioned in what preceded from us in this book of ours, by acceptable routes, that the blood of a Muslim man is not lawful except by one of three: disbelief after faith, or adultery after chastity, or a soul for a soul. And in that is what negates that his blood be lawful by what is besides these three things. And what was mentioned in the first hadith, of striking his neck in it, is other than these three things. So in what we narrated of these acceptable reports is what negated that. Then an objector said: But it is permissible that this ruling was after what is in these acceptable reports, so it joins them. So our answer to him in that was that what is mentioned of that is possible - except that the proof has not stood upon us that that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after what was from him in those reports. So it was not permissible for us to join it in them. And prohibition remained in our view upon its state until the proof stands upon us for releasing something of what is in that prohibition, so we release it. And with Allah is success."
"4907","658 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what came from him in the son of the slave-woman of Zamah, whom Sad claimed for his brother and Abd ibn Zamah claimed for his father. 4244 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik ibn Anas narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas enjoined upon his brother Sad ibn Abi Waqqas that the son of the slave-woman of Zamah is of me, so take him to you. She said: So when it was the year of the Conquest, Sad took him, saying: The son of my brother; he had enjoined to me about him. So Abd ibn Zamah said: My brother and the son of my father slave-woman, born on his bed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The child is for the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Sawdah: Veil yourself from him, for what he saw of him of resemblance to Utbah. She said: So she was not seen by him until she met Allah, Glorious and Exalted."
"4908","4245 - al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, that Abd ibn Zamah and Sad disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the son of the slave-woman of Zamah. So Sad said: O Messenger of Allah, my brother enjoined to me, when I come to Makkah, to look to the son of the slave-woman of Zamah and take him, for he is my son. So Abd ibn Zamah said: My brother and the son of my father slave-woman, born on my father bed. So he saw a clear resemblance to Utbah. So he said: He is yours, O Abd ibn Zamah; the child is for the bed. And veil yourself from him, O Sawdah."
"4909","4246 - And Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas said to his brother Sad - and Utbah was a disbeliever while Sad was a Muslim - : I enjoin to you to take the son of the slave-woman of Zamah when you meet him. Aishah said: So when it was the day of the Conquest, Sad met the son of the slave-woman of Zamah and said: The son of my brother, and embraced him. And Abd ibn Zamah said: Rather he is my brother, born on my father bed of his slave-woman. And they disputed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about him. So Sad said: O Messenger of Allah, this is the son of my brother; look at his resemblance to my brother Utbah. And Abd ibn Zamah said: Rather he is, O Messenger of Allah, my brother, born on my father bed of his slave-woman. Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked, and saw a resemblance - people never saw a resemblance clearer than it to Utbah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is yours, O Abd ibn Zamah; the child is for the bed. And veil yourself from him, O Sawdah. So he never saw her until she died, may Allah be pleased with her."
"4910","4247 - And al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Urwah, from Aishah, then he mentioned this hadith."
"4911","4248 - And Fahd narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, Urwah narrated to me that Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas ... then he mentioned the hadith also."
"4912","4249 - al-Hasan ibn Abdillah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Zamah, that he disputed with a man to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a child born on his father bed. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The child is for the bed. And veil yourself from him, O Sawdah."
"4913","4250 - Tahir ibn Amr ibn al-Rabi ibn Tariq al-Hilali narrated to us, he said: My father Amr ibn al-Rabi ibn Tariq narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Hisham, from his father, from Aishah, that she said: Sad ibn Abi Waqqas and Abd ibn Zamah disputed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a boy. So Sad said: O Messenger of Allah, he is the son of my brother Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas; he enjoined to me about him that he is his son. Look at his resemblance. And Abd ibn Zamah said: O Messenger of Allah, this is my brother, born on my father bed of his slave-woman. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw in him a clear resemblance to Utbah. So he said: He is yours, O Abd ibn Zamah; the child is for the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone. And as for you, O Sawdah daughter of Zamah, veil yourself from him. Aishah said: So he never saw Sawdah. Abu Jafar said: So Hammad ibn Salamah and Yahya ibn Ayyub differed in who narrated to Urwah this hadith from him: Hammad said: it is Abdullah ibn Zamah, while Yahya ibn Ayyub said: it is Aishah. And what Yahya ibn Ayyub said of that was worthier - and Allah knows best - in our view, for its agreement with what the group we mentioned in this chapter narrated of it, and because we do not know for Abdullah ibn Zamah any hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides this hadith. And an objector said: Rather Abdullah ibn Zamah has from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a hadith besides this hadith, and he mentioned in that."
"4914","4251 - What Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Zamah, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his address one day, mentioning the she-camel and the one who hamstrung her. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Her most wretched rose; there rose for her a man mighty, protected in his people, like Abu Zamah. And he mentioned women, saying: For what does one of you proceed to flog his wife as a slave is flogged, then perhaps he has intercourse with her at the end of his day. Then I heard him admonishing them against breaking wind, saying: Why does one of you laugh at what he does?!"
"4915","4252 - And what Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Hammad al-Ansari, known as al-Dulabi Abu Bishr, narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sad al-Jawhari narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us. And what Muhammad also narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Hashim al-Balbakki narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Zamah, then he mentioned the like of it. So our answer to him in that was that Abdullah ibn Zamah mentioned in the first hadith is Abdullah ibn Zamah ibn Qays, the brother of Sawdah, of Banu Amir ibn Luayy, while Abdullah ibn Zamah mentioned in the second hadith is Abdullah ibn Zamah ibn al-Aswad ibn al-Muttalib ibn Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza, of the kin of al-Zubayr. And Muhammad ibn Ishaq clarified that in another hadith."
"4916","4253 - As Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham al-Nahwi narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Abdillah al-Bakkai narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq said: Ibn Shihab said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham narrated to us, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Zamah ibn al-Aswad ibn al-Muttalib, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became heavy from prayer, he said: Command one to pray with people. So I saw none among those present more deserving of it than Umar ibn al-Khattab. And Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was absent. So I commanded Umar to pray with people. Then when he commenced takbir - and he was a man loud-voiced - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard his voice and said: Where is Abu Bakr? Allah Almighty and the Muslims refuse that. So Abu Bakr was called, and prayed with people. So Umar said to Ibn Zamah: Woe to you, what did you do? By Allah, had I not thought that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded you to command me with prayer, I would not have prayed with people. Then an objector said: But what Ibn Ishaq narrated of this hadith is only from other than al-Zuhri. So can you find it for us from the hadith of al-Zuhri, mentioned in it that this Abdullah ibn Zamah is Ibn al-Aswad? So our answer to him in that was that we find that for him from the hadith of al-Zuhri, as we mentioned."
"4917","4254 - As Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Utaridi narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Utbah ibn al-Mughirah ibn al-Akhnas narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Abdullah ibn Zamah ibn al-Aswad, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became heavy from prayer, then he mentioned the like of the hadith we mentioned before this hadith. Abu Jafar said: So we reflected upon the hadith we mentioned at the head of this chapter, and found some people made the claim of Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned in it as no claim; for he claimed it for his brother from a slave-woman of another, not by marriage between him and her. He said: So his claiming was thereby as no claim. Abu Jafar said: And what he said of this in our view is not as he said; for Sad knows better than to claim a claim with no meaning to it. Rather the aspect of his claiming that - and Allah knows best - is that the children of prostitutes in the pre-Islamic period used to be joined in Islam to whoever claimed them, and returned to them."
"4918","As Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, Ismail Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, he said: Ghadarah al-Anbari informed us, he said: We came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, about women - or slave-women - who fornicated in the pre-Islamic period. So he commanded that their children stand upon their fathers, and not be enslaved."
"4919","And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, used to join the children of the people of the pre-Islamic period to whoever claimed them in Islam."
"4920","And as Yunus narrated to us, Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Yahya, from Sulayman, then he mentioned it. Abu Jafar said: And when that claim in the time of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, far from the pre-Islamic period, had this ruling for it, it was in the era of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), near to the pre-Islamic period, more deserving of this ruling. And when that is so, Sad claimed for his brother what was judged with in the like of that; for though his brother did not attend that claim, he claimed by bequest of his brother to him thereby - and his brother had died before that. So as though his claiming for his brother, his claiming it for him, is as the claiming of his brother it for himself were he alive - except that Abd ibn Zamah opposed him in that by what he claimed for his father, opposing him with a claim necessitating emancipation for the claimant; for the claimed-for was owned by him in part when he claimed in it what he claimed, so there was freed of him what his claimant owned, were his claim not. So that was what nullified the claim of Sad, not because it was in its origin false. Then that claimant returned to a son of the slave-woman of Zamah who was inherited from him; one of his heirs claimed in him - and he is Abd ibn Zamah - that he is the child of his father, while he had a partner in him, and she is his sister Sawdah. So there was not from her in that, in what was conveyed to us in these reports, any confirmation of him upon that. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bound him to what he acknowledged in himself, and addressed him with the address he addressed him with, of his saying to him: The child is for the bed. And He did not make that a proof against his sister, since it was not known there was from her in that any confirmation of him in his claim. And he commanded her to veil from him, since he did not make him her brother. Then if an objector says: Then what is the meaning of his saying, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He is yours, O Abd ibn Zamah? Our answer to him in that was that that is upon the meaning: he is yours by your hand upon him, by which you prevent others besides you from him - as he, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said about the lost property to its finder: It is yours or your brother or the wolf - not upon the meaning that you own it by your hand upon it, but of what is yours by your hand upon it, of preventing others from it. So like that is his saying, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to Abd: he is yours by your hand upon him, by which you prevent others from him. And how is it permissible to think of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he made that claimed one a son of Zamah, then commands the daughter of Zamah to veil from her brother, while he disapproves of Aishah veiling her paternal uncle by suckling from her? This is in our view of the impossible which its being is not permissible. And how is it permissible to carry the meaning of this hadith upon other than what we carried it upon, when there is no disagreement between Muslims in its like: when one of those who inherited the claimed claims him, when he has no lineage from the claimed-for, and the rest of the heirs deny, that lineage from the claimed-for is not established by that claim, and that he enters with the claimant in his inheritance in the view of most of the people of knowledge - though what he enters with differs in amount - while he does not enter in the view of others in anything of what is in his hand. Of them is al-Shafii, and it is related that it is the statement of a group of the Madinans. And in what we mentioned of this is a proof upon what we described. Then we found Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) this hadith with an addition of meaning he added over Aishah in it."
"4921","4255 - As Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Makhlad al-Asbahani narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Ali al-Jufi narrated to us, from Zaidah ibn Qudamah, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Yusuf ibn al-Zubayr - or from a freedman of Ibn al-Zubayr - Mansur doubted - from Ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Zamah used to have intercourse with a slave-woman, and she was suspected with a man. So Zamah died, and she gave birth resembling the one they suspected her with. So Sawdah said: O Messenger of Allah, she gave birth resembling the one they suspected her with. So he said: Inheritance is his. And veil yourself from him, O Sawdah, for he is not a brother to you."
"4922","4256 - And as Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Rabi al-Jurjani narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn al-Zubayr - and he did not mention between Mujahid and him anyone - that Zamah had a slave-woman he used to have intercourse with, and they suspected her. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Sawdah: As for inheritance, it is his. And as for you, veil yourself from him, O Sawdah, for he is not a brother to you."
"4923","4257 - And as Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Qudamah narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Yusuf ibn al-Zubayr, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Zamah had a slave-woman he used to have intercourse with, and a man was suspected to fall upon her. So Zamah died while she was pregnant, and she gave birth to a boy resembling the man she was suspected with. So Sawdah mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said: As for inheritance, it is his. And as for you, veil yourself from him, O Sawdah, for he is not a brother to you. So we reflected upon the chain of this hadith, and found al-Thawri narrated it from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn al-Zubayr. And we found Zaidah narrated it from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Yusuf ibn al-Zubayr - or from a freedman of Ibn al-Zubayr - from Ibn al-Zubayr. And Zaidah and Jarir agreed upon their both entering in their two hadiths, between Mujahid and Ibn al-Zubayr, this freedman of his - though Zaidah mentioned that Mansur doubted in it. So in this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) negating that claimed one to be a brother to Sawdah. Then an objector said: Then what is the meaning of his saying, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in it that inheritance is his? So our answer to him in that was that that is possible to mean thereby the inheritance which became due to him in the share of Abd by his acknowledging him, not in what is besides it of the estate of Zamah. And Allah we ask for success."
"4924","659 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what the people of knowledge differed in, of permitting completing prayer in travel for the traveler and whoever forbids him that, by what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it. 4258 - Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Ala ibn Zuhayr al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she performed umrah with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Madinah to Makkah. Then when she came to Makkah, she said: O Messenger of Allah, with my father and mother for you, I shortened and completed, and fasted and broke fast! He said: You did well, O Aishah. And he did not fault that upon her. So the outward of this hadith was that Aishah had shortened prayer once and completed it once. So that was of what whoever permits the traveler completing prayer in his travel argued with."
"4925","4259 - Except that Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us this hadith, from al-Firyabi, saying in it: al-Firyabi narrated to us, al-Ala ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad narrated to me, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in an umrah of Ramadan. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) broke fast and I fasted. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shortened and I completed. Then when we came to Makkah, I said: O Messenger of Allah, you broke fast and I fasted, and you shortened and I completed. And he did not mention in his hadith other than this. So that indicated that shortening was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while completing was from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her. And we needed to establish the hearing of Abd al-Rahman from Aishah, since most of his hadiths tracing to Aishah are only from his father from her. So we considered in that."
"4926","So we found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Ala ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad narrated to me, he said: I used to enter upon Aishah without permission, until when I reached puberty, I greeted and sought permission. So she knew my voice and said: He, O enemy of himself, did it?! I said: Yes, O mother. She said: Enter, O son. So I came, and she asked me about my father and his companions, so I informed her. Then I asked her about what they sent me with to her. So in this hadith is establishment of the hearing of Abd al-Rahman from Aishah. Then we reflected upon what is in this hadith of his, and found it far from hearts, since he narrated from Aishah, of his place in her company and in taking from her and in understanding and eminence and acceptance of narration, below what is his of that - and they are Masruq ibn al-Ajda and Urwah ibn al-Zubayr."
"4927","4260 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, Murajja ibn Raja narrated to us, Dawud, and he is Ibn Abi Hind, narrated to us, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Prayer was first made obligatory two rakahs, two rakahs. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah, he prayed to every prayer the like of it, except sunset, for it is the odd of the day, and the dawn prayer for the length of its recitation. And when he traveled, he returned to his first prayer."
"4928","4261 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: Prayer was made obligatory, the first it was made obligatory, two rakahs. So the travel prayer was affirmed, while the resident prayer was increased."
"4929","4262 - And as Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Maslamah al-Qanabi narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4930","4263 - And as Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, that prayer, the first it was made obligatory, was two rakahs. So the travel prayer was affirmed, while the resident prayer was completed. Ibn Shihab said: So I said to Urwah: Then what is with Aishah that she used to complete in travel? He said: She interpreted as Uthman interpreted, may Allah be pleased with them both. So in what we narrated from Masruq and from Urwah, from Aishah, is what verified that the obligation of prayer in travel is two rakahs, as its obligation in residence is four rakahs. And whoever prayed noon in residence eight is not doing well in the view of any of the people of knowledge; for he mixed his obligation in his prayer with other than it of what is not of it. So like that is whoever prayed noon in his travel four, likewise; for he mixed his obligation in his prayer with what is not of it. And when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Muawiyah which we narrated from him in what preceded from us in this book of ours, about whoever prayed a prescribed prayer then wanted to pray after it, not to do until he stands or speaks - when this prohibition is from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for one praying, having given salutation from his prayer, then his prohibition for whoever does that without giving salutation from his prayer is more emphatic. And the doer of that, in his opposing him in what he commanded, of what we mentioned, is more. And Aishah, for her knowledge and her knowledge and her place in Islam, was to follow the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his act upon what one like her must be upon. And how, when there agreed with her in what she narrated from him, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about the obligation of prayer in travel, Abdullah ibn Abbas."
"4931","4264 - As al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad narrated to us, Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: I asked Tawus about voluntary prayers while traveling, and he said: What prevents you? Then al-Hasan ibn Muslim said: I will tell you; I asked Tawus about this, and he said: Ibn Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated the prayer as four rak'ahs in residence and two rak'ahs while traveling. So just as voluntary prayers are performed here before it and after it, likewise one prays while traveling before it and after it. In this hadith of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, the obligatory prayer in travel by the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is two rak'ahs, and it is reasonable that whoever adds over his obligation in his prayer while traveling is like whoever adds over his obligation in his prayer in residence; and since that is not praiseworthy from its doer in residence, it is also not praiseworthy from its doer while traveling. By that, the hadith of Abd al-Rahman which we mentioned is negated, and the two hadiths of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, through Masruq and Urwah which we mentioned, are established. And from Allah we ask success."
"4932","Chapter 660 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: 'Indeed Allah, blessed and exalted is He, has lifted from the traveler half of his prayer.' 4265 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from a man of Banu Amir, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was eating, and he said: 'Come and eat.' He said: I am fasting. So he said: 'Come close so I may inform you about fasting. Indeed Allah, mighty and majestic is He, has lifted half the prayer from the traveler, and fasting from the pregnant woman and the nursing mother.'"
"4933","4266 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Abu Awanah, from Abu Bishr, from Hani ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from a man of Balharith, he said: We were traveling, and we came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was eating, and he said: 'Come and eat.' I said: I am fasting. He said: 'Come, I will tell you about fasting. Indeed Allah has lifted from the traveler fasting and half the prayer.'"
"4934","4267 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, Abu Qilabah narrated to us, Abu Umayyah narrated to me, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from a journey, and he said: 'Will you not wait for lunch, O Abu Umayyah?' I said: I am fasting. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"4935","4268 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ayyub, he said: Abu Qilabah narrated to me, from an elder of Banu Qushayr, from his uncle; then we met him one day and Abu Qilabah said to him: Narrate to him - meaning Ayyub - so the elder said: My uncle narrated to me that he went out with some camels of his and came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was eating lunch, and he said: 'Come to lunch.' I said: I am fasting. He said: 'Indeed Allah has lifted from the traveler half the prayer and fasting.' Then an objector said: You narrated in the chapter before this chapter that the prayer was first obligated as two rak'ahs, and that the addition to that in residence came later over the two rak'ahs; yet in what you narrated in this chapter, Allah the exalted lifted from the traveler half the prayer, and He only lifts what was already established before He lifts it, so this is a severe contradiction. Our answer to him about that is that there is no contradiction in that as he mentioned; for the meaning of the lifting by Him, mighty and majestic is He, is His leaving the obligation of what He lifted upon the one from whom He lifted it, even if it had not been obligated upon him before that. The like of that is what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which the Muslims have unanimously affirmed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which we mentioned earlier in this book of ours, from his saying: 'The pen has been lifted from three: from the child until he grows up, from the sleeper until he wakes, and from the insane until he recovers,' although what was mentioned as lifted from them had not been recorded upon them before that; rather the meaning of 'lifted from them' is that it was not recorded upon them. Likewise is his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith we mentioned in this chapter, Allah the exalted willing: He lifted from the traveler half the prayer, meaning He did not record it upon him - not that it had been recorded upon him before He lifted it from him. Thus, by the praise of Allah the exalted and His favor, it is clear there is no impossibility in anything we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter."
"4936","Chapter 661 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his freeing whoever came out to him from the slaves of Taif, and that among those who came out to him from them was Abu Bakrah, and that he is thereby a freed client of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4269 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakhai narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever came out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Taif, he freed him. Abu Bakrah was among them, so he is a freed client of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4937","4270 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn al-Khalil narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir informed us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed on the day of Taif whoever came out to him from the slaves of Taif; among those freed that day were Abu Bakrah and others, so they were the freed clients of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). We examined this hadith and found the agreed-upon principle that whoever comes out from the slaves of the people of war to the Muslims as a Muslim, deserting his master, is thereby free, because by that coming out he takes himself as spoils, and there is no allegiance upon him in that to anyone; and that whoever comes out to us from their slaves while remaining upon disbelief returns as spoils for us by our territory securing him. This is what Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, used to say about that. As for whoever of us reaches him first and takes him, he belongs to him thereby apart from the rest of the Muslims except for the fifth due upon him in him, which returns to the same ruling as other fifths - as Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan used to say about that, though they had said before that there is no fifth upon him in him. And we found that Abu Bakrah had been among those overtaken by slavery in the Jahiliyyah due to what the people of Jahiliyyah practiced of enslaving the sons of their slave women from them, just as they enslaved others besides them. Abu Bakrah was among them. Then his coming out from the fortress he was in to the Muslim camp took place. It is possible that it was from him while he was a Muslim, so he would thereby take himself as spoils and become free with a freeing that carries no allegiance to anyone among people over him; and it is possible that it was from him while he was upon disbelief, in which case it was not so, and he was a slave taken as spoils like his like of what we mentioned in this chapter. So we looked into that."
"4938","4271 - We found that Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: I heard Sad ibn Malik and Abu Bakrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever claims kinship to other than his father while knowing he is other than his father, Paradise is forbidden to him.' He said: I said to him: Two men narrated it to you - and what two men! He said: What prevents them from that? As for one of them, he is the first man to shoot an arrow in the path of Allah; and as for the other, he is the first man to come down to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the fortress of Taif. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is what indicates that his coming out was to the Muslim camp while he was a Muslim, because of the praise attached to him in that as mentioned in this hadith; for had he come out while upon disbelief, he would not have been praised for his coming out, nor described by it. And since his Islam which he had before his coming out to the Muslim camp and his joining the Muslim camp while upon it was established, we understood that by his coming out to the Muslim camp he took himself as spoils - free with a freeing that carries no allegiance over him to anyone among people. And we understood that the saying of Ibn Abbas, 'a client of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him),' by his freeing of whoever came out to him on the day of Taif, is upon the meaning that he freed him by his coming out, not by a new act of freeing after his coming out; and that his saying 'he is a client of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)' does not mean the allegiance entailed by manumission, but rather that he is his client in the sense of the loyalty entailed by guardianship, from which is his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): 'Whomever I am his master, Ali is his master,' which he then followed with his saying: 'O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.' By that he informed us that his intent by his saying 'whomever I am his master, Ali is his master' is loyalty upon what it consists of among the causes upon which the people of Islam must be in loyalty to one another. The like of that is what has been narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4939","4272 - From what al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, Abu Malik al-Ashjai informed us, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: 'Aslam, Ghifar, Muzaynah, Juhaynah, Ashja, and whoever was of Banu Kab are my clients apart from people, and Allah, mighty and majestic is He, and His Messenger are their protectors.' Al-Husayn said: I mentioned this hadith to Ahmad ibn Salih, and he said: 'My clients apart from people.' The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in this hadith that Allah, mighty and majestic is He, and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are the protectors of these people whom he mentioned in this hadith, informing thereby that he takes them as allies; and whoever Allah and then His Messenger take as an ally is in the highest ranks. And among what indicates what we mentioned about the affair of Abu Bakrah."
"4940","4273 - What Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, al-Mufaddal ibn Mulhalhil al-Dabbi narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Shibak, from al-Shabi, from a man of Thaqif, he said: We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to return Abu Bakrah to us, but he refused and said: 'He is the freedman of Allah and the freedman of His Messenger.' And Abu Bakrah had come out to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he besieged Taif. In this hadith is what indicates that Abu Bakrah's Islam preceded his coming out to the Muslim camp, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed those who asked him to return him to them that he is the freedman of Allah and His Messenger; and the freedman is one released from the captivity he was in. That indicates he was in captivity in the hands of the polytheists when Allah, then His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), released him from it by his joining the Muslim camp. In what we mentioned is proof of what we described, and Allah is the granter of success."
"4941","Chapter 662 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his giving precedence to the freedmen in stipends over others among people, and what his intent in that was. 4274 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Nafi al-Saigh narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sad, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, that Muawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, when he came to Madinah performing Hajj, Abd Allah ibn Umar came to him, and Muawiyah said to him: Your need, O Abu Abd al-Rahman? Abd Allah said to him: My need is the stipend of the freedmen, for I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when something came to him, begin with none before them."
"4942","4275 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, that he said to Muawiyah: You have withheld the stipend of the freedmen, yet I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) begin with anything before them when he found it."
"4943","4276 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad informed us, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yuhannas narrated to us, from Nuh ibn Abi Bilal, from Abu Attab, that Muawiyah, the year he performed Hajj - Abd Allah ibn Umar said: Begin with the freedmen, for I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distribute a distribution and begin with them. So Muawiyah began and gave the freedmen before the people. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith to determine the reason for what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did with the freedmen - who are the freed clients - what it was. We found that the freedmen had been enemies of the believers, killing them, capturing them, and taking their wealth; and the believers were also enemies to them, seeking from them the like of that, except that in seeking it from them they intended good for them and bringing them into Islam, to be a cause for them to Paradise and to success in this world and the Hereafter. What the disbelievers intended for the believers thereby was the opposite, for they call them to the Fire. The believers would capture the polytheists and treat them well, while the polytheists, when they captured Muslims, treated them badly, tortured them, and starved them. What the believers intended by fighting the polytheists was truth, and what the disbelievers intended by fighting them was falsehood. What came from each of the two parties was of the same kind as what the other party calls to fighting for; yet one of the two fightings was with truth and the other with falsehood. The believers, in fighting the polytheists, intended from them faith in Allah, mighty and majestic is He, so they would become like them in what they are upon in this world and what they will come to in the Hereafter. From that is the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in answer to the one who asked him about his laughing, when he said: 'I saw people being dragged to Paradise in chains,' meaning the non-Arab disbelievers whom the believers dragged by fighting them to Islam, which becomes a cause for their entry into Paradise. The believers' hands would fall upon them while they were upon the disbelief they were upon, yet the believers did not, because of that, withhold kindness from those whose slavery remained upon them, nor withhold dealing with them with the opposites of what they would do to them had their hands fallen upon them. Then they would free them, thereby restoring them as free men - even though what the believers intended thereby for them was seeking what is with Allah regarding them. Since that kindness from them to them persisted in all the states they were upon until freeing overtook them from them, what they did for them of the stipend - for which they became eligible by that freeing - was part of kindness to them, of the same kind as kindness to its people: giving them precedence in stipends over others among its people, so that this kindness after freeing them would be added to their prior kindness to them, so their kindness to them would never leave them."
"4944","Chapter 663 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying to Banu al-Nadir, when he ordered their expulsion from Madinah and they said to him: We have debts not yet due: 'Reduce and hasten.' 4277 - Abd al-Salam ibn Ahmad ibn Suhayl al-Basri Abu Bakr narrated to us, from his original dictation, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Muslim ibn Khalid al-Zanji narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Yazid ibn Rukanah narrated to us, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the expulsion of Banu al-Nadir, some people from them came to him and said: O Prophet of Allah, you ordered our expulsion, yet we have debts owed by people not yet due. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Reduce and hasten.' Abu Jafar said: These Banu al-Nadir are the nobles of the Jews, and they used to dwell in Madinah."
"4945","4278 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us - al-Rabi said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, and Muhammad said: My father and Shuayb informed me - from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Said ibn Abi Said, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: While we were in the mosque, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and said: 'Go to the Jews.' We went out with him until we came to the house of al-Midras. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and called to them: 'O company of Jews, submit and you will be safe.' They said: You have conveyed, O Abu al-Qasim. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: 'That is what I want; submit and you will be safe.' They said: You have conveyed, O Abu al-Qasim. He said: 'That is what I want.' Then he said it a third time: 'Know that the earth belongs to Allah and His Messenger, and I intend to expel you from this land. Whoever among you finds anything for his wealth, let him sell it; otherwise, know that the earth belongs to Allah and His Messenger.' Abu Jafar said: They are the ones among whom the women of the Ansar in the Jahiliyyah, when they wanted to make Jewish those of their children whom they wanted to make Jewish, would make them Jewish among them."
"4946","4279 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Bishr, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, about His saying, mighty and majestic is He: {There is no compulsion in religion}: He said: A woman of the Ansar would hardly have a child live for her, so she would swear in the Jahiliyyah: If a child lives for her, she will make him Jewish. When Banu al-Nadir were expelled and among them were people from the sons of the Ansar, the Ansar said: O Messenger of Allah, our sons! So Allah, mighty and majestic is He, revealed: {There is no compulsion in religion}. Said said: Whoever wished joined them, and whoever wished entered Islam."
"4947","4280 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, he said: I asked Said ibn Jubayr about His saying, mighty and majestic is He: {There is no compulsion in religion}. He said: This verse was revealed concerning the Ansar specifically. I said: Specifically? He said: Specifically. He said: A woman in the Jahiliyyah, if she was childless through loss, would vow that if she bore a child she would place him among the Jews, seeking thereby his long survival. Islam came while among them were some of them. When Banu al-Nadir were expelled, they said: O Messenger of Allah, our sons and brothers are among them. He kept silent about them, so Allah the exalted revealed: {There is no compulsion in religion; the right path has become distinct from error}. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Give your companions a choice; if they choose you, they are from you, and if they choose them, they are from them.' He said: So they expelled them with them. Shubah and Abu Awanah differed over Abu Bishr in the chain of this hadith: Shubah carried it beyond Said ibn Jubayr to Ibn Abbas, while Abu Awanah stopped it at Said ibn Jubayr. Abu Jafar said: They are different from the Jews of Khaybar whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dealt with over it for half of what its palms and land produce, and they remained in it upon that until Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, expelled them from it, as we mentioned about that concerning sharecropping for half of what the land produces in what preceded from us in this book. Then we examined the hadith we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter and found the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Banu al-Nadir, upon expelling them, to reduce part of their deferred debts and hasten the rest. This chapter is among what the people of knowledge differed about: some permitted it, among them Abd Allah ibn Abbas."
"4948","As Ahmad ibn al-Husayn al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan ibn Uyaynah saying: From Amr, from Ibn Abbas, that he saw no harm in saying: Hasten to me and I will reduce for you. And some disliked it, namely Abd Allah ibn Umar and Zayd ibn Thabit."
"4949","As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Uthman ibn Hafs ibn Umar ibn Khaldah, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, that Abd Allah ibn Umar was asked about a man who has a debt owed by a man deferred to a term, and the owner of the right reduces for him while the other hastens to him - so Abd Allah ibn Umar disliked that and forbade it."
"4950","And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abu al-Zinad, from Busr ibn Said, from Ubayd Abu Salih, freedman of al-Saffah, that he said: I sold some cloth of mine to people of Dar Nakhla and of the market on credit to a term; then I wanted to go out to Kufah, and they proposed to me that I reduce for them and they pay me cash. I asked about that of Zayd ibn Thabit, and he said: I do not command you to eat from that nor to feed it."
"4951","And as Ahmad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us that he heard Sufyan saying: Abu al-Zinad, from Busr ibn Said, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he was asked about that and disliked it, saying: Do not eat it nor feed it. Ahmad did not mention Ubayd Abu Salih in his hadith."
"4952","And as al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, Jafar - meaning Ibn Muhammad - narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a man asked him: I have a debt upon a man to a term, and I want to reduce for him so he hastens the debt to me. Abd Allah said: Do not do it. Then an objector said: Do you make the hadith of Ibn Abbas which you mentioned at the beginning of this chapter a proof for whoever permits the mentioned practice against whoever dislikes it? Our answer to him about that is that there is no proof in it with us for whoever holds to permitting that against whoever holds to disliking it; for it is possible that what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that was before Allah, mighty and majestic is He, prohibited usury, then usury was prohibited after that, so its causes were prohibited. This is a weighty question in jurisprudence which must be examined until the correct view in it is determined, Allah willing: reducing part of a deferred debt to be a cause for hastening the rest. Those we mentioned disliked it, while others we described permitted it. The principle in that is that if the matter between the creditor and the debtor proceeded with reduction and hastening upon each of them being conditioned upon the other, it is clear that is not permitted, and it is like the usury which the Quran came prohibiting, with the threat of Allah, mighty and majestic is He, upon it: the Jahiliyyah would pay to those to whom they owed an immediate debt what they paid them of their wealth so they would defer that immediate debt to a term they mentioned in that deferral, thereby purchasing a term with wealth. Allah prohibited that and threatened the threat the Quran brought. The like of that is reducing part of a deferred debt to hasten the rest, in that it is not permitted, for it is buying hastening with what is hastened of it by dropping the rest of the debt dropped from it. This is clear that it is not permitted. Among the people of knowledge who held that were Abu Hanifah, Malik, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad."
"4953","As Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan informed us, Yaqub narrated to us, from Abu Hanifah, with what we mentioned, and he did not report disagreement among them in that."
"4954","And as Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, from Malik, with this meaning also. And among those who held contrary to that was Zufar ibn al-Hudhayl."
"4955","As Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Jufi narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zufar said about a man who has a thousand dirhams upon a man to a year, from the price of goods or a guarantee, and he settles with him for five hundred cash: That is permitted. Al-Shafii, may Allah have mercy on him, had permitted that once, as al-Muzani mentioned to us from him. He said: If the mukatab hastens to his master part of the contract upon his releasing him from the rest, it is not permitted; what he took is returned to him and he is not freed, for he released him from what he had not been released from. Al-Muzani said: He said in this place: 'Reduce and hasten' is not permitted, yet he permitted it in debt. Abu Jafar said: As for when that reduction and hastening are such that neither is conditioned upon the other, but rather upon a reduction hoped thereby to hasten the rest of the debt - that is unlike the first case, and it is not reasonable to nullify it by ruling; rather it is disliked without ruling its nullification, just as a loan that brings benefit is disliked yet not ruled nullified thereby. This is the aspect of this chapter: effecting settlement upon stipulating hastening in the reduction, and the reduction hoped thereby to hasten the rest of the debt without stipulating it in that reduction. And success is from Allah."
"4956","Chapter 664 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his forbidding frightening souls with debt. 4281 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: I heard Haywah ibn Shurayh narrating from Bakr ibn Amr, from Shuayb ibn Zarah, from Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to his Companions: 'Do not frighten yourselves' - or he said: 'souls.' It was said: O Messenger of Allah, with what do we frighten ourselves? He said: 'Debt.'"
"4957","4282 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, Haywah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4958","4283 - Al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Lahiah narrated to us, Bakr ibn Amr narrated to us, from Shuayb ibn Zarah, from Uqbah ibn Amir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Do not frighten yourselves after their security.' They said: What is that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: 'Debt.'"
"4959","4284 - And Fahd narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Nafi ibn Yazid informed us, Bakr ibn Amr narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Zarah narrated to me that he heard Uqbah ibn Amir saying... then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Yunus, from Ibn Wahb, from Haywah, from Bakr, which we mentioned in this chapter. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith to determine what is intended by it, Allah willing. We found the prohibition in it directed to frightening souls with debts, and it was reasonable that nothing frightens souls except what overcomes them until they become thereby fearful of it. That is like what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides this hadith."
"4960","4285 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum al-Maafiri informed me, from Hudayj ibn Sawma al-Himyari, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Heedlessness is in three things: heedlessness of the remembrance of Allah, mighty and majestic is He; from the time one prays the dawn prayer until the sun rises; and that a man is heedless of himself regarding debt until it mounts him.' What is of debts that do not mount the one they are upon is working to free himself from them and clear himself of them to their owners - unlike the debts whose bearer is heedless of clearing himself of them and getting out of them to their owners. Whoever is of this second station is blameworthy and frightens himself with the debt upon him of evil outcome in this world through evil demand, and in the Hereafter with what is sterner than that. As for what is of debt upon him in the first of these two states, he does not fear for himself what one in the other state fears for himself regarding the debt upon him; rather whoever is in the praiseworthy state of these two states regarding the debt upon him is hoped for reward in what he is upon of that, and help from Allah, mighty and majestic is He, to him in what he is upon in it - as was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it."
"4961","4286 - From what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq informed us, Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, that Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), took a loan, and it was said to her: O Mother of the Believers, you borrow while you have no means to repay! She said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: 'Whoever takes a debt intending to repay it, Allah, mighty and majestic is He, will help him.'"
"4962","4287 - And from what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Qudamah informed us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid informed us, from Mansur, from Ziyad ibn Amr ibn Hind, from Imran ibn Hudhayfah, he said: Maymunah used to borrow frequently, and her family spoke to her about that, blamed her, and were upset with her. She said: I will not abandon debt, for I heard my beloved and chosen one, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: 'There is no one who contracts a debt, Allah, mighty and majestic is He, knowing he intends to repay it, except Allah, mighty and majestic is He, will repay it for him in this world.'"
"4963","4288 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn al-Fadl al-Huddani narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ali, that Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, used to borrow, and it was said to her: What have you to do with debt?! She said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: 'There is no servant who intends to repay his debt except he has help from Allah, mighty and majestic is He.' So I seek that help."
"4964","4289 - And what Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, Talhah ibn Sahhaj narrated to us, he said: Warqa bint Harrab narrated to me, she said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when he prayed dawn would pass by the doors of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He saw a man sitting at Aishah's door and said: Why do I see you sitting here? He said: A debt I seek from the Mother of the Believers. Umar sent to her: O Mother of the Believers, is not seven thousand dirhams which I send to you each year sufficient for you? She said: Yes, but we have obligations in it. I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: 'Whoever contracts a debt intending to repay it, there is with him a guardian from Allah, mighty and majestic is He.' So I love that there be with me a guardian from Allah, mighty and majestic is He. Abu Jafar said: The help from Allah, mighty and majestic is He, and the guardian are only for one in debt whose states in it are those praiseworthy states in the two states we mentioned. Among what also permits borrowing upon praiseworthy intention is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which we mentioned earlier in this book in the chapter of whoever dies not associating anything with Allah, from his saying to Abu Dharr: 'I would not love to have Uhud as gold with a night passing over me while I have a dinar of it with me, except a dinar I set aside for a debt.' That indicates he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to borrow. From that also is what was narrated from him about his pledging his armor for the debt he owed to the Jew who held that debt upon him. We shall mention that and what was narrated in it later in this book, Allah, mighty and majestic is He, willing. In that is what indicates the permissibility of borrowing with the intention to repay what is borrowed, or upon not being heedless of the borrower in that until that debt mounts him and returns him to blameworthy states in this world - as was narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4965","From what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Dawud al-Khuraybi narrated to us, from Quraysh ibn Hayyan, from Ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, namely Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Dalaf, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Do not look at a man's prayer nor his fasting, but look at his truthfulness when he speaks, his trustworthiness when entrusted, and his piety when he prospers. Indeed al-Usayfi, Usayfi of Juhaynah, was content with his religion and trust that it be said: He preceded the pilgrims, then borrowed indiscriminately, until he became overwhelmed by it. Whoever has a debt upon him, let him attend the sale of his wealth or the division of his wealth. Indeed debt begins with anxiety and ends with grief. Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz mentioned to us, he said: Abu Ubayd said to us: Abu Zayd said: 'He borrowed indiscriminately' means he borrowed confronting people, borrowing from everyone he could; Abu Zayd said: His saying 'he became overwhelmed by it' means he fell into what he cannot escape nor bear. Abu Jafar said: This debt too which al-Faruq, may Allah be pleased with him, condemned is the debt in which heedlessness of fearing its consequences is practiced, and precaution against them abandoned, until the one it is upon returns to the blameworthy states that befell al-Usayfi, whose outcomes in the Hereafter may be sterner than that. We seek refuge with Allah, mighty and majestic is He, from them, and from Him we ask success."
"4966","Chapter 665 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: 'The one consulted is trusted.' 4290 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, al-Aswad ibn Amir and Talq ibn Ghannam narrated to us, from Sharik, from al-Amash, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, from Abu Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: 'The one consulted is trusted.'"
"4967","4291 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Amr, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu Salamah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Thus Yunus narrated to us, not carrying it beyond Abu Salamah to anyone else."
"4968","4292 - Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, Isa ibn Sulayman al-Shayzari narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. Ali ibn Mabad and Isa ibn Sulayman differed over Ubayd Allah ibn Amr in the chain of this hadith as we mentioned of their disagreement in it. We looked into that to determine which of them has the correct view."
"4969","4293 - We found that Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Shayban al-Nahwi narrated to us. 4294 - And we found Abu Umayyah also narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab narrated to us, Shayban - meaning al-Nahwi - narrated to us. Then both together said: From Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out at an hour in which he would not come out and no one would meet him in it. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came to him and he said: 'What brought you out, O Abu Bakr?' He said: I came out to meet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to look at his face and greet him. It was not long before Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came, and he said: 'What brought you out, O Umar?' He said: Hunger. He said: 'I feel some of what you feel. Go to the house of Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan...' Then he mentioned the hadith at length, and said in it: 'The one consulted is trusted.' We thus understood that the correct view was with Isa, and that he preserved of the chain of this hadith what Ali did not preserve."
"4970","4295 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: It was read to Said ibn Sulayman Sadawayh while I was present, and it was said to him: Hafs ibn Sulayman narrated to you, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from al-Numan ibn Bashir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'The one consulted is trusted.' He said: Yes. We examined this hadith to determine what is intended by what is in it, Allah, mighty and majestic is He, willing. We found that a man in consulting his brother seeks the surplus of his brother's opinion over his own opinion, so that by proceeding in his affair upon what his brother consulted him with he is upon the surplus he estimated with him in his opinion over what he has - thereby entrusting to him what he does of what he consults him about, following what he advises him with. If what he advises therein is correct, he has of reward for that what the like has in the like of that; and if he advises him therein contrary to what is correct while knowing it is so, he thereby brings him into what he does of what he advised him with. The like of that also is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entering into this meaning."
"4971","4296 - From what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to me, from Bakr ibn Amr, from Amr ibn Abi Nuaymah, from Abu Uthman Muslim ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whoever consults his brother and he advises him with other than right guidance has betrayed him.'"
"4972","4297 - And as Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan al-Basri narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us... then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4973","4298 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Bakr ibn Amr, from Amr ibn Abi Nuaymah, from Abu Uthman al-Tunbudhi, foster-brother of Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the like of it."
"4974","4299 - And as al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Azdi al-Jizi narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us... then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in this hadith that whoever consults his brother and he advises him contrary to right guidance has betrayed him. Under this speech is that when he advises him with right guidance, from him is the opposite of betrayal, namely sincere counsel; and whoever has betrayal in him deserves punishment for it, while whoever has trustworthiness from him deserves reward for it. By what we mentioned it is clear what is intended by the trustworthiness mentioned in the hadith with which we began in this chapter. Allah is the praised for that, and from Him we ask success."
"4975","Chapter 666 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he commanded his Companions in the Hajj they performed with him, when they circumambulated the House and al-Safa and al-Marwah, to dissolve their ihram except whoever had the sacrificial animal with him. 4300 - Al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Hatim ibn Ismail al-Madani narrated to us, Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Hajj intending nothing but Hajj, until when it was the last circuit upon al-Marwah, he said: 'Had I faced of my affair what I have turned my back on, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal, and I would have made it Umrah. Whoever among those with no sacrificial animal with him, let him dissolve.'"
"4976","4301 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Ibn al-Had, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4977","4302 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Sad, from Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived when four of Dhu al-Hijjah had passed. When they circumambulated the House and al-Safa and al-Marwah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Make it Umrah.'"
"4978","4303 - And Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ruayni narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Ata, from Jabir, he said: When we arrived with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Makkah in the Farewell Hajj, he asked the people: 'With what did you enter ihram?' Some said: We entered ihram for Hajj; others said: We arrived as mutamattis; others said: We pronounced the talbiyah with your talbiyah, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: 'Whoever arrived without driving a sacrificial animal, let him dissolve. Had I faced of my affair what I have turned my back on, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal until I would be lawful.'"
"4979","4304 - And Bakkar narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: We arrived with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the morning of the fourth, and he ordered us to dissolve. We said: Which dissolving, O Messenger of Allah? He said: 'The complete dissolving. Had I faced of my affair what I have turned my back on, I would have done like what you do.'"
"4980","4305 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Ata, that he heard him narrating from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: We entered ihram with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Dhu al-Hulayfah for Hajj purely, mixing it with nothing else. We arrived at Makkah, and when we circumambulated the House and ran between al-Safa and al-Marwah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us to make it Umrah and to dissolve to women. We said: There are only five nights between us and Arafah, and we shall go out to it with one's ذكر dripping semen. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'I am the most righteous of you and the most truthful of you; were it not for the sacrificial animal, I would have dissolved.'"
"4981","4306 - Al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibari narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Maqil ibn Ubayd Allah al-Absi narrated to us, from Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as pilgrims intending nothing but Hajj, not intending Umrah. We circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us, so we dissolved. He said: 'Had I faced of my affair what I have turned my back on, I would not have driven a sacrificial animal; were it not for the sacrificial animal, I would have dissolved. Whoever has no sacrificial animal with him, let him dissolve.'"
"4982","4307 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions arrived pronouncing talbiyah for Hajj, and al-Zubayr had the sacrificial animal with him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: 'Whoever has no sacrificial animal with him, let him dissolve.' She said: I had no sacrificial animal with me that year, so I dissolved."
"4983","4308 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, Dawud - namely Ibn Abi Hind - narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We went out from Madinah crying out with Hajj loudly. When we arrived, we circumambulated, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Make it Umrah, except whoever has the sacrificial animal with him.'"
"4984","4309 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Abu Malih, from Maqil ibn Yasar, he said: We performed Hajj with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he found Aishah removing her clothes. He said to her: 'What is the matter with you?' She said: I was told you dissolved and dissolved for your family. He said: 'He dissolves who has no sacrificial animal with him; as for us, we have not dissolved, for we have a sacrificial animal with us until we reach Arafat.' Abu Jafar said: An asker asked about the meaning by which those who drove the sacrificial animal and those who did not differed in this matter - those without the animal dissolved while those with it did not - though both groups had entered ihram for a Hajj and their Hajj was turned to Umrah. From where does driving the animal versus not driving it differ in this? Our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah, mighty and majestic is He, and His help, is that all the people - though their ihram was for a Hajj and they were all turned to an Umrah - it is the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for whoever enjoys Umrah to Hajj without driving the animal that he dissolves after finishing his Umrah, just as the one performing Umrah who does not intend enjoyment dissolves; and that had he driven an animal for his enjoyment, he would not dissolve between his Hajj and Umrah until his dissolving from both is together. It was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that."
"4985","4310 - What Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the matter with people dissolving while you have not dissolved from your Umrah? He said: 'I have matted my head and garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I shall not dissolve until I dissolve from Hajj.'"
"4986","4311 - And what Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Bundar narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, Ubayd Allah narrated to us, he said: Nafi informed me, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4987","4312 - And what Ishaq narrated to us, Abu Hammam narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah. And what Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, al-Min jab narrated to us, Ali narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah... then he mentioned the like of it."
"4988","4313 - And what Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah... then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4989","4314 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Nafi, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4990","4315 - And what Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman Abu Ayyub narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hafsah narrated to me... then he mentioned the like of it."
"4991","4316 - And what Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu al-Ashath al-Ijli narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Musa ibn Uqbah narrated to us, Nafi informed me... then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that driving the sacrificial animal for enjoyment prevents dissolving between Umrah and Hajj until the dissolving from both is together. It was also narrated from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what indicates this meaning."
"4992","4317 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bashshar informed us, Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: 'This is an Umrah we enjoyed. Whoever has no sacrificial animal with him, let him dissolve completely, for Umrah has entered into Hajj.' It was also narrated from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what indicates this meaning as well."
"4993","4318 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me that Aishah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Had I faced of my affair what I have turned my back on, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal, and I would have dissolved with the people when they dissolved from Umrah.' Abu Jafar said: Thus the Kufans - Abu Hanifah and his companions, and al-Thawri - used to say about one enjoying Umrah to Hajj: he does not dissolve between them if he drove the animal until he dissolves from both together. As for the Hijazis, they differ from them in that and give no meaning to his driving the animal in this; they say the one enjoying, after finishing his Umrah, dissolves from it whether he drove an animal for it or not. No one may depart from what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of word and deed without a specification in that for one apart from another. And success is from Allah."
"4994","Chapter 667 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying to Ali, when he came to him from Yemen in his Hajj: 'With what did you enter ihram?' He said: I said: O Allah, I enter ihram with what Your Messenger enters ihram with; and in his commanding him to remain upon his ihram until he dissolves from his Hajj; and what was narrated from him about Abu Musa, after he informed him that he entered ihram like his ihram, that he circumambulate, run, and dissolve. 4319 - Al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad narrated to us, Hatim narrated to us, Jafar narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about their entering ihram with him in the Farewell Hajj with monotheism, his commanding them after finishing the running between al-Safa and al-Marwah to dissolve and make it Umrah except whoever had an animal with him, his saying to them: 'Had I faced of my affair what I have turned my back on, I would not have driven the animal, and I would have made it Umrah'; and that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came to him from Yemen with an animal with him, and he said to him: 'What did you say when you obligated Hajj?' He said: I said: O Allah, I enter ihram with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enters ihram with. He said: 'Do not dissolve, for I have an animal with me.' Abu Jafar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) related in what was from him to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, what is in this hadith; and it was narrated from him about Abu Musa al-Ashari."
"4995","4320 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us. 4321 - And what Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Shaba bah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us. And what al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us. Then all together said: From Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was dismounted at al-Batha, and he said to me: 'Did you enter ihram?' I said: An ihram like the ihram of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: 'You did well. Circumambulate the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then dissolve.' Abu Jafar said: An asker asked about the meaning by which what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - what he commanded each of Ali and Abu Musa - differed, though each of them had informed him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he had entered ihram like his ihram. Our answer to him in that is that Ali informed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he had an animal with him, while Abu Musa had no animal with him. So he commanded Ali with what one who enjoys with an animal with him is commanded, and commanded Abu Musa with what one who enjoys with no animal with him is commanded. Though both of their entering ihram was with what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram with, entering ihram does not entail remaining between Umrah and Hajj until the dissolving from both is together; only the driven animal for them entails that, nothing else. So he commanded each of them with what is due upon him of remaining upon what he is in between his Umrah and Hajj, or coming out of that to dissolving between them. Then we sought what is in these two hadiths of what indicates other chapters of jurisprudence besides this chapter, and we found that each of Ali and Abu Musa had entered ihram like the ihram of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before knowing the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had entered ihram and before knowing what he entered ihram with; yet the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made them thereby in ihram, entered into the like of his ihram. That indicates that whoever enters ihram like the ihram of so-and-so, not knowing what it is, is in ihram like the ihram of so-and-so with what he entered ihram with, and his ignorance of that does not harm him; and that whoever enters a thing before knowing its time entered, or before knowing that what he entered is his, returns that to the reality of that thing - so one who entered it in ignorance is made like one who entered it in knowledge. From that: a man who enters the noon prayer not knowing the sun has declined, then learns it had declined - his prayer suffices him just as it would suffice him had he entered it after knowing its time entered. The like of that: a man who enters fasting a day intending it as Ramadan not knowing the crescent had been sighted before that - that fast suffices him for Ramadan. As Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad."
"4996","Chapter 668 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: 'I have not left after me a trial more harmful to men than women,' and in his saying: 'Every nation has a trial, and the trial of my nation is wealth.' 4322 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Yaqub al-Sadusi, keeper of al-Silah, narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us. And Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Hawdhah ibn Khalifah al-Bakrawi narrated to us. They said: Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'I have not left after me a trial more harmful to men than women.'"
"4997","4323 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, al-Mutamir narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Uthman, from Usamah ibn Zayd and Said ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4998","4324 - And Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, Arim and Musaddad narrated to us, they said: al-Mutamir narrated to us, from his father, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Then an objector said: In this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is what you mentioned from him in it, yet you narrated from him what contradicts that."
"4999","4325 - He mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Muawiyah ibn Salih informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from Kab ibn Iyad, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: 'Every nation has a trial, and the trial of my nation is wealth.' He said: In this hadith the trial of his nation is wealth, so how can the trial of women be greater than that? Our answer to him in that is that his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): 'I have not left after me a trial more harmful to men than the trial of women' is upon the trial that befalls men apart from women; and in that is what indicates he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left in his nation trials besides women. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): 'The trial of my nation is wealth' is upon a trial encompassing the men and women of his nation, so that trial is broader and has more people than the other trial; each of them has the people whom each of these two hadiths indicates who they are. It was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his warning against the trial of this world and the trial of women."
"5000","4326 - What Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Maslamah, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Indeed this world is sweet and green, and Allah, mighty and majestic is He, has made you stewards in it, and sees how you act. So beware the trial of this world and the trial of women, for the first trial of the Children of Israel was through women.' In this hadith is his mention of the trial of women which he mentioned in the hadith of Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, and his mention of the trial of this world, in which is the trial of wealth mentioned in the hadith of Kab ibn Iyad, and trials with other than that. And Allah is the granter of success."
"5001","Chapter 669 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a debtor eating from the wealth of the one to whom he owes that debt with his willing consent - is that permitted to him or not? 4327 - Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Aban narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, al-Fadl ibn Musa al-Sinani narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Ziyad al-Ashjai, from Jami ibn Shaddad, from Tariq al-Muharibi, he said: When Islam appeared, we went out in a company with a woman traveler of ours until we alighted near Madinah. While we were returning, a man came to us wearing two white garments, greeted, then said: From where has the company come? We said: From al-Rabadhah, and with us was a red camel. He said: Will you sell me the camel? We said: Yes. He said: For how much? We said: For such-and-such measures of dates. He took it without bargaining us down at all. He said: I have taken it. He took the camel's head until he disappeared behind the walls of Madinah. We blamed one another among ourselves, saying: You gave your camel to a man you do not know! The woman traveler said: Do not blame one another; I saw the face of a man who would not betray you. I never saw anything more like the moon on the night of fullness than his face. In the evening a man came to us and said: Peace be upon you; I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to you. He commands you to eat until you are full and to measure until you take in full. So we ate until we were full and measured until we took in full."
"5002","4328 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Jafar al-Dhuhli al-Kufi narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Yazid ibn Ziyad ibn Abi al-Jad narrated to us, Abu Sakhrah Jami ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Tariq al-Muharibi, then he mentioned the like of it."
"5003","4329 - Fahd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Salt al-Kufi narrated to us, Hibban ibn Ali narrated to us, from Sad - Abu Jafar said: It was said he is Ibn Said al-Ansari - from Imran ibn Talhah, from Khawlah al-Ansariyyah, she said: In the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) there was a measure of dates owed by a man to a man of Banu Ghifar. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man of the Ansar: 'Settle it.' He gave him dates inferior to his dates, and he rejected it. The Ansari said: Do you reject it upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?! He said: Yes - and who is more entitled to justice than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)! The eyes of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) filled with tears, and he said: 'He spoke truthfully - and who is more entitled to justice than me? Indeed Allah does not sanctify a nation in which the weak among them cannot take his right from the strong among them without faltering.' Then he said: 'O Khawlah, promise him, take him away, and settle for him. There is no creditor who leaves his debtor while he is pleased except the beasts of the earth and the fish of the seas pray for him; and there is no debtor who evades his creditor while able except a sin is recorded upon him every day and night.'"
"5004","4330 - And Malik ibn Abd Allah ibn Sayf al-Nahwi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salim al-Himsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Hamzah ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Abd Allah ibn Salam narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, that Zayd ibn Sanah was among the rabbis of the Jews. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with eighty dinars, then said: I give them to you upon your giving me named measures from a named orchard to a named term. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: 'I will not take them from you upon named measures from a named orchard, but I will take them from you upon named measures to a named term.' Then Zayd ibn Sanah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) demanding payment, pulled his garment from his right shoulder, then said: You, O Banu Abd al-Muttalib, are people of delay; I know you well. Umar rebuked him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: 'He and I needed other than this from you: that you command me to repay well and command him to demand well. Go, O Umar, to the orchard of Banu so-and-so and fulfill his right. Indeed three days remain of his term - and add thirty measures for your harshness to him.' Then an objector said: Does this hadith enter the musnad of Abd Allah ibn Salam or not? If it does not enter it, it returns disconnected. Our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah, mighty and majestic is He, and His help, is that it does not thereby return disconnected, since it is possible it ended with it to Yusuf ibn Abd Allah - for Yusuf was born in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he named him Yusuf."
"5005","4331 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi al-Haytham al-Attar, from Yusuf ibn Abd Allah ibn Salam, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named me Yusuf. Then an objector said: How do you accept these reports when you narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his forbidding eating through certain things, among them his forbidding eating through the Quran?"
"5006","4332 - And he mentioned what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Zayd ibn Sallam, from Abu Sallam, from Abu Rashid al-Hubrani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: 'Read the Quran, do not go to excess in it, do not turn away from it, do not eat through it, and do not seek abundance through it.'"
"5007","4333 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, al-Mughirah ibn Ziyad informed us, he said: Ubadah ibn Nusayy informed me, from al-Aswad ibn Thalabah, from Ubadah, he said: I used to teach some people of the Suffah the Quran, and a man among them gifted me a bow on condition I accept it in the path of Allah, mighty and majestic is He. I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: 'If you want Allah to collar you with a collar of fire, accept it.' Abu Jafar said: Since it is forbidden for a man to eat through the Quran, it is reasonable that it is forbidden for him to eat through his wealth, and that if he does that he enters a door of the doors of usury. Our answer to him in that is that what is in the first reports is with us - and Allah knows best - of what may have been before the prohibition of usury, then usury was prohibited, so its causes were prohibited. The proof of that is what was narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after him, about which we found no disagreement among them. From that."
"5008","What Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Said ibn Abi Burdah, from his father, he said: My father sent me to Madinah to the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to learn. I met Abd Allah ibn Salam, stood to him, greeted him, and he took my hand. He said: Who are you? I said: So-and-so son of so-and-so. He said: Welcome, O son of my brother. I said to him: I only walked with you to teach me something. He said: I shall not teach you until you go with me to the house. I went with him, and he brought near to me sawiq and dates, and I ate. Then he said: O son of my brother, you are in a land in which usury is abundant and subtle. If you lend a man of the dhimmis silver to a term and he brings it to you with a load of fodder or feed with it, do not touch it, for that is among the greatest doors of usury. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see that in this hadith Abd Allah ibn Salam forbade Abu Burdah what he forbade him, of what the hadith of Ibn Sanah permits the like of to him? That indicates the ruling of that practice in the time he forbade it to him differs from its ruling in the time the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted what he permitted in the hadith of Zayd ibn Sanah, which Abd Allah ibn Salam knew."
"5009","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Abu Hurrah and Said narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, that Ubayy ibn Kab borrowed ten thousand from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and gifted him from the fruit of his land. He returned it, then Ubayy came to him and said: Do you return my fruit to me, though you know I have the best fruit of the people of Madinah? We have no need for what you returned of our gift. So he gave him the ten thousand. To here ended the hadith of Said; Abu Hurrah said in his hadith: When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, returned the money to him, Ubayy accepted his gift."
"5010","Al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb ibn Said narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Kulthum ibn al-Aqmar, he said: Zirr ibn Hubaysh said: Ubayy ibn Kab said to me: If you lend a loan and its owner brings your loan carrying it with a gift with it, take your loan from him and return the gift to him."
"5011","And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Shubah, from Yahya ibn Said, from Anas, he said: If you lend a man a loan, do not ride his mount nor accept his gift, unless there was prior companionship between you before that. Abu Jafar said: This is with us - and Allah knows best - upon Anas ibn Malik making what was of prior companionship, if the like is done after the loan, upon what the companionship had been upon before the loan. From this, with us - and Allah knows best - Ubayy ibn Kab saw fit to gift to Umar after borrowing from him what he borrowed, because he used to exchange gifts with him before that."
"5012","And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Abd Rabbih ibn Said narrated to him that Nafi narrated to him, he said: Abd Allah ibn Umar had a friend to whom he lent, and Abd Allah ibn Umar would gift to him. This is with us - and Allah knows best - from Ibn Umar upon that not being because of the loan; perhaps he had been gifting him before that. In what we mentioned in this chapter from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is what indicates that things taken through causes of others return to what they were taken through the causes of, in their dislikedness, until they are like what was contracted upon. We shall bring after this chapter what strengthens this meaning also, Allah the exalted willing, and from Allah we ask success."
"5013","Chapter 670 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about gifts to rulers. 4334 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that Abu Humayd, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), brother of Banu Saidah, narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed Ibn al-Lutbiyyah, one of the Azd, over the charities of Banu Sulaym. He came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when he audited him, he said: This is for you and this was gifted to me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Would you not have sat in the house of your father or mother until your gift comes to you, if you are truthful?' Then he stood as a preacher, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: 'To proceed: I employ a man among you over work from what Allah the exalted entrusted to me, and he comes to me saying: This is for you and this was gifted to me. Would he not have sat in the house of his father or mother until his gift comes to him? By the One in Whose hand is my soul, none among you takes anything without its right except he will meet Allah carrying it on the Day of Resurrection. Let me not know any of you meeting Allah carrying a camel with a grunt, a cow with a moo, or a sheep that bleats.' Then he raised his hands until I looked at the whiteness of what was under his armpits, then said: 'Have I conveyed?' Abu Humayd said: My eyes saw and my ears heard."
"5014","4335 - And Fahd narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: I heard Abu Humayd al-Saidi saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed a man called Ibn al-Lutbiyyah al-Azdi over charity. When he came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) audited him... then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"5015","4336 - And al-Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn Dawud al-Absi Abu al-Qasim Mamun narrated to me, Isa ibn Hammad Zughbah narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Hisham, from Urwah, that Abu Humayd, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed Ibn al-Lutbiyyah al-Azdi over Banu Sulaym. When he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he audited him... then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"5016","4337 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Hashimi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from Urwah, he said: Abu Humayd al-Saidi informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed Ibn al-Lutbiyyah, one of the Azd. When he audited him upon his arrival... then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. In this hadith is the auditing by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Ibn al-Lutbiyyah over what passed at his hand of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had employed him over; the saying of Ibn al-Lutbiyyah after that what he said, mentioned in this hadith; and the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him in answer, mentioned in this hadith as well."
"5017","4338 - And Raja ibn Zakariyya ibn Kamil al-Khawlani Abu Muhammad narrated to us, Nasr ibn Huraysh al-Samit narrated to us, al-Mushmail - namely Ibn Milhan - narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Said al-Ansari, from Hisham, from Urwah, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed a man over some work. He remained in his work as long as Allah willed, then returned from that work with properties, saying: This is for you and this is a gift gifted to me. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: 'Would he not have sat in the house of his father or mother until his gift comes to him?' Then he went out, ascended the pulpit, praised Allah the exalted and extolled Him, then said: 'What is the matter with men we employ over some works, and when he finishes his work he comes saying: This is for you and this is a gift gifted to me? Would he not have sat in the house of his mother or father until his gift comes to him? By the One in Whose hand is my soul, none takes of this wealth anything without its right, or of this fay anything without its right, except he will come on the Day of Resurrection carrying it upon his neck. Let me not know a man coming on the Day of Resurrection carrying upon his neck a camel with a grunt, a cow with a moo, having carried upon his neck a sheep with a bleat. Have I not conveyed?' They said: Yes. He said: 'O Allah, bear witness that I conveyed.'"
"5018","4339 - And Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dhakwan, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abu Humayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a charity collector to Yemen. He came with much blackness of wealth. When he arrived, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent one to take it over from him, and he would say: This is for you and this is mine. It was said to him: From where is this for you? He said: It was gifted to me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed of that, and he came walking until he ascended the pulpit and said: 'Why do I send peoples over charity, and he comes with much blackness of wealth, and when we send to him one to receive it he says: This is for you and this is mine? If he was truthful, would he not have been gifted while in the house of his mother or father?' Then he said: 'Whoever we send over work and he misappropriates anything, he will bring it on the Day of Resurrection carrying it upon his neck. So fear Allah that any of you comes on the Day of Resurrection with a camel with a grunt upon his neck, a cow with a moo, or a sheep that bleats.'"
"5019","4340 - Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed a man of the Azd called Ibn al-Utbiyyah over charity. When he arrived he said: This is for you and this was gifted to me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon the pulpit, praised Allah, mighty and majestic is He, and extolled Him, then said: 'What is the matter with the worker we send, and he comes saying: This is for you and this was gifted to me? Would he not have sat in the house of his mother or father to see who gifts to him? By the One in Whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, none takes anything from it except he will bring it upon his neck carrying it on the Day of Resurrection: a camel with a grunt, a cow with a moo, or a sheep that bleats.' Then he raised his hands until we saw the knot of his armpits, and said: 'O Allah, have I conveyed? O Allah, have I conveyed? O Allah, have I conveyed?'"
"5020","4341 - And Abd al-Ghani narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: My ears heard and my eyes saw; I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Zayd ibn Thabit heard it with me. Abu Jafar said: In these reports is what indicates that earning through office - from gifts and the like - a ruler over it must return to the property he ruled over, for which what was gifted to him was gifted due to his rule over it. Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them both, differed about this: Abu Yusuf used to say: What the people of war gift to the Imam of the Muslims is his specially, without obligation to return it to Muslim properties. Muhammad said about that: He returns it to the fay of the Muslims, placing its fifth in the place of the fifth and returning the rest to Muslim properties - due to the meaning for which it was gifted to him. This is the better of the two sayings with us, and worthier, with what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter. It was narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to do the like in what was gifted to him while undertaking what he undertook of Muslim affairs."
"5021","As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, my father narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, Amr ibn Murrah narrated to us, from Abu Salih - allegedly al-Hanafi - he said: I entered upon Umm Kulthum daughter of Ali with a veil between me and her, and she said: Sit until I finish, for I am combing my head. She would order me with needs of hers to buy for her, so I sat. Al-Hasan and al-Husayn came, lifted the veil, and entered upon her. When she finished, she ordered me with her need and said: Feed us. I said: The food of the Commander of the Believers will now come to us in varieties. We were brought a broth with cold grains. I said: I thought your food would now be varieties; I said: The food of the Commander of the Believers. Al-Hasan or al-Husayn said: Did they bring you nothing of the citrons? He said: No. She said: A great one among the great ones of the Commander of the Believers sent him many citrons, and he sent to men who came to him and valued them. I saw one of his boys come to him, take a citron, and go to pull it from him; he cried, wanted to take it but refused, so he snatched it from him and left him crying until he valued it, then gave it to him. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is Ali's valuing what was gifted to him of what is mentioned in it, since he did not see it permitted for him to keep it exclusively - for it was only gifted to him due to his rule over what he rules, and because the one who gifted it to him was a great one among his great ones whose gift to him was by what he sought thereby of reaching from his heart to affirming him in the position he was in, by which he was counted great; and it is not so. Likewise whoever gifts to his like among those not so - like Ubayy ibn Kab in what he gifted to Umar ibn al-Khattab, which he accepted from him after returning it to him before that, due to the debt he had upon him, then accepted it from him after returning the debt to him. In this is what indicates that gifts and the like, when done with what we mentioned like it in the chapter before this chapter - of disliking accepting gifts from one in debt by the one to whom that debt is owed - is because that is only intended thereby to abandon the demand by the gifted-to from the gifter for that debt he has upon him. That enters the doors of usury into which its doers fall knowingly and unknowingly. It was also narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, in the like of this meaning, what Abu Masud al-Ansari agreed with him upon."
"5022","As Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Said, freedman of Abu Masud, he said: The head of al-Jalut gifted Abu Masud a hundred thousand dirhams. When Abu Masud came, his wife said: How cool upon the liver! He said: What is that? She said: The head of al-Jalut gifted my daughters. Abu Masud said: How hot upon the liver! He mentioned that to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and informed him of what his wife said. Ali said: What did you say? He said: I said: How hot upon the liver! Ali said: Yes indeed, by Allah - how hot upon the liver! When did the head of Jalut gift your daughters! Carry it and place it in the treasury of the Muslims. This Ali and Abu Masud - in this hadith was narrated from them their returning the gift from whoever gifted it to Abu Masud to the Muslim treasury, due to what he was upon of ruling Muslim affairs, and what Abu Masud was upon for him of commanding his police. In that is what indicates that likewise is the ruling of gifts to rulers from whoever seeks by his gifts to them what he seeks from those upon whom he gifts them thereby: they return to the like of what Ali returned it to, of what we mentioned from him in this hadith. Abu Masud did not oppose him in it. And from Allah we ask success."
"5023","Chapter 671 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his accepting gifts from non-Arab kings and keeping them exclusively, and what was narrated indicating he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that differs from whoever undertakes Muslim affairs after him. 4342 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Thuwayr - meaning Ibn Abi Fakhitah - from his father, namely Abu Fakhitah Said ibn Ilaqah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Khosrau gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he accepted from him; kings gifted to him, and he accepted from them."
"5024","4343 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Mindal ibn Ali narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: al-Muqawqis, ruler of Egypt, gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a glass cup, and he used to drink in it."
"5025","4344 - And Fahd narrated to us, Mualla ibn Rashid narrated to us, Umarah ibn Zad han al-Saydalani narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the king of Dhu Yazan gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a robe for thirty young she-camels, or thirty camels. Umarah said: A man narrated to me from Thabit, from Anas, that he wore it."
"5026","4345 - And Fahd narrated to us, Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Umarah ibn Zad han narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the king of Dhu Yazan gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a robe taken for thirty-three camels, or thirty-three she-camels."
"5027","4346 - And Fahd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Sanani narrated to us, he said: I heard Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Kathir ibn al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib informed me, from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, he said: I witnessed Hunayn with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I and Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon a white mule gifted to him by Farwah ibn Nufathah al-Judhami."
"5028","4347 - And Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Dalham - meaning Ibn Salih - narrated to us, Hujayr - or so-and-so son of Hujayr - narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from his father, that the ruler of Abyssinia gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two plain leather socks, and he wore them and wiped over them."
"5029","4348 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Amr, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clothed me with a robe of sira silk from what Fayruz gifted to him. I wore the lower wrap, and it overwhelmed me in length and width, so I dragged it; I wore the upper wrap and covered myself with it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: 'O Abd Allah ibn Umar, raise the lower wrap, for what touches the dust below the feet of the ankles of the lower wrap is in the Fire.' Abd Allah ibn Muhammad said: I never saw anyone more severe in shortening his lower wrap than Abd Allah ibn Umar."
"5030","4349 - And Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Hatib ibn Abi Baltah to al-Muqawqis, ruler of Alexandria - meaning with his letter with him to him. He accepted his letter, honored Hatib, treated his stay well, then sent him back to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), gifting him with Hatib clothing, a gray mule with its saddle, and two slave girls: one of them the mother of Ibrahim; as for the other, he gave her to Jahm ibn Qays al-Abdari - she is the mother of Zakariyya ibn Jahm, who was the deputy of Amr ibn al-As over Egypt. I heard Yunus saying: Harun ibn Abd Allah the judge said to me: O Abu Musa, we heard with you here something we had not heard before coming to you. I said to him: What is it? He said: A hadith from Ibn Wahb, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari informed me - though what we knew of the hadith of Ibn Shihab from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd is what he narrated from Urwah from him, or from others besides him from him, among them Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman. I said to him: It is as you heard; Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us of it, then I narrated to him this hadith. Abu Jafar said: His house is the house of al-Ayzar by the police. Abu Jafar said: More than one of the people of knowledge of history claimed that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd had performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so we entered his hadith in the musnad for that."
"5031","4350 - And Musa ibn al-Hasan, known as al-Saqali, narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Makki narrated to us, Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Bashir ibn al-Muhajir, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: The chief of the Copts gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two Coptic sister slave girls and a mule. As for the mule, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to ride it; as for one of the two girls, he took her as a concubine and she bore him Ibrahim; as for the other, he gave her to Hassan ibn Thabit al-Ansari. Abu Jafar said: In these reports is the acceptance by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the gifts of those whose gifts to him are mentioned in these reports, his keeping them exclusively, and his leaving returning them to Muslim properties. An asker asked about the meaning in that, and in his differing between himself and others of his nation in this meaning, as we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. Our answer to him in that: Allah, mighty and majestic is He, had singled out the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the properties of the people of war with a particularity differing between him and others of his nation. He, mighty and majestic is He, said in what He sent down of His Book upon him: {Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger from them, you did not urge upon it any horse or camel.} The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was particularized with that. With this meaning Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, argued against al-Abbas and Ali with what he argued them in what they disputed to him in."
"5032","4351 - As Yazid ibn Sinan and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, they said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying to Ali and al-Abbas: Do you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'We are not inherited; what we leave is charity'? They said: Yes. He said: Allah, mighty and majestic is He, singled out His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a particularity He did not single out anyone among people with, saying: {Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger from them, you did not urge upon it any horse or camel, but Allah gives His messengers mastery over whom He wills, and Allah is over all things capable.} Allah, mighty and majestic is He, bestowed upon His Messenger Banu al-Nadir. By Allah, he neither kept it exclusively from you nor took it apart from you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would take from it his household's provision - or his and his family's provision for a year - and make the rest like other wealth. Then he turned to those men - meaning Uthman, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, and Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with them - and said: I adjure you by Allah by Whose permission the heaven and earth stand: Do you know that? They said: Yes."
"5033","4352 - And as al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Shihab, Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan heard saying: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The properties of Banu al-Nadir were of what Allah, mighty and majestic is He, bestowed upon His Messenger from what the Muslims did not urge upon with horse or camel. They were the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exclusively. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would spend upon his family from them a year's provision, and make the rest in horses and mounts as preparation in the path of Allah, mighty and majestic is He. Abu Jafar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - Allah had singled him out with what He singled him out with of polytheist properties over which there was no urging with horse or camel. Of that was what came of polytheist gifts over which there was no urging with horse or camel, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept them exclusively for that. Whoever besides him of his nation in the like of it is otherwise. Of him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding whoever kept exclusively something of it is what we mentioned in the reports we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. Then an objector said: It was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his returning polytheist gifts and his saying: 'We do not accept the zabd of the polytheists,' meaning their support. He mentioned in that."
"5034","4353 - What Abu Ayyub al-Urdunni, known as Ibn Khalaf, narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Bazzar narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from al-Hasan, from Iyad ibn Himar, he said: He was the intimate of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Jahiliyyah, and he gifted him a gift which he returned, saying: 'We do not accept the zabd of the polytheists.'"
"5035","And what Abu Ayyub narrated to us, Khalaf narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, he said: I asked al-Hasan: What is the zabd of the polytheists? He said: Their support."
"5036","4354 - And what Fahd and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Imran - namely al-Qattan - narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Iyad ibn Himar, he said: I gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a she-camel - or he said: a gift - and he said to me: 'Have you submitted?' I said: No. He said: 'I have been forbidden the zabd of the polytheists.'"
"5037","4355 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu Mamar Abd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, Abd al-Warith ibn Said narrated to us, Abu al-Tayyah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, that Iyad ibn Himar was the intimate of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Jahiliyyah. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent, he came to him with a she-camel. When he saw it he said: 'O Iyad, what is this?' He said: I gifted it to you. He said: 'Lead it.' So he led it, then said: 'Return it.' So he returned it. He said: 'O Iyad, have you submitted yet?' He said: No. So he did not accept it, saying: 'Indeed Allah the exalted has forbidden us the zabd of the polytheists.' He said: The Arabs call the gift al-zabd. Abu Ubaydah said: Al-hirmi is one of the people of the sanctuary, and also the friend - he is called hirmi. This objector said: In these reports is the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about polytheist gifts what he said in them, and his informing Iyad that Allah the exalted forbade him accepting them - contrary to what you narrated in this chapter of the acceptance by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he accepted of them. Our answer to him in that: It is possible Allah the exalted forbade him accepting the zabd of the polytheists in one state and permitted it in another state; his preventing him from that was before His sending down upon him: {Whatever Allah bestowed upon His Messenger from them...} - the verse we recited in this chapter - then He sent down upon him this verse, making for them of their properties what came without urging from him upon it with horse or camel. What came to him of their gifts was like what he gained over them of their properties besides that without urging upon it with horse or camel, so he accepted them for that. Allah knows best what that was upon in reality, and from Him we ask success."
"5038","Chapter 672 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying about the blind man: 'Take us to visit that seeing man.' 4356 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar al-Ramadi narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Take us to Banu Waqif; let us visit that seeing man' - though he was blind. We examined this hadith to determine the meaning for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned that man as seeing though blind, while Allah, mighty and majestic is He, mentioned his like in His Book as blind. From that: His saying the exalted: {There is no blame upon the blind}; His saying: {He frowned and turned away, that the blind man came to him}; His saying: {You cannot guide the blind from their error}; His saying: {Can you guide the blind though they do not see?} We found Allah the exalted mentioned one with blindness with other than that, saying: {It is not the eyes that are blind, but blind are the hearts in the breasts.} In that is what indicates that the blind may be called seeing for his seeing with his heart what he sees thereby, though veiled in sight. That indicates it is permitted to describe with blindness one who sees, and permitted to describe with sight what is in his heart. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned that man by the better of his two states, though he could mention him by the other of them. And from Allah we ask success."
"5039","Chapter 673 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his answering one who asked him about possessors of noble traits in the Jahiliyyah who did not reach Islam. 4357 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Dawud - namely Ibn Abi Hind - from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Ibn Judan in the Jahiliyyah used to join kinship and feed the poor - will that benefit him? He said: 'No, O Aishah. He never said: My Lord, forgive me my sin on the Day of Recompense.'"
"5040","4358 - And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Aishah, the like of it - except he did not say in it 'O Aishah,' and said in it in addition to what is in the hadith of Abu Umayyah: And he freed the captive."
"5041","4358m - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"5042","4359 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, Umarah ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me about my cousin Ibn Judan. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'What was it?' I said: He used to slaughter fat camels, fetch water, honor the neighbor, entertain the guest, speak truthfully, join kinship, fulfill covenants, free the captive, feed food, and render trusts. He said: 'Did he ever say one day: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the fire of Hell?' I said: He did not know what the fire of Hell was. He said: 'Then no.' Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Aishah about Ibn Judan when she asked him about him, describing to him of his states in the Jahiliyyah what she described, and his answer to her in that that it will not benefit him; he added nothing for her beyond that."
"5043","4360 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Murri ibn Qatari, a man of Banu Thal, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, my father used to do such-and-such and join kinship. He said: 'Your father sought a matter and attained it.'"
"5044","4361 - And Ibrahim narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Simak, from Murri, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: I said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): My father used to feed the poor and free slaves - is there for him in that any reward? He said: 'Your father sought a matter and attained it.' Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Adi when he asked him about his father, describing to him of the states he was upon what he described, and the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him thereupon with what he mentioned of his answer to him in this hadith: that what was from his father was only for a meaning he attained, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not go beyond that."
"5045","4362 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abu Naamah, from Abd al-Aziz, a man of Banu Dabbah, from Salman ibn Amir, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My father used to entertain the guest and do this and that, yet he died before Islam. He said: 'That will not benefit him.' When he turned away he said: 'Bring the elder to me.' When he came he said: 'That will not benefit him, but in his offspring is that they shall never be poor, never be humbled, and never be disgraced.' Al-Bukhari mentioned that this Abd al-Aziz mentioned in this hadith is Abd al-Aziz ibn Bashir, a man of Banu Dabbah; others of the people of hadith said he is of the offspring of Salman ibn Amir. The answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith did not depart from what he answered Aishah and Adi with in the first two hadiths, except what is in it of what he said to him after ordering him returned to him, mentioned in this hadith of his. That is possible with us - and Allah knows best - that the return of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of him was by something the angel said to him about the father of Salman: that he used to do what he did of those things so his offspring would attain what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed Salman they shall never be poor, never be humbled, and never be disgraced - just as he returned the man who had asked him in the hadith of Abu Qatadah: What if I am killed in the path of Allah patiently, seeking reward, advancing not retreating - will Allah the exalted expiate my sins? He said: 'Yes.' When he turned away he called him and said to him: 'Except if he has a debt - thus Jibril, peace be upon him, said to me.'"
"5046","4363 - And al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: Hakim ibn Hizam said: O Messenger of Allah, I used to leave something voluntarily in the Jahiliyyah. He said: 'You keep what you submitted upon of prior good.'"
"5047","4364 - Musab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz al-Darawardi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Muslim ibn Shihab al-Zuhri narrated to us, from his uncle, from Urwah, that Hakim ibn Hizam informed him that he said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Have you seen things I used to do devoutly in the Jahiliyyah - charity, freeing, joining kinship - is there for me in them any reward? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'You submitted upon prior good of yours.' In this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in answer to Hakim about what he asked him, is his saying to him: 'You submitted upon prior good of yours.' That is possible: that good is the good for which its like is praised for what was from him, though there is no reward for him in it - so that does not depart from what is in the first reports we narrated in this chapter."
"5048","4365 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Hisham informed us, from Shayban, from Jabir, from Amir, from Alqamah, from Salamah ibn Yazid, he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, our mother used to entertain the guest and join kinship, yet she buried alive in the Jahiliyyah and died before Islam - will any deed benefit her if we do it for her? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Islam benefits none but whoever reaches it. Your mother and what she buried are in the Fire.' In this hadith is that Islam benefits none but whoever reaches it - meaning embraces it and enters it. The mentioned benefit in this hadith is possible to be the benefit of Islam, not of other than it of prior praiseworthy matters in the Jahiliyyah; and possible to be beneficial to its people in other than Islam, as it would benefit them had they done it in Islam - except that the whole of what we narrated in this chapter returns to the intent of the doers of things in doing them, for what they did them: as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Deeds are but by intentions, and to each man what he intended. Whoever's emigration was to Allah, mighty and majestic is He, and to His Messenger, his emigration is to Allah and His Messenger; and whoever's emigration was to a worldly thing he attains or a woman he marries, his emigration is to what he emigrated to.' We shall mention that with its chains later in this book, Allah the exalted willing. Since deeds in Islam benefit their doers only by their intending Allah, mighty and majestic is He, thereby - being thereby desiring Him and aiming to Him, so He rewards them thereupon - and when they do them for other than that of their worldly matters they are not so, with nothing in that for them - what they did in the Jahiliyyah of good, without Islam with them nor the intentions by which they intend Allah, mighty and majestic is He, in their deeds therein, is worthier not to be rewarded upon, and not to be given thereby except what they aimed at in their worldly life of worldly causes. These reports we narrated in this chapter thus agree, the meanings of some confirming others, and nothing in them departs from anything to contradict it. And success is from Allah."
"5049","Chapter 674 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating the intent of Allah, mighty and majestic is He, by His saying in the verse of the mukatabs: {Give them of the wealth of Allah which He gave you.} 4366 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Men of knowledge informed me, among them Yunus ibn Yazid and al-Layth ibn Sad, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Barirah came to me and said: O Aishah, I contracted with my people for nine ounces, an ounce each year - so help me. She had not paid anything of her contract. Aishah said to her: Return to your people; if they wish that I give them that all together with your allegiance to me, I shall do it. She went to her people, offered that to them, and they refused, saying: If she wishes to count it as charity upon you, let her do it, with your allegiance to us. She mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: 'That does not prevent you from her. Buy and free, for allegiance is only to whoever frees.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among the people, praised Allah and extolled Him, saying: 'To proceed: What is the matter with peoples stipulating conditions not in the Book of Allah? Whoever stipulates a condition not in the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic is He, it is void, even if a hundred conditions. The decree of Allah is truer, the condition of Allah surer. Allegiance is only to whoever frees.'"
"5050","4367 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and al-Layth ibn Sad informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that."
"5051","4368 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: Barirah came to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: I contracted with my people for nine ounces, an ounce each year. Aishah said: If your people wish that I count it for them with your allegiance to me, I shall do it. Barirah went to her people, told them that, and they refused it upon her. She came from her people while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting, and said: I offered that to them but they refused unless allegiance be to them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard that and asked her; Aishah informed him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Take her, stipulating allegiance to them, for allegiance is to whoever frees.' Aishah did so, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among the people, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Abu Jafar said: In these two hadiths is what indicates that nothing of his contract must be remitted for one who contracts his slave; and that His saying the exalted: {Give them of the wealth of Allah which He gave you} is upon urging and encouraging good - of helping mukatabs, by those who contracted them and others besides them, from their properties until they are freed by coming out of their contracts - as said this saying who said it of the people of knowledge, among them Abu Hanifah, Malik, al-Thawri, Zufar, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad; contrary to what others besides them of the people of knowledge said, among them al-Shafii, who held that the interpretation of His saying, mighty and majestic is He: {Give them of the wealth of Allah which He gave you} is upon obligation and incumbency, not upon recommendation and urging, and upon that being from the contract they contract them upon. In the two hadiths we narrated is the standing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon Barirah not having paid anything of her contract, and upon the saying of Aishah to her: Return to your people; if they wish that I give them that all, or count it for them all, with your allegiance to me, I shall do it; and the leaving by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of censuring that upon her. That indicates the obligatoriness of the entire contract to the contracted for upon the contractors - for had remission been obligatory upon them of it for whom they contract, he would have said to Aishah: Why do you pay them for her what is not due to them upon her, and what Allah, mighty and majestic is He, obligated for her upon them to drop from her? The like of that also is what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what was from him about Juwayriyah daughter of al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar."
"5052","4369 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took the captives of Banu al-Mustaliq, Juwayriyah daughter of al-Harith fell in the share of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas, or a cousin of his. She contracted herself. She said: She was a beautiful sweet woman; hardly anyone saw her but she captivated him. She came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking his help in her contract. By Allah, no sooner did I see her at the door of the room but I disliked her, knowing he would see from her the like of what I saw. She said: O Messenger of Allah, I am Juwayriyah daughter of al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar, chief of his people. What befell me has not been hidden; I fell in the share of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas, or a cousin of his, and contracted him. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking his help upon my contract. He said: 'Have you aught better than that?' She said: What is it, O Messenger of Allah? He said: 'I pay your contract for you and marry you.' She said: Yes. He said: 'I have done it.' The news went out to the people that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Juwayriyah daughter of al-Harith. They said: In-laws of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)! So they released what was in their hands. She said: A hundred households of Banu al-Mustaliq were freed by his marrying her. We know no woman greater in blessing upon her people than her. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered to Juwayriyah payment of her entire contract for her to the one who contracted her. That indicates her entire contract was upon her to the one who contracted her, without reduction for her from him; the like of that is what was narrated from him also about Salman al-Farisi."
"5053","4370 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad al-Zuhri informed us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq. And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Idris al-Awdi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us. Then both together said: From Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Ibn Abbas, Salman al-Farisi narrated to us his account from his mouth, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: 'Contract.' I asked my master that, and kept at him until he contracted me upon my reviving for him three hundred palm trees and forty ounces of silver. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Help your companion with palms.' Each man among them helped me according to his capacity: with thirty, twenty, fifteen, and ten. Then he said to me: 'O Salman, go dig for them; when you want to place them, do not place them until you come to me informing me, so I am the one who places them with my hand.' I stood digging, my companions helping me, until we dug their drinking channels for three hundred young palms; each man brought what he helped me with of palms. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, placing them with his hand, leveling their soil, until he finished all of them. He said: By the One in Whose hand is my soul, not one of them died. The dirhams remained upon me. While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among his Companions, a man among his Companions came to him with the like of an egg of gold he found in some mine, giving it as charity. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'What has the poor Persian mukatab done? Call him to me.' I was called and came. He said: 'Go pay it for what is upon you of wealth.' I said: Where does this fall of what is upon me, O Messenger of Allah? He said: 'Indeed Allah the exalted will fulfill it.' The wording is Fahd's. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not take the master of Salman to remit of his contract nor reduce it for him. In that also is proof of what we mentioned. Then we found the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed in interpreting this verse as those after them differed in interpreting it. It was narrated in its interpretation from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, what some carry beyond him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5054","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah Musa ibn Masud narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Abd al-Ala al-Thalabi, he said: I witnessed Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami contract a slave of his for four thousand dirhams, stipulating upon him that if he fails he returns to slavery, and what I took from you is mine. He remitted a thousand dirhams of the four thousand, then said: I heard your friend Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: {Give them of the wealth of Allah which He gave you} - it is the fourth. Thus al-Thawri narrated from Abd al-Ala as we mentioned, not carrying it beyond Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5055","And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: {Give them of the wealth of Allah which He gave you} - he said: A fourth of the contract."
"5056","And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb informed us, he said: Ahmad ibn Sulayman al-Rahawi narrated to us, Yazid - meaning Ibn Harun - narrated to us, Abd al-Malik - namely Ibn Abi Sulayman - informed us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Ayan, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, that he contracted a slave of his for four thousand dirhams, then remitted a thousand; then said: Had I not seen Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, contract a slave of his then remit the fourth, I would not have done it. Abu Jafar said: Thus Jarir also narrated this hadith from Ata ibn al-Saib, not carrying it beyond Ali; thus Abd al-Malik ibn Ayan also narrated it, not carrying it beyond Ali. Ibn Jurayj narrated it from Ata ibn al-Saib, carrying it beyond Ali to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5057","4371 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Qawwas narrated to us, Abd al-Majid ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us. And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us. Then both together said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, Ata ibn al-Saib narrated to us that Abd Allah ibn Habib informed him, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: {Give them of the wealth of Allah which He gave you} - he said: 'A fourth of the contract.'"
"5058","4371m - And as Ahmad narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Said informed us, Hajjaj - meaning Ibn Muhammad - narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Ata ibn al-Saib informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Habib, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Ibn Jurayj said: More than one informed me from Ata that he used to narrate this hadith without mentioning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar said: The one who raised this hadith from Ali to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is Ibn Jurayj, from Ata; and Ata had become confused at the end, and his unconfused hadith from him is what four narrate from him apart from others: al-Thawri, Shubah, Hammad ibn Salamah, and Hammad ibn Zayd. The hadith of Ibn Jurayj from him is what he took from him in the state of confusion, so that does not necessitate raising this hadith. Abu Jafar said: This hadith with us is also not a proof for the obligatoriness of remitting part of the contract from the mukatab upon his master, since it is possible it was from Ali upon seeking good, not upon its obligatoriness upon him. Then we looked: Was anything narrated in that from any of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"5059","We found Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb al-Washihi narrated to us, Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, Ubayd Allah narrated to me, from my father, he said: Maymun said: From my uncle, he said: My mother and family narrated to me that my grandfather said to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: Contract me. He said: Offer. I said: For a hundred ounces. He said: He did not ask me more. He wanted something to give me but found none, so he sent to Hafsah saying: I contracted my slave and want to give him something - send me dirhams. She sent him two hundred dirhams. He said: Take them, may Allah bless them for you. Allah, mighty and majestic is He, blessed them for me - I freed more than one from them. I sought his permission, saying: O Commander of the Believers, I want you to permit me to go to Iraq. He said: Since I contracted you, go where you wish. Some clients of Banu Ghifar wanted to accompany me, saying: Speak to the Commander of the Believers to write us a letter by which we are honored. I knew he would dislike that, so I spoke to him; he rebuked me, never having rebuked me before, saying: Do you want to wrong people? You are the model of the Muslims. I went out, and when we arrived I came with a carpet and a velvet cloth, saying: O Commander of the Believers, these two are a gift to you. He looked at them, admired them, then returned them to me, saying: A remainder of your contract remains - seek help with them in your contract. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith from Umar is what indicates he did not remit of his contract anything."
"5060","And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir al-Dubai narrated to us, from Juwayriyah ibn Asma, from Muslim ibn Abi Maryam, from a slave of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Uthman, Commander of the Believers, sent me in trade. I came to him, and he praised my management. One day I stood to him saying: I want the contract. He frowned, then said: Yes - were it not for a verse in the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic is He, I would not do it. I contract you for a hundred thousand dirhams upon your counting them to me in two counts; by Allah, I shall not diminish for you a dirham of them. I went out from him, and al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, met me, saying: What is this I see in you? I said: The Commander of the Believers sent me in trade; I came to him and he praised my management. I stood to him saying: O Commander of the Believers, I ask you the contract. He frowned, then said: Were it not for a verse in the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic is He, I would not do it. I contract you for a hundred thousand upon your counting them to me in two counts; by Allah, I shall not diminish for you a dirham of them. He said: Return. He entered upon him, stood, and said: O Commander of the Believers, so-and-so - contract him. He frowned, then said: Yes - were it not for a verse in the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic is He, I would not do it. I contract him for a hundred thousand upon his counting them to me in two counts; by Allah, I shall not diminish for him a dirham of them. Al-Zubayr grew angry, saying: By Allah, I shall stand before you - I only ask you a need you bar with an oath - and said with his hand thus: Contract him. So he contracted me. Al-Zubayr took me to his family, gave me a hundred thousand, and said: Go seek therein of Allah's bounty; if a matter overcomes you, pay Uthman what is his of it. I went, sought therein of Allah's bounty, paid al-Zubayr his wealth and Uthman his wealth, with eighty thousand remaining in my hand. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is Uthman's swearing not to diminish for this mukatab of his contract a dirham, with al-Zubayr standing upon that from him and leaving opposing him in it. In that is what indicates that His saying, mighty and majestic is He, in this verse: {Give them of the wealth of Allah which He gave you} was with them not upon remitting anything of the contract from its mukatab."
"5061","And we found Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mumin al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, from al-Husayn ibn Waqid, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, he said: I heard my father saying about His saying, mighty and majestic is He: {Give them of the wealth of Allah which He gave you} - he said: Urging people to that. Abu Jafar said: In that is what indicates what we mentioned in this chapter: that is upon urging, not obligation - for since people other than the contractors were intended thereby, not upon its being obligatory upon them, likewise the contractors were intended thereby, not upon obligation upon them. Since there was disagreement in that as we mentioned, worthiest of what was said in it is what agreed with what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then from those we mentioned of his Companions. And from Allah we ask success."
"5062","Chapter 676 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated of what the people of knowledge differed about selling a slave woman with a husband: some say it is a divorce for her, others say it is not a divorce for her - with what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what was from him about Barirah. 4372 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yala Abu Muhayyah narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she bought Barirah, stipulating allegiance to those who sold her. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Allegiance is to whoever buys.' So she freed her, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her a choice; her husband was free, so she chose herself, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) separated them. In this chapter are many reports we deferred to places worthier of them, which we shall bring after this chapter in the accounts of Barirah in this book, Allah the exalted willing. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed about selling a slave woman with a husband: some said it is a divorce for her, others said it is not a divorce for her. Among those narrated from among them that it is not a divorce for her: Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"5063","As Fahd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Said Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik informed us, from Ubayd Allah, from Yasar ibn Numayr, he said: Umar ordered me to buy him a slave girl. I bought him a slave girl with a husband, and he ordered me to buy him her private part from her husband. So I bought him her private part from her husband. Among them: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5064","As Ahmad ibn Khalid ibn Yazid al-Farisi narrated to us, Dawud ibn Amr al-Dabbi narrated to us, Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ishaq ibn Kab, brother of Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi, that his father Kab bought for Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, a slave girl. He asked her: Do you have a husband? She said: Yes. He said: He sent her to my father, saying: Return her. So he returned her, bought her private part from her husband, and she was returned to him, so he accepted her."
"5065","And as Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariyya and Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, they said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Ali and Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with them both, about His saying, mighty and majestic is He: {And married women except those your right hands possess} - Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Polytheist women when taken captive; Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Polytheist and Muslim women."
"5066","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Dawud and Ubaydah informed us, from al-Shabi, that Murrah ibn Sharahil, keeper of the Salihin, sent to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, a slave girl. He asked her: Do you have a husband? She said: Yes. So he returned her, writing to Murrah: I found your gift occupied. Murrah bought her private part from her husband for five hundred dirhams and sent her to him, so he accepted her. Among them: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5067","As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, that Abd Allah ibn Amir gifted to Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, a slave girl with a husband, which he bought for him in Basrah. Uthman said: I shall not approach her until her husband leaves her. Ibn Amir pleased her husband, so he left her. Among them: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf."
"5068","As Yunus narrated to us, Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf bought a slave girl from Asim ibn Adi, finding her with a husband, so he returned her."
"5069","Yunus narrated to us, Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, Ibn Shihab narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Asim ibn Adi, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf bought from him a slave girl with a husband without knowing that. When he learned that, he returned her to him. Among them: Abd Allah ibn Umar."
"5070","As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he said about a slave of his with an owned wife who was sold: He is more entitled to her wherever he meets her. Some said: It is a divorce for her. Among those who said that: Abd Allah ibn Abbas."
"5071","As Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to say about selling a slave woman: It is her divorce. Among them: Ubayy ibn Kab."
"5072","As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, from Ubayy ibn Kab, that he said: Selling a slave woman is her divorce. Among them: Jabir ibn Abd Allah and Anas ibn Malik."
"5073","As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, and Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, they said: Selling a slave woman is her divorce. Abu Jafar said: Their disagreement in that was only due to what they differed in of what they interpreted His saying, mighty and majestic is He, upon: {And married women except those your right hands possess.} Some held they are captive women with husbands left behind in the abode of war. Their opponents in that held it is every sold woman with a husband. What those of the first of these two sayings held is with us worthier in interpreting this verse, due to what we narrated earlier in this book of the hadith of Abu al-Khalil, from Abu Alqamah, from Abu Said, about the revelation of this verse in that; and what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his affirming Barirah upon the marriage she was upon before Aishah bought her, and his giving her a choice after her freeing - what indicates her purchase was not a divorce from her husband for her. Then an objector said: You narrated from Ibn Abbas that he used to say: Selling a slave woman is her divorce, yet you narrate from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his giving Barirah a choice after her freeing between remaining with her husband and leaving him - a severe contradiction. Our answer to him in that: What he mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his giving Barirah a choice after her freeing between remaining with her husband and leaving him was narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as mentioned; we shall bring it among these narrated hadiths about Barirah later, Allah the exalted willing. Some used to say: That saying of Ibn Abbas after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - that selling a slave woman is her divorce - was not with him contrary to what he narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his giving Barirah a choice after her freeing between remaining with her husband and leaving him; that was only because Barirah was bought by Aishah, who is of those for whom private parts are not lawful, so the marriage of Barirah remained after that as before it; while the purchase by men for whom private parts are lawful like her entails the lawfulness of the private part to them, and in its lawfulness to them is the lawfulness of the marriages upon them to whoever held them upon them. Abu Jafar said: This is possible to be what Ibn Abbas held in that. After that, we considered the cause by which separation falls between captive women with husbands in the abode of war and their husbands there, and found them separated from their husbands by slavery falling upon them through captivity - while in that state they had not become lawful to particular men, due to what is in them."
"5074","Chapter 677 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his giving Barirah a choice between leaving her husband and remaining with him: Was that due to the freeing that befell her in all states of her husband, of freedom or slavery, or particular apart from freedom? Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun informed us, Shubah informed us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the husband of Barirah was free. 4373 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The husband of Barirah was free, and she was given a choice, choosing herself."
"5075","4374 - And Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, she said: The husband of Barirah was free. When she was freed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her a choice, and she chose herself."
"5076","Abu Umayya narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Dahhak al-Bablutti narrated to us, Abu Jafar al-Razi narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the husband of Barira was a free man, a freed slave of the family of Abu Ahmad. Abu Jafar said: In these reports from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) is that the husband of Barira was free on the day the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her the choice. And it has been narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that he was a slave."
"5077","As Abu Umayya narrated to us, Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwa narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that Barira was freed when Aisha freed her, and that her husband was a slave."
"5078","And as Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Ismail ibn Salim narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us (h), and as Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The husband of Barira was a slave."
"5079","And as Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz i.e. al-Darawardi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwa narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the husband of Barira was a slave. So al-Darawardi inserted between Hisham ibn Urwa and the one after him in the isnad of this hadith Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, so it returned to al-Qasim from Aisha, and al-Darawardi agreed with Hatim ibn Ismail in that while Jarir differed from him in it. So in these reports is the opposite of what is in the first reports concerning the husband of Barira. Some people said: There has been narrated from Aisha in other than these reports what indicates the correctness of what is in these reports, so he mentioned in that."
"5080","4375 - What Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, and al-Qasim ibn Jafar confirmed it to me from him, he said: Abu Ali al-Hanafi narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mawhab narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrating from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she had a slave boy and a slave girl who were married, so she said: O Messenger of Allah, I want to free them both, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Begin with the man before the woman. He said: So in that is what indicated that the husband of Barira was a slave, and that a slave girl has no choice when she is freed while her husband is free. Our answer to him in that is that this is understood to be that the male of these two slaves is other than the husband of Barira, and that the female in it was other than Barira, because Aisha only bought her then freed her, and she was not in her ownership before that. And we examined this hadith and found it far from acceptance by hearts, because it is impossible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would command in a matter protection for one of two and not protection for the other of them, and that he would command a freeing that nullifies the right of the wife which is obligatory for her by his law when she is freed, while protecting the husband by not making that choice obligatory for his wife. But it is in our view - and Allah knows best - that he intended by it from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that there be from her in her two slaves directing them to kinship ties with them, and that that is more deserving for her than freeing them, as has been narrated from him of what he said to his wife Maymuna when she freed a slave girl of hers."
"5081","4376 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad narrated to us, Ibn Lahia narrated to us, Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to us, from Kurayb the freed slave of Ibn Abbas who said: I heard Maymuna the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I freed a slave girl of mine in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: If you had given her to your maternal uncles it would have been greater for your reward."
"5082","4377 - And as al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Maymuna the like of it. And it is possible that when there came to Aisha the intention for freeing that came to her, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to free of her two slaves the greater of them in reward for freeing, because freeing a male is better than freeing females according to what is in the hadith of Murra ibn Kab which we have mentioned before in this book of ours, and he deferred the matter of the slave girl for her to consider regarding her between keeping her and giving her as kinship to one whom she might join with her of her relatives. Then we looked: has anything been narrated about the husband of Barira from other than Aisha or not?"
"5083","4378 - So we found Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, Qatada narrated to us, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the husband of Barira was a black slave named Mughith, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave four rulings concerning her: that her masters stipulated the wala, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled that wala belongs to the one who paid the price, and he gave her the choice, and he commanded her to observe waiting period, and charity was given to her so she gifted some of it to Aisha, so that was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so he said: It is for us a gift, and for her charity."
"5084","4379 - And we found Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Khalid narrated to us, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: When Barira was given the choice, I saw her husband following her in the streets of Medina while his tears flowed upon his beard, so al-Abbas spoke to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his behalf to intercede with her, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Your husband and the father of your child! So she said: Do you command me with him O Messenger of Allah? So he said: I am only an intercessor. So she said: If you are an intercessor then I have no need of him, and she chose herself, and he was called Mughith, and he was a slave of the family of al-Mughira of Banu Makhzum. Abu Jafar said: So in this from Ibn Abbas without disagreement from him is that he was a slave. And when this disagreement occurred in this meaning, correcting what was narrated in it became obligatory, since we find a way to that, so it must be that he was a slave at one time and was free at another time. The state of slavery may be followed by freedom, while the state of freedom is not followed by slavery, so we made it that he was a slave in the beginning then became free after that at the time when his wife was given the choice between staying with him and leaving him. Then we returned to what reflection requires in that and found that a slave girl belongs to her master to marry her off while he owns her to whom he sees fit of free men and slaves, and we found that when he frees her while she has a slave husband whom he had married her to, she has the choice to leave him or stay with him. And they differed when he is free: some said it is likewise, and of those who said that: Abu Hanifa, al-Thawri and all their companions. And some said: she has no choice to leave him, and of those: Malik and the generality of the people of Hijaz. An arguer argued for them saying: the choice was given to her when her husband is a slave because he cannot marry off her daughters nor protect her as a free man protects her, so the choice was given for that. Their opponents replied that the matter for which she was given the choice is her owning herself, so she was given to carry out what her master had contracted upon her or to lift it from herself. And it was contrasted with the minor girl when her father marries her off before puberty then she reaches puberty, for no choice is given to her whether he married her to a free man or a slave. And the report of Hisham ibn Urwa was then discussed."
"5085","4380 - So he mentioned what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Ismail ibn Salim narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The husband of Barira was a slave, and if he had been free the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have given her the choice. Our answer to him in that is that we do not know who is the author of this statement among the narrators of this hadith: is it from Aisha? Or from one below her among them? And since we did not know that it is from Aisha so as to make it the statement of a Companion with no opponent to him in it, it is possible that it is from the statement of a Tabii being his opinion from her, or from the statement of one below him among the narrators of this hadith. Then we looked: has anything been narrated from any of the Tabiun that requires the choice for this freed woman or not?"
"5086","We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father who said: The slave girl has the choice when she is freed, even if her husband is a man from Quraysh. So if Urwa is the one who said what is in the hadith we narrated, his statement in that is countered by the statement of Tawus which opposes it. And success is from Allah."
"5087","678 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the choice he gave to Barira when she was freed: is it like her choice if her husband had given her choice? Or otherwise? 4381 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahid ibn Abbud narrated to us, Marwan i.e. al-Tatari narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us - and he mentioned another before him i.e. Ibn Lahia - he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Amr ibn Umayya al-Damri that he narrated to him that men from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any slave girl who is under a slave and is freed, she has the choice, as long as her husband has not had intercourse with her. Thus Marwan narrated this hadith from Ibn Lahia and al-Layth, with one wording, and Ibn Wahb narrated it from them both with different wordings."
"5088","4382 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: And Ibn Lahia informed me, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Jafar, from al-Fadl ibn Hasan al-Damri who said: I heard men from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: When the slave girl is freed while she is under a slave, her affair is in her hand; if she remains until he has intercourse with her, she is his wife and cannot leave him."
"5089","4383 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, and al-Layth ibn Sad informed me, from Ibn Abi Jafar, from al-Damri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So we understood thereby that Marwan brought this hadith with his narration of it from Ibn Lahia and al-Layth as he narrated it from them both, while in reality this wording with which he narrated it is only the wording of Ibn Lahia, and that the hadith of al-Layth differs from it according to what we mentioned from each of them from the narration of Ibn Wahb from them both."
"5090","4384 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Ibn Lahia informed me, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad that Aisha the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with her) informed him that Barira was under an owned slave; when she was freed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: You own yourself more; if you wish you stay with your husband, and if you wish you leave him, as long as he has not touched you."
"5091","4385 - And Ibrahim ibn Ahmad ibn Marwan al-Wasiti narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Ala al-Kalai Abu Abd Allah al-Shami narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Ishaq al-Dimashqi narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Barira in the hadith of her freeing and his giving her the choice (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): If he has intercourse with you then you have no choice."
"5092","4386 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Antaki known as al-Qalla narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Abu Jafar and Aban ibn Salih, from Mujahid and Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that Barira was freed, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her the choice and said to her: If he approaches you then you have no choice. Abu Jafar said: So in what we narrated is that his approaching her cuts her off from choosing herself over her husband, and that is of what cannot occur from him to her in the sitting in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed her of that in it. So that indicates that the choice remains for her after rising from her sitting in which she learned of the choice being obligatory for her, until there occurs from her what cuts her off from that, of her allowing between her husband and herself that he do with her of what is not proper for him to do with her except while their marriage stands between him and her, as the people of Medina say in that, not as the Kufans say in it that the choice is only obligatory for her in her sitting in which she learns of that in it as long as she does not rise from it, or take up another act, or other speech. And when that is likewise in his approaching her by her choice, likewise is her enabling him to kiss her and what is besides that of what is not lawful for him of her except while the marriage between them stands as it is, and that from her is as if she said with her tongue: I have chosen my husband. And this indicates that one to whom belongs effecting divorce upon one of his two wives by his saying to them: one of you is divorced, that his approaching one of them cuts him off from that, and that he is thereby choosing her by his approaching her. And the like of that in his saying to his two slave girls: one of you is free, so he has the choice to effect that freeing upon whichever of them he wishes, then he does not effect it until he has intercourse with one of them, and he is by that intercourse choosing her, as his saying with his tongue: I have chosen her. And the like of that: the slave girl he buys then finds in her a defect obliging for him thereby returning her upon her seller, then he does not do that until there occurs from him to her of what is not lawful for him of her except by his owning her, so that cuts him off from returning her with that defect upon her seller, and that from him is as his saying with his tongue: I am pleased with her with her defect. And of what confirms this saying also is what we narrated before in these chapters about Barira from Ibn Abbas that when she was given the choice he saw her husband following her in the streets of Medina while his tears flowed upon his beard, so that indicated that she also was moving about in her own affairs, and that does not cut her off from exercising the choice she has in herself if she exercised it. And of what confirms that also is the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to her after he informed her of the choice being obligatory for her in her husband, and his saying to him: Your husband and the father of your child, so she said to him in answer to that what she said, and chose herself, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deem what had occurred from her before that as cutting her off, from her husband following her while she moved from place to place, with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) aware of that from her, and his carrying out after that her choice of herself. And there has come from those after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that what confirms this meaning."
"5093","As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Ibn Shihab, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr that a freedwoman of Banu Adi called Zabra informed him that she was under a slave while she was a slave girl then, so she was freed. She said: So Hafsa the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to me and called me, so she said: I will inform you of news, and I do not like that you do anything: your affair is in your hand as long as your husband has not touched you. She said: So she separated from him with three."
"5094","And Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: And Musa ibn Uqba informed us, from Nafi, from Abd Allah i.e. Ibn Umar that he used to say: When the slave girl is under a slave, then freeing befalls her, she is given the choice as long as he has not touched her; if she wishes she is his wife, and if she wishes she leaves him; if he approaches until he has intercourse with her she cannot extricate herself from him."
"5095","And as Yahya narrated to us, Nuaym narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata who said: If the slave has intercourse with her before she knows that she has the choice, she has the choice over him; and if he has intercourse with her hastily, he said: Evil is what he did. Abu Jafar said: And in his saying: if he has intercourse with her before she knows that she has the choice, is what indicated that if he had intercourse with her while she knows, she would have no choice. And Allah knows best."
"5096","679 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying about what was given as charity to Barira so she gifted it to Aisha: It is upon her charity, and for us a gift. 4387 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: And Malik informed me, from Rabia, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aisha the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: In Barira were three sunnas; one of the three sunnas was that she was freed, so she was given the choice in her husband; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wala belongs to the one who frees; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered while the pot boiled with meat, so bread and condiment of the house were brought near to him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did I not see a pot in it meat? They said: Yes O Messenger of Allah, but that is meat given as charity to Barira, and you do not eat charity, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is upon her charity, and it is for us from her a gift."
"5097","4388 - And Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Jawaribi narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Muadh al-Anbari narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Qatada, he heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: Barira gifted to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meat given as charity to her, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is for us a gift, and upon her charity. Abu Jafar said: And in this chapter are hadiths besides these, we have brought some of them in what preceded from us in these chapters, and of what we shall bring in the rest of these chapters. And this in our view - and Allah knows best - is because that charity left the ownership of the one who gave it as charity to Barira to the ownership of Barira of it, and left after that from her ownership of it to the ownership of the one she gifted it to of those for whom charity is forbidden either by his lineage, or by other than that of his wealth, and it was lawful for him; since he only owned it by gift not by charity. And a people argued by this for the permissibility of the Hashimi working upon charity and taking wage from it, even though charity is forbidden for him; because he takes what he takes by his work upon it not by the charity of its people with it upon him. And of those who said that: Abu Yusuf. And others disliked that; because charity only leaves the ownership of its owner to its deservers, and among them are the workers upon it, so when it is not lawful for them it is not lawful for them to take it as wage upon their work upon it, because they take what is forbidden for them. So a speaker said: We have seen it permissible for the wealthy to work upon it, and to take his wage from it, while it was not forbidden for him by its leaving the ownership of the giver to his ownership. He said: So the like of that is the one of lineage for whom charity is forbidden by his lineage in his working upon charity, and in his taking what he takes of it by his wage upon it, likewise it is not forbidden for him even though it only leaves the ownership of the giver to his ownership. Our answer to him in that is that if we were left with analogy it would be what was mentioned, but there was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that what indicated the opposite of this meaning."
"5098","4389 - As Abu Umayya narrated to us, Qabisa ibn Uqba narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi Aisha, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Razin, from his father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I said to al-Abbas: Ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to employ you over the charities, so he asked him, so he said: I would not employ you over the washing of the sins of people. So we understood thereby that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only disliked for al-Abbas his employing him over charity to raise him above being a worker over the washing of the sins of people, not for other than that of its lawfulness for him if he worked upon it. And the like of that is what has been narrated from him about Abu Rafi for the wala he has among Banu Hashim."
"5099","4390 - As Bakkar ibn Qutayba and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Rafi the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a man from Banu Makhzum over charity, so he said to Abu Rafi: Accompany me so that you may obtain of it, so he said: Until I come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and ask him. So he came to him and asked him, so he said: The family of Muhammad, charity is not lawful for them, and the freed slave of a people is of themselves."
"5100","4391 - And as al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Warqa ibn Umar narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib who said: I entered upon Umm Kulthum daughter of Ali, so she said: A freed slave of ours called Hurmuz or Kaysan informed me that he passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: So he called me and I came, so he said: O so-and-so, we are a household who do not eat charity, and the freed slave of a people is of themselves, so do not eat charity."
"5101","4392 - And as Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nuaym narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Umm Kulthum, from a freed slave of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Maymun or Mihran that he said: O Maymun - or Mihran - we are a household forbidden from charity, and our freed slaves are of ourselves, so do not eat charity. And we understood in the hadith of Abu Rafi which we narrated in this chapter that he did not intend by it in following the Makhzumi governor over charity to obtain of it except what would be wage for him, not other than that of it, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him in that what he said to him in it. And that in our view - and Allah knows best - is as he said the like of it to al-Abbas when he asked him to employ him over charity which is the washing of the sins of people, not that if they worked upon it there would not be forbidden for them what they take of it by their wage upon it, as that is not forbidden for the wealthy when he works upon it despite the wealth by which the like of it is forbidden for him. So this is the aspect of these reports, and Allah knows best the intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in them, and Allah we ask for success."
"5102","4393 - And it is what al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Hisham ibn Urwa, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Wahb from Malik, from Hisham ibn Urwa which we mentioned in the chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating the intent of Allah the Mighty and Majestic by His saying in the verse of the mukatabun: Give them of the wealth of Allah which He gave you, in what preceded from us in this book, except that he said: Take her and stipulate, for wala belongs to the one who frees. So that was contrary to what is in the hadith of Ibn Wahb from Malik, from Hisham: Take her and stipulate wala for them. Because the meaning of wa ashrti may be intended as: make manifest, for ishrat in the speech of the Arabs is manifestation, and he cited the verse of Aws ibn Hujr meaning he manifested himself. So the like of that may be that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by his saying wa ashrti i.e. stipulate for them the wala which your freeing obliges, that it be upon what the law obliges in it for the one from whom that freeing comes, not another. And some people held the meaning of his saying wa ishtarti lahum al-wala in the hadith of Ibn Wahb from Malik, from Hisham is: stipulate wala against them. Of those: Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham the grammarian, as Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to me, he said: I asked Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham about the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Aisha about Barira: stipulate wala for them? He said: its meaning is stipulate wala against them. He said: So I said to him: is there proof for that? He said: Yes, the saying of Allah: If you do good you do good for yourselves, and if you do evil then for it, meaning upon it. So I mentioned that to Ahmad ibn Abi Imran, so he said to me: Muhammad ibn Shuja used to carry that upon another meaning, which is threat whose outward is command and inward is prohibition, and of it is the saying of Allah: Incite whom you can of them with your voice and rally against them your cavalry and infantry, and His saying: Do what you will. That is not upon permitting that for them, but upon threatening them if they do that with what the likes of them are threatened with for opposing His command. And he said: Do you not see he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) followed that by ascending the pulpit and addressing the people by his saying: What is with men who stipulate conditions not in the Book of Allah - and the Book of Allah is His rulings - every condition not in the Book of Allah is void, even if a hundred conditions, then he followed that by his saying: wala belongs to the one who frees. Abu Jafar said: And when Malik narrated this hadith from Hisham ibn Urwa as we mentioned from him, while Amr and al-Layth differed from him in it from Hisham, two are more deserving of retention than one. And the hadith of Aisha has been narrated from other than this aspect; of those who narrated it contrary to that is Abd Allah ibn Umar: some make it from Ibn Umar from Aisha, and some make it from Ibn Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the story of Aisha."
"5103","4394 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him from Nafi, from Ibn Umar that Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) wanted to buy Barira then free her, so her people said: We sell her to you upon wala being for us. So she mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: That does not prevent you, for wala belongs to the one who frees."
"5104","4395 - And as al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, from Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Aisha, then he mentioned this hadith. So Ibn Wahb and al-Shafii differed upon Malik in the isnad of this hadith according to what we mentioned of their differing from him in it. So we looked: do we find it from the narration of other than Malik from Nafi, so it strengthens in our hearts that it is as narrated by the one agreeing with that of Ibn Wahb and of al-Shafii from Malik?"
"5105","4396 - So we found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar that Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) bargained for Barira; when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned she said: They refused to sell me except to stipulate wala, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wala belongs to the one who frees. So it strengthened in our hearts that the hadith of Malik is as Ibn Wahb narrated it from him, not as al-Shafii narrated it. And the hadith of Aisha was narrated from her by al-Aswad ibn Yazid: four narrated it from Ibrahim from him, and they differed upon him in it. Of them: al-Hakam ibn Utayba."
"5106","4397 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aisha that she wanted to buy Barira then free her, while her masters stipulated her wala, so she mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Buy, then free her, for wala belongs to the one who frees. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her the choice over her husband, and meat was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so it was said to him: This is meat given as charity to Barira, so he said: It is for her charity, and for us a gift. And of them: Mansur ibn al-Mutamir."
"5107","4398 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Awana narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aisha that she bought Barira to free her, so her people stipulated her wala. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon her, so she said: I bought - or I wanted to buy - Barira to free her, and her people stipulated her wala, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Free her, for wala belongs to the one who paid the silver, or he said: to the one who conferred the favor. So she bought her and freed her, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her the choice. So she said: If I were given such and such I would not be with him - meaning her husband. He said: And al-Aswad said: Her husband was free."
"5108","4399 - And as Abu Umayya narrated to us, Qabisa narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: I bought a slave girl called Barira, while her masters stipulated wala be for them, so I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Buy her, for wala belongs to the one who conferred the favor, who paid the price. And we mentioned before in these chapters this hadith also from the hadith of Abu al-Muhayyah from Mansur. And of them: al-Amash."
"5109","4400 - As Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Muawiya narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: And her people - meaning Barira - wanted to sell her and stipulate wala for them. Aisha said: So I mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Buy her and free her, for wala belongs to the one who frees. And of them: Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman."
"5110","4401 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aisha that she bought Barira and freed her, while stipulating for her people that wala be for them. So she mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wala belongs to the one who frees. And he said to her: O Barira, choose, the affair is to you; if you wish stay with your husband, and if you wish leave him. So she said: The affair is to Allah. He said to her: Fear Allah, for he is the father of your child. So she chose herself, and charity was given to her, so she gifted it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so it was said to him: It is charity given as charity to her. He said: It is for her charity, and for us a gift. Ibrahim said: And her husband was free. So the hadith of al-Aswad was divergent: in the hadith of al-Hakam that she wanted to buy Barira then free her while her masters stipulated her wala, and the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Aisha after that: Buy her and free her, for wala belongs to the one who frees. And in the hadith of Mansur that she bought Barira to free her, so her people stipulated her wala, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon her and what occurred occurred after that. And in the hadith of al-Amash that the people of Barira wanted to sell her and stipulate wala, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Aisha after that: Buy her and free her, for wala belongs to the one who frees. And in the hadith of Hammad that she bought Barira and freed her and stipulated wala for her people, and that what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying wala belongs to the one who frees was after all that. And this is severe divergence, except there is nothing in it of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting the people of Barira their stipulating wala, nor his permitting that to Aisha for them. And of those who narrated it from her also: al-Qasim ibn Muhammad."
"5111","4402 - As Abu Umayya narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, Zaida narrated to us, from Simak, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha that she bought Barira from people of the Ansar, while they stipulated wala. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wala belongs to the one who conferred the favor. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her the choice, while her husband was a slave. And she gifted meat to Aisha, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you would prepare for us of this meat something. So Aisha said: It was given as charity to Barira. He said: It is upon her charity, and for us a gift. So in this hadith is the precedence of Aisha buying Barira and her people stipulating her wala, and the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Wala belongs to the one who conferred the favor. And of them: Amra bint Abd al-Rahman, so she narrated it from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her)."
"5112","4403 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him from Yahya ibn Said, from Amra bint Abd al-Rahman that Barira came seeking help from Aisha the Mother of the Believers. So Aisha said to her: If your people like that I pour for them your price in one payment and free you, I will do. So Barira mentioned that to her people, so they said: Except that your wala be for us. Malik said: Yahya said: So Amra claimed that Aisha mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Buy her and free her, for wala belongs to the one who frees. So in this hadith also is the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Aisha to buy Barira, not stipulating in her buying her in her wala. And of them also: Ayman Abu Abd al-Wahid ibn Ayman."
"5113","4404 - As Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ayman narrated to us, my father narrated to me, he said: I entered upon Aisha, so she said: Barira entered upon me while she was a mukataba, so she said: Buy me and free me. So I said: Yes. So she said: My people will not sell me until they stipulate my wala. So I said to her: We have no need of that. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard of that, or it reached him, so he mentioned that to Aisha, so Aisha mentioned what she said to her. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Buy her and free her, and let them stipulate what they wish. So Aisha bought her and freed her, while her people stipulated wala. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wala belongs to the one who frees, even if they stipulate a hundred conditions. So what is in this hadith of Ayman was contrary to what he related in it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Let them stipulate what they wish, upon threat. And it is contrary to what is in the hadiths of other than him of the narrators of this hadith whom we mentioned in this chapter. And what the group narrated in that opposing what Ayman is in it is more deserving of Aisha than what Ayman narrated from her in it. And we found this hadith also from the hadith of Ismail ibn Jafar from Rabia, from al-Qasim with what its meaning is the meaning of threat also."
"5114","4405 - As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Ismail ibn Jafar informed us, from Rabia that he heard al-Qasim say: In Barira were three sunnas: Aisha wanted to buy her and free her, so her people said: And wala is for us. So she mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: If you wish you may stipulate it for them, for wala belongs to the one who frees. Then he stood after noon or before it, so he said: What is with men who stipulate conditions not in the Book of Allah? Whoever stipulates a condition not in the Book of Allah the Exalted it is void; wala belongs to the one who frees. And Barira was freed, so she was given the choice to remain under her husband or leave him. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered one day the house of Aisha while upon the fire was a pot boiling, so he called for lunch and bread and condiment of the house were brought, so he said: Did I not see meat in the house? They said: Yes, but it is meat given as charity to Barira, so she gifted it to us. He said: It is charity upon her, and for us a gift. And the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Aisha in this hadith: Stipulate it for them - meaning the wala they asked - is upon threat, not upon permitting her to stipulate it for them. And in the sum of what we mentioned besides the hadith of Malik from Hisham ibn Urwa there is no permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Aisha in her buying Barira to stipulate her wala after her freeing her for her people. So it became clear, praise be to Allah, the negation of what we negated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what was narrated from him of his permitting Aisha to stipulate the wala of Barira in her freeing her for her people, with what the hadith of Malik from Hisham bore of the two interpretations we mentioned in it. And of what also indicates that the matter in that is upon what we mentioned of its negation from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is that Ibn Umar stood upon what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Aisha, and upon what the affair of Barira proceeded upon in that, then he said after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5115","What Fahd narrated to us, Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, Nafi narrated to me, from Ibn Umar who said: No private part is lawful except a private part which if its owner wishes he sells it, and if he wishes he gifts it, with no condition in it."
"5116","And what Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Yunus ibn Ubayd informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar that he used to dislike that a man buy a slave girl upon not selling nor gifting. So in these two hadiths of Ibn Umar is dislike of buying upon the condition stipulated in it, and in that is what indicated that the affair of Barira did not proceed contrary to that, and that the contract of sale in her between Aisha and her people, of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her in it what he said, was not by imposing a condition for her people upon her in her buying her from them of wala, nor of other than it. Abu Jafar said: And we mentioned in this chapter in the hadith of Hisham ibn Urwa from his father, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Stipulate wala for them - meaning her people. And we mentioned that we did not find this meaning in this hadith of Hisham except in the hadith of Malik narrating it thereby from him. Then we found after that Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid agreed with Malik upon that, so he mentioned this meaning in this hadith of Hisham ibn Urwa, as Malik mentioned it in his hadith from him."
"5117","4406 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us, from Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha who said: I contracted Barira upon herself for nine uqiyas, in every year an uqiya. Then he mentioned the hadith with the meaning of what Malik mentioned in his hadith from Hisham, and he said in it: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Buy her, and stipulate wala for them, for wala belongs to the one who frees. Then he mentioned after that the rest of what is in the hadith of Malik from Hisham. Abu Jafar said: And the discussion after that in the narration of Jarir from Hisham of it likewise is as the discussion we mentioned in the narration of Malik of it from Hisham in what preceded from us in this chapter. And we found it also in the narration of Yazid ibn Ruman from Urwa likewise, except he did not mention it from Aisha, but he mentioned it from Barira."
"5118","4407 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Amr ibn Ali informed us, from al-Thaqafi, Ubayd Allah ibn Umar narrated to us sixty years ago, from Yazid ibn Ruman, from Urwa, from Barira that she said: In me were three of the sunna: charity of meat was given to me so I gifted it to Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and said: What is this meat? So she said: Meat given as charity to Barira, so she gifted it to us. So he said: It is upon Barira charity, and it is for us a gift. And I contracted upon nine uqiyas. So Aisha said: If your masters wish I count your price in one count. So she said: They say: except that you stipulate wala for them. So she mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Buy her, and stipulate for them, for wala belongs to the one who frees. She said: And she freed me, so I had the choice. Abu Jafar said: So the discussion in this is as the discussion in what we mentioned in the hadith of Hisham in that meaning in this chapter. And success is from Allah."
"5119","As Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan ibn Mubashshir al-Basri narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Salama who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Amr ibn al-Harith narrating from Zaynab the wife of Abd Allah ibn Masud that she sold Abd Allah a slave girl while stipulating her service. So that was mentioned to Umar, so he said: Do not buy her while anyone has a remainder in her. Abu Jafar said: And what is in this hadith of Umar and Abd Allah was his agreeing with Abd Allah ibn Umar upon what we mentioned from him in this meaning. And in it also is the acceptance of Zaynab the wife of Abd Allah of that from them both, while she is of the emigrant women, and her leaving opposing them in it. And in what we mentioned is proof for repelling what was interpreted of the meaning we mentioned of the hadith of Barira upon what he interpreted it upon of what opposed him in it, and of what we did not find stated in any of her hadiths. And success is from Allah."
"5120","682 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what was from him in the camel he bought from Jabir ibn Abd Allah in his permitting him to ride it to Medina: was that by a condition upon which the sale occurred between him and him? Or otherwise? 4408 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaida informed us, from al-Shabi, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah that he was traveling upon a camel of his, so it became exhausted. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caught up with him and said: What is with you O Jabir? So he said: My water-carrier is exhausted O Messenger of Allah. He said: Have you something? So he gave him a stick or rod, so he prodded it with it - or he said: struck it with it - so it walked a walking it had not walked the like of. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Will you sell it to me for an uqiya? So I said: O Messenger of Allah, it is your water-carrier. He said: So I sold it for an uqiya, while excepting its carrying me until I arrive at Medina. So when I arrived I brought him the camel, so I said: This is your camel O Messenger of Allah. He said: Perhaps you see that I only bargained with you to take your camel! O Bilal, give him from the spoils an uqiya. And he said: Go with your camel, it is yours."
"5121","4409 - And Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi and Fahd ibn Sulayman both narrated to us, they said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaida informed us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, and with its text."
"5122","4410 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Sayyar, Yahya ibn Abbad al-Ansari narrated to us, from Jabir who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a journey, so I hurried upon a camel of mine. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caught up with me, so he prodded my camel, then he bargained with me. So I sold it to him for seven uqiyas, or nine uqiyas, while its back is mine until I arrive. So when I arrived, I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the camel and handed it to him, so he paid me. So when I left, his messenger called me from behind me, so I said in myself: He wants me to cancel. So when I entered upon him he said: Did you think I rescind with you? Then he said: The camel is yours, go with it. So when I left from him, a man from the Jews met me, so I told him, so he said: He weighed for you seven uqiyas and returned to you the camel! So he marveled."
"5123","4411 - And Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me while I was upon a lean camel. So he took its bridle, while in his hand was a stick, so he prodded it and supplicated - or he said: supplicated and prodded it - and said: Ride it. So I rode it, and I used to restrain it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to hear his hadith. So he came to me and said: Will you sell me your camel O Jabir? I said: Yes O Messenger of Allah, while its back is mine. So he said: And its back is yours. So he bought it from him for five uqiyas. So when I arrived at Medina, I came to him, so he gave me the uqiyas, and increased me."
"5124","4412 - And Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Makki al-Khallal narrated to us, Ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, Hisham narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Ata and others informed me, some adding upon others, no one man of them conveyed it all to him, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a journey, while I was upon a slow camel - meaning it is at the rear of the people. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me and said: Who is this? He said: Jabir. So he said: What is with you? I said: I am upon a slow camel. He said: Have you a rod? I said: Yes O Messenger of Allah. He said: Give it to me. So I gave it to him, and he struck it and prodded it and urged it, and it was from that place at the front of the people. He said: Will you sell it to me? I said: It is yours O Messenger of Allah. He said: Rather sell it to me; I have taken it for four dinars, while its back is yours until I come to Medina."
"5125","4413 - And Fahd narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from al-Mughira, from Amir, from Jabir. And Ibn Abi Layla, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought from me a camel - Abu Jafar said: dropped from my book 'from me' - upon that its back is mine for its journey or my journey that, then he carried me upon it. So a speaker said: In this hadith is the stipulating of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for Jabir to ride that camel he bought from him to his family, and he contracted the sale between him and him upon that. So he permitted thereby, and branched sale upon the like of this condition, and argued in it with these reports. So we examined this hadith to stand upon its requiring that as he said or not."
"5126","4414 - So we found Fahd narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abu Nadra, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a journey, so my water-carrier lagged. So I kept riding it, it hardly moved. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caught up with me from behind me and said: Who is this lagging from the people? So I said: Jabir. He said: What kept you? I said: This water-carrier of mine, I ride it, it hardly moves. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you something? I said: Yes. So I handed him a stick that was with me, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prodded it, then said: Ride and mention Allah. So I rode it, and by the One who sent him with truth I saw myself restraining it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he said: O Jabir, will you sell me this water-carrier of yours when we arrive at Medina for a dinar, and Allah forgives you? I said: O Messenger of Allah, when we arrive at Medina it is your water-carrier. He said: Then sell it to me for two dinars and Allah forgives you. So he kept increasing me while saying with every dinar: Allah forgives you, until it reached twenty dinars. So when we arrived at Medina, I came with the water-carrier leading it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I said: This is your water-carrier O Messenger of Allah. So he said: O Bilal, give him twenty dinars."
"5127","4415 - And we found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah who said: We came from Mecca to Medina with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So my camel became exhausted, so I lagged upon it driving it, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had lagged for a need of his. So he caught up with me and said: Why are you lagging? I said: Nothing O Messenger of Allah, except my camel limped upon me, so I wanted to join it with the people. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took its tail and struck it, then urged it, and said: Ride. He said: I saw myself racing with it. He said: Then he said to me: Sell me your camel. I said: No, rather it is yours. He said: No, rather sell it to me. I said: No, rather it is yours. He said: No, rather sell it to me. I said: A man has upon me an uqiya of gold, so it is yours for it. He said: I have taken it. He said: So convey yourself upon it to Medina. So when I arrived at Medina, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Bilal: Give him an uqiya of gold, and increase him. So he gave me an uqiya of gold, and gave me two qirats. I said: The increase of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shall never leave me. He said: So it was in a purse of mine, then the people of Sham took it on the day of Harra. Abu Jafar said: So in these two hadiths is other than what is in the first hadiths. And in the first of them is from the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Jabir: Will you sell me this water-carrier of yours when we arrive at Medina? And in the second is his buying it from him without a condition between them in that buying. And the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after that to Jabir: Convey yourself upon it to Medina, is favor from him upon him. And these two meanings are contrary to the first meanings in the hadiths we mentioned in this chapter. And the narrators of these two hadiths are not below the narrators of the first hadiths in standing in knowledge, nor in precision, nor in ranks with its people. So when the narrations are equal in that they are lifted, and none of them is more deserving to be carried upon what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in them by what was narrated from him in others. So it came out, praise be to Allah, that there is not in this hadith what requires permissibility of sale with this condition. And it agreed with what we narrated from Umar, Abd Allah ibn Masud, Abd Allah ibn Umar, and Zaynab wife of Abd Allah ibn Masud in forbidding sale with a condition in it of what is not of it. And that agreed also with what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his forbidding sale and loan."
"5128","4416 - As Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, al-Khasib narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama informed us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale and loan, and two conditions in a sale."
"5129","4417 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Loan and sale is not lawful, nor two conditions in a sale."
"5130","4418 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"5131","4419 - And as Abu Umayya narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Sadusi narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"5132","4420 - And as al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5133","4421 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya informed us, from Amir al-Ahwal, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5134","4422 - And as Yunus narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Nafi al-Madini informed me, from Dawud ibn Qays, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sale and loan. Abu Jafar said: So that indicated that these things which are not of sales, when they are in them, corrupt them. And Allah is the granter of success."
"5135","683 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his answering the Bedouins when they asked him: What is the best of what a servant is given? By his saying to them: Good character. 4423 - Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa, from Usama ibn Sharik who said: I witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while the Bedouins asked him: What is the best of what a servant is given? He said: Good character."
"5136","4424 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa, so he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except he said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, and he did not mention the Bedouins asking him. So an objector denying this hadith said: We find the servant given faith; is it permissible that good character be better than it?! Our answer to him is that good character may fall upon different things: of them softness of disposition, and of them the nature which some people praise of others, and of them religion, and of them His saying to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And indeed you are upon great character, i.e. upon great religion."
"5137","As Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Warqa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Bakr, from Mujahid: And indeed you are upon great character, i.e. upon religion. And the people of Arabic incline to this interpretation, of them al-Farra. So the meaning of character which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the best of what a servant is given is good religion. And there has been narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what enters in this chapter."
"5138","4425 - What Muhammad Abu Umayya narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Asim, from Awsaja, from Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, from Abd Allah ibn Masud who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah, You made good my creation, so make good my character. And its meaning in our view - and Allah knows best - is: so make good my religion. And there was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what enters in it."
"5139","4426 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us. And what al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah al-Balisi narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, then they joined, so both said: From Khalaf ibn Hawshab, from Maymun ibn Mihran who said: I said to Umm al-Darda: Do you remember from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: Yes, I heard him say: The heaviest of what is placed in the Scale is good character. So that in our view - and Allah knows best - is upon good religion. And there was narrated from him also of what enters in it."
"5140","4427 - What Yunus narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Ibn al-Had, from Amr ibn Abi Amr freed slave of al-Muttalib, from al-Muttalib ibn Abd Allah, from Aisha who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The believer reaches by good character the ranks of the night-prayer and the day-faster. So that in our view - and Allah knows best - is that he reaches by good religion, even if there is not with him in it night prayer nor day fasting, what the night-prayer and day-faster reaches by night prayer and day fasting. And there was narrated from him also of what enters in this meaning."
"5141","4428 - What Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazza narrated to us, he said: I heard Ata al-Kaykharani narrating from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Nothing is heavier in the Scale than good character. So that in our view - and Allah knows best - is upon good religion. And there was narrated from him of what enters in this meaning also."
"5142","4429 - What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Yusuf al-Saffar narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from his father and his uncle, from their father, from Abu Hurayra who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: By what thing do most people enter Paradise? He said: By good character, and by consciousness of Allah. He said: And he was asked: By what thing do most people enter Fire? He said: By the two hollows: the private part and the mouth. So that also in our view - and Allah knows best - is upon good religions, which Allah called His creation to, which is Islam. And Allah we ask for success."
"5143","684 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The most complete of believers in faith are the best of them in character. 4430 - Ibn Yunus informed us, Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most complete of believers in faith are the best of them in character."
"5144","4431 - And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most complete of believers in faith are the best of them in character, and the best of you are the best of you to their women. Abu Jafar said: And the character in this hadith in our view - and Allah knows best - is the disposition that is with some believers and not with others, so it is a virtue for the one it is with over the one it is not with of them. And Allah is the granter of success."
"5145","685 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: I was only sent to perfect righteous characters. 4432 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz al-Darawardi narrated to us, Ibn al-Ajlan informed me, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was only sent to perfect righteous characters. So its meaning in our view - and Allah knows best - is that Allah only sent him to complete for people their religion, and He revealed upon him of what enters in this meaning, which is His saying: Today I completed for you your religion. So His sending him was to complete for people their religions which He had commanded those before him of His prophets with what He commanded them of them, then He completed it for him by His saying: Today I completed for you your religion. And completion is perfection; so it is the meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I was sent to perfect righteous characters, i.e. righteous religions, which is Islam. And success is from Allah."
"5146","686 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated of the character of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4433 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us, from Shuba, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Abd Allah al-Jadali say: I asked the Mother of the Believers about the character of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so she said: He was not obscene nor foul-mouthed nor loud in markets, but he used to pardon and forgive. Abu Jafar said: And these are the best qualities of characters which are the disposition one of praised disposition is upon."
"5147","4434 - And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman son of Shurahbil's daughter narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Khushani narrated to me, Zayd ibn Waqid narrated to us, from Busr ibn Ubayd Allah al-Hadrami, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I asked Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) about the character of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so she said: His character was the Quran; he is pleased by its pleasure, and angered by its anger. And this also is the best of what people are upon, because nothing is better than the manners of the Quran and of what Allah called people to in it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon that, not exiting from it to other than it."
"5148","4435 - And al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Mubarak ibn Fadala narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Sad ibn Hisham who said: I came to Aisha and said: O Mother of the Believers, inform me of the character of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)! So she said: His character was the Quran. Do you not recite the saying of Allah: And indeed you are upon great character? I said: I want to be celibate. She said: Do not do; do you not recite: There was for you in the Messenger of Allah a good example? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married and had children. And the saying of Aisha: His character was the Quran, i.e. following what the Quran commands him of, and leaving what it forbids him of. And in that is what strengthened what preceded from us in what we interpreted upon it the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the Bedouins when they asked him: What is the best of what a servant is given? By his saying: Good character. And Allah we ask for success."
"5149","687 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his leaving the punishment of Hatib ibn Abi Baltaa for what was from him in his letter to the people of Mecca of the disbelievers of Quraysh informing them of some affair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4436 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated to us, Abu Zumayl narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Abbas narrated to me, Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, he said: Hatib ibn Abi Baltaa wrote to the people of Mecca, so Allah informed His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he sent Ali and al-Zubayr after the letter, so they caught a woman and took it out from a braid of her braids. So they brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and it was read upon him. So he sent to Hatib and said: O Hatib, did you write this letter? He said: Yes O Messenger of Allah. He said: What carried you upon that? He said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I am indeed sincere to Allah and His Messenger, but I was a stranger among the people of Mecca, while my family were among them, so I feared for them. So I wrote a letter that harms Allah and His Messenger nothing, hoping there be in it benefit for my family. Umar said: So I drew my sword, then said: O Messenger of Allah, enable me over Hatib, for he has disbelieved, to strike his neck. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O son of al-Khattab, what makes you know? Perhaps Allah looked upon this band of the people of Badr and said: Do what you wish, for I have forgiven you."
"5150","4437 - And Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar who said: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali informed me that he heard Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi say: I heard Ali say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me, I and al-Zubayr and al-Miqdad, so he said: Go until you come to Rawdat Khakh, for a traveling woman is there with a letter, so take it from her. So we went, our horses racing with us, until we came to the Rawda, when we were with the traveling woman. So we said: Bring out the letter. So she said: I have no letter. So we said: You shall bring out the letter or we shall overturn the clothes. So she took it out from her braids. So we brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when in it is: From Hatib ibn Abi Baltaa to the people of Mecca, informing them of some affair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said: O Hatib, what is this? So he said: O Messenger of Allah, do not hasten against me, for I was an attached man - meaning I was an ally - and I was not of themselves. And those with you of the Emigrants have kinships protecting their families, so I loved, since that escaped me, to make for myself with them a hand by which they protect my kin. And I did not do it as apostasy from my religion nor pleasure with disbelief after Islam. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He has told you truth. So Umar said: Leave me O Messenger of Allah to strike the neck of this hypocrite. So he said: He witnessed Badr. And what makes you know? Perhaps Allah looked upon those who witnessed Badr and said: Do what you wish, for I have forgiven you."
"5151","4438 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Sahl ibn Bakkar informed us, Abu Awana narrated to us, from al-Husayn, from Sad ibn Ubayda, from Abu Abd al-Rahman who said: I heard Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me and al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam and Abu Marthad - all of us horsemen - he said: Go until you reach such and such Rawda, for there is a woman with a page from Hatib ibn Abi Baltaa to the polytheists, so bring me it. So we went upon our horses and caught her where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, traveling upon a camel of hers, while he had written with her to the people of Mecca about the march of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to them. We said: Where is the letter with you? She said: I have no letter. So we made her camel kneel with her, and searched in her saddle, and found nothing. So my two companions said: We see nothing with her. He said: I said: We know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not lie. So he said: By the One I swear by, you shall bring out the letter or I shall strip you. So she reached to her waist while girded with a cloak, and brought out the letter. So we brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, he has betrayed Allah and His Messenger and the believers; leave me to strike his neck. So he said: What carried you upon what you did? So he said: It is not that I am not a believer in Allah and His Messenger, except I wanted there be for me a hand with the people by which Allah repels from my family and wealth. And none of your companions but has of his people one by whom Allah repels from his family and wealth. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He spoke truth; do not say to him but good. So Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, he has betrayed Allah and His Messenger and the believers; leave me to strike his neck. So he said: Is he not of the people of Badr? Perhaps Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said: Do what you wish, for I have forgiven you. So tears flowed from the eyes of Umar, and he said: Allah and His Messenger know best."
"5152","4439 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Sad ibn Ubayda, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Ali, then he mentioned this hadith."
"5153","4440 - And al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, my father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth informed me, then they joined, so both said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir that he informed him that Hatib ibn Abi Baltaa wrote to the people of Mecca a letter mentioning that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended raiding them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was guided to the woman with the letter, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to her, and her letter was taken from her head. So he said: O Hatib, did you do? He said: Yes, but I did not do it as treachery to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nor hypocrisy. I knew that Allah will manifest His Messenger and complete for him his affair, except I was a stranger among them while my mother was with them, so I wanted to take with them a hand. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Shall I not strike the head of this? So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you kill a man of the people of Badr? And what makes you know? Perhaps Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said: Do what you wish. So a speaker said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his leaving punishment upon Hatib for what was from him against him and the believers? If you say: Because he was of the people of Badr, and there preceded for them from Allah what preceded, it is said to you: There preceded for them from Allah what preceded, yet that does not repel from them punishments upon their sins they sin to be carried out upon them. And he mentioned in that."
"5154","4441 - What Fahd narrated to us, Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, Yahya ibn Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to me, from Thawr i.e. Ibn Zayd, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas that drinkers used to be struck in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with hands and sandals and sticks until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away. So they were in the caliphate of Abu Bakr more than them in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Abu Bakr said: If we were to impose for them a hadd. So he aimed at about what they were struck in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) used to flog them forty until he passed away. Then Umar after him used to flog them likewise, until a man of the first Emigrants came having drunk, so he commanded him to be flogged. So he said: Why do you flog me? Between me and you is the Book of Allah. So Umar said: And where in the Book of Allah do you find that I not flog you? So he said: Allah says in His Book: There is no sin upon those who believe and do righteous deeds in what they tasted when they fear and believe and do righteous deeds, then fear and believe, then fear and do good. I witnessed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Badr and Uhud and Khandaq and the scenes. So Umar said: Will you not answer him what he said? So Ibn Abbas said: These verses were revealed as excuse for the past and proof upon the remaining. So the past were excused that they met Allah before wine was forbidden upon them, and proof upon the remaining because Allah says: O you who believe, wine and gambling and idols and divining arrows, the verse, then he recited the whole verse. If he was of those who witnessed Badr yet that did not repel the hadd from him, likewise the precedence of Hatib in Badr does not repel punishment from him for what he did. So Umar consulted the Companions, so Ali said: We see to flog him eighty; when he drinks he intoxicates, and when he intoxicates he raves, and when he raves he slanders. So Umar flogged him eighty for wine. So it became clear that being of the people of Badr does not repel punishments for sins, and that the meaning of his saying: Do what you wish, for I have forgiven you, is: I have forgiven for you what you did of fighting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Badr, not that He permitted for them after that what He forbade others. And success is from Allah."
"5155","4442 - And Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Sadaqa ibn Khalid narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Shaqiq, from Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) like the first hadith in the story of Hatib, except he said in it: O Hatib, do not return to this, for Allah has forgiven you. Abu Jafar said: So in this is what indicates that what Hatib did was forgiven by his saying: Do what you wish, for I have forgiven you, with his saying to him after that: Do not return to this. And in that is what refutes the interpretation of the speaker who made Do what you wish upon permitting what is forbidden. And success is from Allah."
"5156","4443 - And Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us. And Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, Asim ibn Bahdala narrated to me, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ibn Masud ... then they mentioned the like of it, except they did not mention in their two hadiths: So I took from him seventy suras which no human contested with me. Abu Jafar said: So a speaker said: How do you accept from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he asked one who is not owner of those sheep he herded: Have you milk? - i.e. to give them drink of it - while he does not own those sheep? Our answer to him in that is that it may be that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon those sheep being with Ibn Masud by their outward affair and in his hand over them, so he said to him what he said of what is mentioned in this hadith for that. And his saying that to him may have intended buying milk if it were with him, not other than that. As for the saying of Ibn Masud to him: I am entrusted, and his affirming trust for himself over what he herds, that is what made the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stop that he is not owner of them. And one who herds sheep for another by hiring from him upon herding them does not herd with them sheep for another as exclusive hireling. And the exclusive hireling with all people of knowledge is entrusted over what he is hired upon, differing only in the joint hireling: some make him likewise, and some make him otherwise. Then this speaker said: What is the meaning of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking Ibn Masud for a ewe not mounted by a male of sheep he knew were not Ibn Masud's? Our answer to him in that is that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to show him a miraculous sign by which proof stands for him upon him and others in obligation of believing him and faith in him. And what was from him in the ewe in it is benefit for its owner of softening its udder. And the milk Allah created in its udder is not of its teat; it is milk Allah placed in its udder for what He made for him without ownership, occurring upon it for the owner of that ewe. As for the saying of Ibn Masud to him after that: So I learned from him seventy suras which no human contested with me, that in our view - and Allah knows best - is upon that no human shared with him in them; for contest may be upon sharing. And of that is the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he knew people recited behind him in prayer: Why am I contested in the Quran! - i.e. shared in the Quran I recite in my prayer. And we mentioned that with its isnad before in this book. So this speaker said: How do you accept this while you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his command to take the Quran from those he commanded it be taken from of his Companions, preferring in them Ibn Masud over others he commanded it be taken from? And we shall mention that with its isnads after in this book in what is more deserving of it than this place, God willing. Our answer to him in that is that those seventy suras mentioned in this hadith no human shared with him in taking them from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while he shared in taking the rest of the Quran from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with who shared with him in it of those who took it from him of his Companions. So it became clear, praise be to Allah, all of what in this hadith of what was difficult upon this questioner of that, and of other than it of what is mentioned in it explained. And success is from Allah."
"5157","689 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about condiment: what is it? 4444 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, al-Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, from Hushaym, Abu Bishr narrated to me, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Excellent condiment is vinegar."
"5158","4445 - And Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"5159","4446 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, al-Muthanna ibn Said narrated to us, Talha ibn Nafi narrated to us - Abu Jafar said: and he is Abu Sufyan - from Jabir ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took my hand and brought me to one of his houses, and said: Is there lunch? They said: No, except pieces. He said: Bring them. He said: Is there condiment? They said: No, except vinegar. He said: Bring it, for excellent condiment is vinegar. Jabir said: Vinegar pleases me since I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say in it what he says. He said: And Talha said: Vinegar pleases me since I heard Jabir say in it what he says."
"5160","4447 - And Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, al-Muthanna ibn Said informed us, from Talha ibn Nafi Abu Sufyan, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5161","4448 - And Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, Abu Humma Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Abu Qurra narrated to us, from Zamaa ibn Salih, from Ziyad i.e. Ibn Sad, from Zayd ibn Aslam who said: I heard my father say: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take oil as condiment and anoint of it; for it is taken from a blessed tree. Abu Jafar said: We were only prevented from making this hadith the head of this chapter - even though nothing was narrated from any of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning more eminent than this hadith - because we found it divergent in its text: Ziyad ibn Sad narrates it from Zayd as we narrated it, while Mamar narrates it from Zayd contrary to that."
"5162","4449 - As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sari narrated to us. And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, Yahya ibn Musa i.e. Ibn Khatt informed us, both said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Eat oil and anoint with it; for it is of a blessed tree. Abu Jafar said: So what is in this hadith is other than what is in the hadith before it, because in this hadith is 'Eat' while in the hadith before it is 'Take as condiment with it'."
"5163","4450 - So Abu Hanifa and Abu Yusuf used to say: Condiment is the things dipped with of vinegar and oil and what resembles them. And they used to say: Roast is not condiment, and meat likewise is not condiment. Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Yaqub, from Abu Hanifa without disagreement in it between Abu Hanifa and Abu Yusuf. And he said in this narration: And Muhammad said: All these things are condiment, and everything whose predominant is that bread is eaten with it is condiment."
"5164","4451 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Rabia ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered while the pot boiled with meat and condiment of the house. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did I not see a pot in it meat? They said: Yes O Messenger of Allah, but that is meat given as charity to Barira, and you do not eat charity. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is upon her charity, and it is for us a gift. So in this hadith is that that pot mentioned in it boiled with meat and condiment of the house. So what occurs to hearts is that that condiment is of what is seen in the pot as the meat in it is seen, and that is other than oil and what resembles it of what does not remain in its like as meat remains in it."
"5165","4452 - And Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, my father narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: The earth on the Day of Resurrection will be one loaf which the Almighty overturns with His hand, as one of you overturns his loaf in travel, as hospitality for the people of Paradise. So a man from the Jews came and said: May the Merciful bless you O Abu al-Qasim, shall I not inform you of the hospitality of the people of Paradise on the Day of Resurrection? He said: Yes. He said: The earth will be one loaf, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at us, then laughed until his molars appeared, then said: Shall I not inform you of its condiment? He said: Yes. He said: Its condiment is lam and nun. He said: And what is this? He said: Ox and fish; seventy thousand eat of the appendage of their livers. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is that the ox and fish mentioned in it are condiment for the people of Paradise with which they eat what they eat of the loaf mentioned in this hadith."
"5166","4453 - And Fahd narrated to us, Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakhai narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Yahya al-Aslami, from Yazid al-Awar - thus in my book, and the correct in that is from Yazid ibn Abi Umayya al-Awar - and he is nephew of Uthman ibn al-As, from Yusuf ibn Abd Allah ibn Salam who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) take a piece of barley bread, so he placed upon it a date and said: This is condiment of this. So he ate it. So in these two hadiths of Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam and Fahd ibn Sulayman is what indicated that what bread is eaten with, even if not dipped in it, is condiment, as vinegar is condiment, and as oil is condiment. And this saying is more deserving of the two sayings we mentioned in this chapter. And the speech of the Arabs indicates it, for they say: May Allah make affection between them, meaning the spouses, i.e. He made between them love and agreement. Until that ascended to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying to al-Mughira ibn Shuba when he informed him that he proposed to a woman, so he said to him: Have you looked at her? So he said: No. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Look at her; for it is more likely to make affection between you. And when that was likewise among the children of Adam, the like of it is what food is made pleasant with to be eaten, to be thereby condiment for it, as Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said. And success is from Allah."
"5167","690 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about loan, of what those who obligate its guarantee argue with and of other than that of what was narrated from him in it. 4454 - al-Hasan ibn Makhlad ibn Hazim al-Kufi al-Khazzaz narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz i.e. Ibn Rufay, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, from Umayya ibn Safwan ibn Umayya, from his father who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) borrowed of Safwan ibn Umayya iron coats of mail on the day of Hunayn. So he said to him: O Muhammad, guaranteed? So he said: Guaranteed. So some of them were lost, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: If you wish we compensate them for you. He said: No, I am more desirous in Islam than that O Messenger of Allah. He said: So in this hadith is the stipulating of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Safwan in what he had loaned him of those coats guarantee. So we examined this hadith in its isnad how it is."
"5168","4455 - So we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Sallam informed us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abd Allah informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Umayya ibn Safwan ibn Umayya, from his father, then he mentioned the like of it, while he did not mention in its isnad Ibn Abi Mulayka. So Yazid and al-Himmani differed upon Sharik in the isnad of this hadith as we mentioned. Then we sought it from the narration of other than Sharik of it, from Abd al-Aziz."
"5169","4456 - So we found Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, from Israil ibn Yunus, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, from Ibn Safwan ibn Umayya, without exceeding him in its isnad to his father nor to another, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) borrowed of Safwan ibn Umayya coats of mail, so some of them were lost. So he said: If you wish we compensate them for you. He said: No O Messenger of Allah. So it strengthened in our hearts the entry of Ibn Abi Mulayka in the isnad of this hadith, and the judgment in that for al-Himmani over Yazid. Then we found Sharik and Israil differed in who is after Ibn Abi Mulayka in the isnad of this hadith: in the isnad of Sharik it is from Umayya ibn Safwan from his father, while in the hadith of Israil it is from Ibn Safwan - and he is Umayya - without mention of him: from his father. Then we looked in this hadith also whether we find it in other than the two narrations of Sharik and Israil, to stand upon its reality how it is in that."
"5170","4457 - So we found Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from people of the family of Safwan ibn Umayya who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) borrowed of Safwan ibn Umayya weapons. So Safwan said to him: Loan or seizure? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Rather loan. So he loaned him between thirty to forty coats. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided Hunayn. So when the polytheists were defeated the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gather the coats of Safwan. So they missed of his coats coats. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Safwan: If you wish we compensate them for you. So Safwan said: O Messenger of Allah, in my heart is of faith what was not that day."
"5171","4458 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: And Musaddad narrated it to us another time, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Safwan ibn Umayya that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) borrowed of him coats of mail, then he mentioned this hadith. Abu Jafar said: So we found Abu al-Ahwas confused in the isnad of this hadith this confusion: he made it once from people of the family of Safwan, and once from Safwan himself. And both his narrations of it were from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, not from Ibn Abi Mulayka. And this was of what he opposed Sharik and Israil in in the isnad of this hadith. And in neither of his two narrations is mention of a guarantee stipulated upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he had loaned him of those coats. Then we looked whether other than Sharik and Israil and Abu al-Ahwas narrated it from Abd al-Aziz or not."
"5172","4459 - So we found al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from people of the family of Abd Allah ibn Safwan who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to raid Hunayn, so he said to Safwan: What weapons have you to loan us? So he said: Loan or seizure? He said: Rather loan. So he loaned him between thirty to forty coats. So he wanted to raid with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: You are of the nobles of Mecca and their chiefs, and I dislike to raid Mecca, so stay. So he stayed, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided. So when they finished their raid he commanded the coats of Safwan to be gathered, so they were gathered, and they missed of them coats. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Safwan: If you wish we compensate them for you. So Safwan said: No, in my heart is of faith what was not that day. So in this hadith is that what Abd al-Aziz took from whom he took of the family of Abd Allah ibn Safwan, so he opposed all we mentioned before him in this chapter of the narrators of this hadith from Abd al-Aziz. And his narration of it returned interrupted, not connected in isnad. And in neither his narration nor the narration of Abu al-Ahwas of it from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay is mention of guarantee for loan. So we stood thereby upon the confusion of this hadith this severe confusion. And what this is its way, the like of it does not establish proof for anyone against his opponent in it. And success is from Allah. And it was understood that if loan were guaranteed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have been free of mentioning its guarantee to Safwan, and would have said to him: And is loan but guaranteed? So in his leaving that is proof that his originating for him by his saying: It is guaranteed, is a guarantee his saying obliged, not the loan itself. And Safwan was that day recent in pre-Islam, for Hunayn the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided only after the conquest of Mecca. And Safwan had before that known from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stipulations for belligerents of what the law does not oblige of Muslims to one another: of that his stipulating (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hudaybiya that whoever comes to him of the polytheists desiring his religion leaving what the polytheists are upon he returns him to them, and that whoever comes to the polytheists of his companions they do not return him to him, and that whoever comes to him of the women of the polytheists entering his religion he returns to them what he had driven to his wife of dower for the marriage between him and her. And Safwan made him stand upon the like of these things the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to stipulate for the polytheists of what the likes of them are not permissible among Muslims, so that is permissible for the polytheists, and obliges the Muslims for them. He asked the like of that to oblige for him the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - not that of his law is obligation of guarantee in loan. And this is a sound cause Muhammad ibn al-Abbas mentioned to me from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan without his mentioning from whom he took it from him. And that resembles what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon, then what the Arabs were upon in his language and their languages; for what they were upon in that is brevity, not other than it. And if loan were its law obliging its guarantee, he would have been free by mentioning it of mentioning its guarantee. But what was from him after that of what Safwan asked him originated a ruling not before it, which is obligation of its guarantee by the stipulation he stipulated for him in it. And of what indicated that is what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about loan in other than this hadith."
"5173","4460 - As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir informed us, Said ibn Abi Said narrated to me, from one who heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Indeed loan is to be returned, and a grant is to be returned, and debt is to be paid, and a guarantor is liable."
"5174","4461 - And as al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Shurahbil ibn Muslim al-Khawlani, from Abu Umama al-Bahili, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So in these two hadiths is the informing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to people that loan is to be returned. And in that is what requires that it is a trust, as Allah said: Allah commands you to render trusts to their owners. So that uncovered what we mentioned of what we carried the hadith of Safwan upon, although the hadith of Safwan was narrated by Qatada from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, who is not below Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, while he did not exceed him with it with this wording also."
"5175","4462 - As Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Qasim al-Kufi narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Said, from Qatada, from Ata that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) borrowed of Safwan ibn Umayya coats of mail on the day of Hunayn, so he said to him: Is loan to be returned O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. So what Abd al-Aziz narrated upon him of the hadith of Safwan was not more deserving of him than what Qatada narrated upon him, with their equivalence in its interruption in most narrations from Abd al-Aziz. So a speaker said: We narrated from Abd Allah ibn Abbas and from Abu Hurayra what requires compensation of loan when lost in the hand of its borrower to its lender."
"5176","And he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Saib, from Abu Hurayra, and from Amr, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Loan is guaranteed if its owner pursues it."
"5177","And what Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, Nuaym narrated to us, Jarir i.e. Ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he wrote to him about loan: that guarantee it for its owner. Our answer to him in that is that we did not deny that among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is one who holds guarantee of loan. But - even if those mentioned in these two hadiths guaranteed it - of them are those who did not guarantee it and made it a trust: Umar ibn al-Khattab and Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"5178","As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Ammar ibn Umar al-Halabi judge of the people of Mecca narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Hilal ibn Abd al-Rahman i.e. al-Wazzan, from Ibn Ukaym i.e. Abd Allah ibn Ukaym al-Juhani, from Ibn Umar, from Umar that he used not to guarantee loan."
"5179","And as Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Ibrahim al-Muzani narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Israil ibn Yunus, from Abd al-Ala, from Muhammad son of al-Hanafiya, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) that he used not to make loan liable, saying: It is kindness. Abu Jafar said: And when the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed in its ruling this differing, we returned to what reflection requires in what they differed in of that. So we found loan taken from its owner with his goodwill thereby, without compensation compensating him upon what he permitted of it. And we found hired things taken from their owners with compensations obligatory upon their hirers of them to them. And the ownership of used things upon that was not guaranteed. And when with obligation of compensations in their use it was not guaranteed, in their use without obligation of compensations in that it is more deserving not to be guaranteed. And thus the Kufans Abu Hanifa and al-Thawri and their companions and many others besides them held in that. As for the Medinans: they make what is lost of that of what its loss is apparent lost upon trust, and what of that is of what its loss is hidden lost upon guarantee. And there is no difference in analogy in that between what its loss is apparent and what its loss is hidden, as no difference between that in guaranteed usurpations and in trusted deposits. And in their lifting guarantee in what its perishing is apparent is what obliges upon them lifting guarantee in what its perishing is hidden."
"5180","And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, Talha ibn Abi Said narrated to me, Khalid ibn Abi Imran narrated to me, he said: I asked Sulayman ibn Yasar about a man who borrowed a mount of a man, informing him of what he wants with it, so he loaned it to him upon that, then it was afflicted in that loan: is upon him compensation? He said: No, unless he killed it deliberately. Al-Layth said: Upon this we found our elders: that there is no guarantee in loan unless he transgresses what he borrowed it for, so he guarantees. And Ibn Shihab said: Upon this we found people until the rulers accused people so made them liable. And in what we mentioned is that the group of early people of Medina and early people of Egypt are upon leaving making loan liable as long as there is no transgression in it. And we contemplated the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Safwan about what was lost of his coats: we found in it that he said to him: If you wish we compensate them for you. So we understood thereby that compensating them was not in reality obligatory, were it not what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave of the statement he gave him in them. And if it were guaranteed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have left compensating them for him, nor returned that to his wish. He would have affirmed obligation of compensating them for him upon him, as people of knowledge say in debt of some people upon others: it is obligatory for the one it is upon to be demanded by the one it is for upon him, taking it from him until his liability is cleared. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the most deserving of people of that and strongest of them in holding to it. And in the answer of Safwan to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by his saying: In my heart today is of faith what was not that day, is proof that what he had stipulated upon him of guarantee when he loaned him was upon a ruling other than faith, as Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said of what we mentioned of the narration of Muhammad ibn al-Abbas. And in that is what indicated that the ruling of loan among people of faith is otherwise of negation of guarantee from it. And success is from Allah."
"5181","691 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating binding of guarantees of persons. 4463 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn al-Husayn who said: Thaqif captured two men of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) captured a man of Banu Amir ibn Sasa. So he was passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while bound. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him, so he said: For what am I detained? So he said: For the offense of your allies. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on, so he called him, so he came to him. So the captive said to him: I am a Muslim. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you had said it while you owned your affair you would have succeeded entire success. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on, so he called him also, so he came. So he said: I am hungry so feed me. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is your need. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ransomed him with the two men Thaqif had captured."
"5182","4464 - And Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn who said: al-Adba belonged to a man of Uqayl who was captured, so al-Adba was taken from him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon him, so he said: O Muhammad, for what do you take me, and take the forerunner of pilgrims while I have become Muslim? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: If you had said it while you owned your affair you would have succeeded entire success. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I take you for the offense of your allies. And Thaqif had captured two men of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon a donkey with a velvet cloth. So he said: O Muhammad, I am hungry so feed me, and thirsty so give me drink. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is your need. Then the man was ransomed with the two men, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) detained al-Adba for his journey. So we contemplated this hadith and found in it what indicated to us that the people of whom that captive was had no safety nor truce between them and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), by his detaining the mount. And it is not permissible to detain it except because there was no safety nor truce between him and its people. And in this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing upon the Islam of that captive, and his leaving lifting captivity from him by his Islam, because Islam in this does not lift a prior obligation. Do you not see that if the captive were a scripturalist who would be enslaved had he not become Muslim, he is enslaved even if he becomes Muslim? And that Islam only lifts from him killing specifically? So likewise that captive: his Islam which was from him did not lift from him the detention upon him for the offense of his allies - and they are other than him - and that he is not returned to them even if he became Muslim, until they return to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the two men they captured of his Companions. And what was obligatory upon him of that for what is between him and his allies upon what they were of war to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And since he was taken thereby - even if he did not oblige it upon himself, the law obliged it upon him - had he obliged upon himself the like of that of freeing of captivity of Muslims upon him, he obliged. And in ruling it is more binding for him. And if that is likewise, the like of it are guarantees of persons when some people oblige them upon themselves: they are obligatory likewise, as the Kufans and Medinans all held in that, and as al-Shafii held in it - except he weakened them once and did not nullify them. So we brought what we brought of what we mentioned to know their strength, and that their weakness is not obligatory from any aspect. And how is weakened what what we mentioned indicated? And the like of that also is the appointing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of chiefs over the Ansar - and they are the trusted over them who deliver to him what is of them of what they deserve praise upon, and of what they deserve blame upon. And they were taken thereby, so they are as guarantors with it. And Muhammad ibn Ishaq mentioned in his campaigns."
"5183","4465 - What Fahd narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar: I appoint over you chiefs to be over you as the chiefs of the Children of Israel, as guarantors. And in that is what affirmed guarantee of persons, especially with one who argues with campaigns and makes them proof against his opponent. And we found from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what requires its establishment."
"5184","And of that is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed us, my father narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Hamza ibn Amr al-Aslami, from his father that Umar sent him as alms-collector over Sad Hudhaym. So Hamza was brought wealth to collect it. When a man says to his wife: Pay the charity of the wealth of your freedman, while the woman says to him: Rather you, pay the charity of the wealth of your father. So Hamza asked about their affair and saying. So he was informed that that man is the husband of that woman, and that he fell upon a slave girl of hers, so she bore a child, so his wife freed him. They said: So this wealth is for his father of her slave girl. So Hamza said: I shall stone you with your stones. So it was said to him: May Allah set you right, his affair was raised to Umar ibn al-Khattab, so Umar flogged him a hundred and did not see stoning upon him. So Hamza took the man with a guarantor until he came to Umar and asked him about what was mentioned of Umar flogging him without seeing stoning upon him. So Umar confirmed them in that, and said: He only averted stoning from him because he excused him by ignorance."
"5185","And of that is what al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jazari al-Mayafariqini narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Sulayman Abu al-Husayn al-Ruhawi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Haritha ibn Mudarrib who said: I prayed dawn with Abd Allah ibn Masud in the mosque. So when he gave salam a man stood, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: As for after, by Allah I spent this night with no malice in myself against anyone of people. And I had sought a man of Banu Hanifa to shoe my horse, so he ordered me to come to him at dawn. And I came to him, and when I reached the mosque of Banu Hanifa, the mosque of Abd Allah ibn al-Nawwaha, I heard their caller testifying there is no god but Allah and that Musaylima is the Messenger of Allah. So I doubted my hearing, and restrained the horse until I heard the people of the mosque agree upon that. So Abd Allah did not belie him, and said: Who is here? So men stood, so he said: Bring me Abd Allah ibn al-Nawwaha and his companions. Haritha said: So they were brought while I sat. So Abd Allah said to Ibn al-Nawwaha: Woe to you! Where is what you used to recite of the Quran? He said: I used to shield myself with it from you. He said to him: Repent. So he refused. So Abd Allah commanded Qaraza ibn Kab al-Ansari with him, so he took him out to the market and struck his head. Haritha said: So I heard Abd Allah say: Whoever is pleased to look at Abd Allah ibn al-Nawwaha slain in the market, let him go out and look at him. Haritha said: So I was of those who went out to look at him. Then Abd Allah consulted the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the rest of the group. So Adi ibn Hatim al-Tai stood, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: As for after, a wart of disbelief has raised its head, so cut it off, and there will be nothing after it. And al-Ashath ibn Qays and Jarir ibn Abd Allah stood and said: Rather make them repent, and make their clans guarantee them. So he made them repent and they repented, and made their clans guarantee them, and exiled them to Sham. So in these two hadiths is the employment by Abd Allah of guarantee of persons by counsel of those who counseled him with it, and in presence of those who attended it, without anyone denying that upon him nor opposing him in it. So that indicated their following him upon it. And what comes this coming is more deserving of strength, and more deserving of negation of weakness from it. And Allah knows best."
"5186","692 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from Abd Allah ibn Abbas about the cause in which was revealed: If they come to you then judge between them, to His saying: And if you judge then judge between them with justice. 4466 - Fahd narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas who said: When this verse came down: If they come to you then judge between them or turn from them, and if you judge then judge between them with justice, Allah loves the just, he said: When Banu al-Nadir killed of Banu Qurayza a slain they paid half blood-money, and when Banu Qurayza killed of Banu al-Nadir a slain they paid the blood-money to them. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made equal between them in blood-money."
"5187","4467 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Asadi narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas who said: The verses in al-Maida: And if you judge then judge between them with justice, Allah loves the just, were only revealed about blood-money between Banu Qurayza and Banu al-Nadir. And that is that the slain of Banu al-Nadir - who had nobility - were paid full blood-money, while Qurayza were paid half blood-money. So they sought judgment in that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so Allah revealed that about them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carried them upon truth and made blood-money equal. And Allah knows best which in that it was. Abu Jafar said: Meaning his returning whoever took full blood-money of the two parties to half blood-money the other party took, or his returning whoever took half blood-money to all blood-money the other party took. So a speaker said: You narrated from Ibn Abbas from other than this aspect that the descent of this meaning was in contrary to what its descent was mentioned in this hadith."
"5188","4468 - And he mentioned what Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Yusuf al-Qattan narrated to us, Ubayd Allah i.e. Ibn Musa narrated to us, from Ali ibn Salih, from Simak, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas who said: Qurayza and al-Nadir were, and al-Nadir were nobler than Banu Qurayza. And when a man of Banu Qurayza killed a man of Banu al-Nadir he was killed for him, and when a man of Banu al-Nadir killed a man of Banu Qurayza they paid a hundred wasqs of dates. So when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent, a man of Banu Qurayza killed a man of Banu al-Nadir. So they said: Hand him to us to kill him. So they said: Between us and you is the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So they came to him, so was revealed: And if you judge then judge between them with justice, and justice is life for life. Then was revealed: Do they seek the judgment of ignorance."
"5189","4469 - And what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Zakariyya informed us, Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, Ali ibn Salih informed us, then he mentioned this hadith with its isnad and text. He said: So in this hadith is that the descent of this meaning was about retaliation not blood-money, and this is severe divergence. Our answer to him in that is that it may be the people disputed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in both these meanings together: of blood-money of their slain killed the killing not obliging retaliation, and of retaliation for their slain the killing obliging retaliation. So Allah revealed this verse in both causes together. So He made equal between them in blood-money, and made equal between them in equivalence of lives and obligation of retaliation in them. And a speaker said: The blood-money of covenanters is four thousand dirhams, arguing for that with what was narrated."
"5190","Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Sadaqa, from Said ibn al-Musayyib who said: Uthman ruled in the blood-money of the covenanter with four thousand dirhams. Abu Jafar said: And this Sadaqa is Sadaqa ibn Yasar, and it is said his origin is of Khurasan, then he dwelt in Medina and settled it, and people took from him. Of those who took from him: Malik ibn Anas and others. So of the proof against this speaker in this meaning for his opposing in it is what was narrated from Uthman about blood-money of covenanters opposing Malik."
"5191","As Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Ibrahim al-Uthri and Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father that a Muslim killed a disbeliever of the people of covenant, so Uthman ibn Affan ruled upon him with the blood-money of a Muslim. And it indicated that what in this hadith from Uthman is more deserving than what in the first hadith from him, since what in the first hadith is only from Said, from Uthman, while there was narrated from Said of his own saying in this meaning."
"5192","What Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Ismail ibn Hud al-Wasiti narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn al-Husayn, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib who said: The blood-money of every covenanter in his covenant is a thousand dinars. Then others of the Tabiun agreed with Said ibn al-Musayyib upon this saying."
"5193","As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Samina narrated to us, Abu Usama narrated to us, from Abu Umays, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Alqama who said: The blood-money of the Jew and Christian is like the blood-money of the Muslim."
"5194","And as Ahmad narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani narrated to us, Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from al-Shabi who said: The blood-money of the Jew and Christian is equal, thus in my book."
"5195","And as Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ulayya narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid and Ata who said: The blood-money of the Muslim and Christian is equal. And in the hadith of Ibn Abbas with which we began our narration of it in this chapter is what indicated negation of the hadith of Said from Uthman about the blood-money of the covenanter that it is four thousand, because in that hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carried them upon truth and made blood-money equal. So it indicated that He returned blood-money for them all to full blood-money, or returned full blood-money to half blood-money. So in that is negation of four thousand being blood-money for the covenanter. Then we returned to uncovering the meaning in this divergence, and found Allah said in His Book: Whoever kills a believer by mistake, then freeing of a believing neck and blood-money delivered to his family unless they remit, then He followed that by His saying: And if he is of a people between you and them a covenant, then blood-money delivered to his family and freeing of a believing neck. So Allah in what we recited of killing the believer by mistake made the blood-money mentioned in this verse, and the freeing of the neck mentioned in it. Then He made in one between us and them a covenant blood-money and expiation also. So He made equal between them in the obligatory expiation in them. So it was understood thereby that they are equal together in blood-money, since the address with the obligatory in the Muslim killed by mistake, and in the covenanted killed by mistake is equal. And we did not find from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter anything better than a hadith narrated from Amr ibn Shuayb in it, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr."
"5196","4470 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The blood-money of the People of the Book is upon half of the blood-money of Muslims, and they are the Jews and Christians. If this hadith is established, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the explainer from Allah the Exalted of the blood-money He mentioned in the covenanted what it is. And if otherwise, the outward of the Quran indicates equality of Muslims and covenanted in blood-money. And of those saying equality in that: Abu Hanifa and his companions. And of those saying the obligatory in them is half blood-money: Malik and his companions. And of those saying in their blood-money that it is four thousand: al-Shafii."
"5197","Except there was narrated from al-Zuhri in that what Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, al-Uwaysi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab who said: Abu Bakr and Umar and Uthman used to make the blood-money of Jews and Christians when covenanted like the blood-money of the Muslim. So in this also is what confirmed what those who made equal between blood-money in Muslims and covenanted held. And success is from Allah."
"5198","693 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about gift and promise and dower before the bond of marriage, and in that after its bond. 4471 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Amr ibn Shuayb narrated to me, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any woman married upon dower or gift or promise before the bond of marriage, it is hers; and what is after the bond of marriage is for the one it is bonded for. And the most deserving of what a man is honored upon is his daughter and his sister. Abu Jafar said: As for the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he said: Before the bond of marriage, then the bond of marriage here is the knot. And of it is the saying of Allah: And do not hold to the bonds of disbelieving women, i.e. do not keep them as wives for you, and release them. As for his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And what is after the bond of marriage, its meaning is: what is after its knot, it is for the one it is bonded for, i.e. for the one it is made for. For it is said: I bonded so-and-so, when you make for him something he bonds with, i.e. resorts to it, and is sufficed with it from seeking its like. Then we contemplated this hadith, and the best of what came to us in it is that the woman proposed to her guardian: her guardian may be gifted, or promised something to be aid to the suitor upon what he attempts of the marriage he seeks. So what was gifted nor what was promised thereby is not pleasant for her guardian therein, since it was only intended thereby the marriage sought from him. So the woman whose marriage is sought is more deserving of that than him, because what is owned by that proposal is her private part, not other than it. And the compensation of that private part, and the causes sought thereby to reach it, are in its ruling by the ownership of the one who owns that private part, and she is the woman, not other than her. And the like of that is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he said to Ibn al-Lutbiya when he returned from governorship over charity and reckoned him, so he said: This is yours, and this was gifted to me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said denying that upon him: Why did he not sit in the house of his father or the house of his mother to see whether his gift comes to him? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned the ruling of the gift to him to his governorship for which it was gifted to him, to his governorship he governs. So the like of that is what in this hadith of returning gift and promise to the cause for which they were, which is the private part whose marriage is sought. So they were made for the woman, not for the proposed-to. Since what is sought from him is for another, not for him. As for what of that is after the bond of marriage, it is for the one it is bonded for, because there became for him a cause deserving to be upon him, as said in this hadith: And the most deserving of what is honored upon is his daughter and his sister. So when he deserved honoring, what he is honored with thereby was pleasant for him. And when there was not for him before marriage a cause deserving thereby honoring from the one who gifted him and promised him, what he is honored with of that was not pleasant for him, nor was it lawful for him to keep it for himself. And more deserving of it than him is the one honored for whose sake to reach thereby what is sought from him. And Allah we ask for success."
"5199","694 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of proof upon the claimant and oath upon the defendant: are they established from him by way of isnad or not? Abu Jafar said: What we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what the people of isnads do not dispute its soundness. 4472 - What Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Nafi ibn Umar al-Jumahi narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The oath is upon the defendant."
"5200","4473 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If people were given by their claims, people would claim the blood of peoples and their wealth; but the oath is upon the defendant. So we looked in this hadith and found Ibn Abi Mulayka did not take it from Ibn Abbas by hearing; he only took it from him by his writing it to him."
"5201","4474 - As Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Khalid ibn Nizar al-Ayli narrated to us, Nafi ibn Umar informed us, from Ibn Abi Mulayka who said: I was a governor for Ibn al-Zubayr over Taif, so I wrote to Ibn Abbas: Two women were in a house stitching a mat of theirs, so one struck the hand of her companion with the awl, so she went out bleeding, while in the room were attendants. So she said: She struck me, while the other denied that. So Ibn Abbas wrote to me: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled that the oath is upon the defendant; and if people were given by their claims, people would claim the blood of people and their wealth. So call her and recite upon her this verse: Those who purchase with the covenant of Allah and their oaths a small price. So I recited the verse upon her, so she confessed. Nafi said: And I thought he said: So that reached Ibn Abbas and pleased him. So we stood thereby upon that this hadith Ibn Abi Mulayka only narrated from the writing of Ibn Abbas with it to him, not from his hearing it from him. Then we looked: was that narrated from Ibn Abbas with a meaning stronger than the meaning of correspondence?"
"5202","4475 - So we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Yahya, from Abd Allah ibn Abbas that two men disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked the claimant for proof upon what he claimed with him. He had no proof, so he made the defendant swear by Allah besides whom there is no god. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have done, but Allah has forgiven you by your saying: There is no god but Allah. So we stood by this hadith upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking the claimant for proof, and that when he had no proof he made the defendant swear for him upon what he made him swear upon. So this from Ibn Abbas was stronger than the first hadith. And in it is what indicates that what in the first hadith is transmitted from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And in it also is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking the claimant for proof. So that indicated that proof is sought from the claimant as oath is sought from the defendant. And this meaning was also narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from other than the way of Ibn Abbas."
"5203","4476 - As Fahd and Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq ibn Salama, from Abd Allah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever swears upon a binding oath to cut thereby wealth while he is sinful in it, meets Allah while He is angry with him. And the confirmation of that came down in the Book of Allah: Those who purchase with the covenant of Allah and their oaths a small price, the whole verse. So al-Ashath ibn Qays passed by him and said: What does Ibn Masud narrate to you? They said: He narrated to us such and such. He said: He spoke truth, by Allah. This verse came down about me and a companion of mine: between me and him was a well in land. So he said: It is mine. So we came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and disputed to him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Have you witnesses? So I said: No. So he said to my companion: Swear. So he swore. At that this verse came down."
"5204","4477 - And as Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Sadaqa ibn Khalid narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Shaqiq, from Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever swears upon an oath to cut thereby the wealth of a Muslim meets Allah the Exalted on the day he meets Him while He is angry with him. Then Abd Allah recited: Those who purchase with the covenant of Allah and their oaths a small price, to the end of the verse. So al-Ashath ibn Qays said: This verse came down about me. Between me and a man was dispute over land. So we came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said: Your proof. So I said: I have no proof. He said: Then he swears. I said: Then my wealth goes. So this verse came down: Those who purchase with the covenant of Allah and their oaths a small price."
"5205","4478 - And as Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to me that Yahya ibn Said narrated to him that Abu al-Zubayr informed him, from Adi ibn Adi, from his father that he said: Two men came disputing to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about land. So one said: It is mine. And the other said: It is mine; I possessed it and seized it. So he said: In it the oath is for the one in whose hand is the land. So when he uttered to swear, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: But whoever swears upon the wealth of a Muslim man meets Allah the Exalted while He is angry with him. He said: So who leaves it? He said: Paradise is his."
"5206","4479 - And as Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Harith ibn Sulayman al-Kindi narrated to us, Kurdus al-Thalabi narrated to me, from Ashath ibn Qays al-Kindi that a man of Kinda and a man of Hadramawt disputed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about land in Yemen. So the Hadrami said: O Messenger of Allah, my land; the father of this seized it from me. So he said to the Kindi: What do you say? He said: I say it is my land and in my hand; I inherited it from my father. So he said to the Hadrami: Have you proof? He said: No, but he swears O Messenger of Allah by Allah besides whom there is no god: he does not know that it is my land his father seized. He said: So the Kindi prepared for the oath. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No man cuts wealth by his oath but meets Allah on the day he meets Him while leprous. So the Kindi returned it."
"5207","4480 - And as Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, al-Harith ibn Sulayman narrated to us, then he mentioned this hadith with its isnad. And we mentioned before in this book the hadith of Wail ibn Hujr about the dispute of Imru al-Qays ibn Abis with Rabia ibn Aydan to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and his saying to the claimant of them: Your proof, and his saying to the claimant also when he said about his oath: does the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seek taking it for him from him that he goes with it? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him at that: You have nothing but that. And in what we mentioned in this chapter is the establishment of proof from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the obligation of proof upon the claimant, and of the obligation of oath upon the defendant. And Allah we ask for success."
"5208","695 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the two sellers differing in price. Abu Jafar said: This is a chapter the people of knowledge of isnads claim that what they find in it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is: 4481 - What Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, al-Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, Man ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from al-Qasim who said: Abd Allah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two sellers when they differ while there is no witness between them, then the statement is what the seller said, or they mutually return."
"5209","4482 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb and Musaddad narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Aban ibn Taghlib, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman that al-Ashath ibn Qays bought of Abd Allah slaves of the slaves of the emirate. So he came to him demanding him. So they differed in price. So Abd Allah said to him: Are you pleased that I judge between me and you with what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the two sellers differ, then the statement is what the seller said, or they mutually return, or they mutually leave."
"5210","4483 - And what Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Hafs narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Man ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Yazid from Muammal exactly. Abu Jafar said: So I mentioned this chapter to Ahmad ibn Shuayb, and said to him: Have you anything connected to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So he said to me: Yes."
"5211","4484 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris i.e. Abu Hatim informed us, Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Abu Umays, Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Ashath narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather who said: Abd Allah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When the two sellers differ while there is no proof between them, then it is what the owner of the commodity says, or they mutually leave. He said: So this hadith was what we found connected from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter - even if some people mention that it is far in his heart that Abu Umays met Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Ashath, because this Abd al-Rahman is of those al-Hajjaj killed, and that may be after ninety to a hundred. That of his saying in our view is nothing, for Abu Umays is old in age, and he says in this hadith: Abd al-Rahman son of al-Ashath narrated to me. And Abu Umays narrated from the likes of Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad: from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from al-Shabi, and from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman. And I mentioned this chapter before this to Ahmad ibn Abi Imran, and said to him: Have you anything connected from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So he said to me: As for finding it stated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), no. But proof was established with it from the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The oath is upon the defendant. And the two sellers when they differed in the price of the sold had each claimed a sale with a price other than the sale his companion claimed with the price he claimed. So they were thereby two mutual claimants of two different sales. And we understood from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the oath is upon the defendant. So we understood thereby that they both swear, and the claim of each of them against his companion is negated. And the slave remains in the hand of the defendant without proof established for him upon what was claimed against him in it, and without proof established for the claimant of sale upon him with the sale he claimed upon him in it. If a speaker says: These two mutual claimants both agreed that the buyer of the slave had sale established in him, differing only in price, so it is obligatory they return to the ruling of two men one claimed upon the other wealth, so he affirmed part of it and denied its rest: what he affirmed is binding, and he swears for him if he seeks his oath upon the rest of what he claimed upon him of it, and the slave is safe for the demanded by their agreement and his seller upon his ownership. My answer to him in that is that the matter is not as they mentioned: that differing in the two prices is differing in the two contracts. That is because I saw that when a man claims upon a man a thousand dirhams and five hundred, while the defendant denies that, and the claimant establishes a witness with a thousand and a witness with the thousand and five hundred he claimed, he is judged with what his two witnesses agreed upon. And I saw that if that were in a claim of sale with a thousand and five hundred, establishing two witnesses where one testifies for him upon what he claimed, and the other testifies that the sale was with a thousand, testimony is void and nothing is obligatory for him in it. So I understood thereby that differing in the two prices we mentioned requires a claim of two sales of the two mutual claimants, and that differing in the amount of the claimed price without annexing to a sale price requires one wealth differed in its amount. And when the two sellers differ in what we mentioned, and their two mutual claimants swear upon that, annulling what each claimed upon his companion is obligatory, and the slave being safe for the one in whose hand he is is obligatory, since no proof stood upon him with what was claimed against him in it. So I was sufficed thereby from seeking isnad from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the ruling of the two mutual claimants in the price differed in. He said: And Abu Hanifa and Abu Yusuf both held what this speaker I argued with this proof said. And what in my view in that is what I mentioned of what I argued in this chapter, which is the school of Muhammad ibn al-Hasan in it. And for what I mentioned from Abu Hanifa and Abu Yusuf they said: When they differ in the price of the sold, they both swear and mutually return if the sold stands; and when they differ in it while it is perished, the statement in it is the statement of the buyer. Abu Hanifa said: Because what analogy requires in my view in all that is that the statement be the statement of the buyer. But when there was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it what I mentioned, I said with it, and returned the answer after it to what analogy requires. Ibn Abi Imran said: But I say: had there been nothing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this, analogy would require what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it. And when that is likewise, employing it in the living and in the dead is obligatory, for what its return is obligatory when living, its value's return is obligatory when perished. Abu Jafar said: And this is a subtle good meaning. And success is from Allah."
"5212","696 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who eats by a Muslim man, or clothes by him, or stands by him a stand of repute. 4485 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Sulayman i.e. Ibn Musa said: Waqqas ibn Rabia narrated to us that al-Mustawrid narrated to them that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats by a Muslim man a meal, then Allah feeds him the like of it of Hell; and whoever clothes by a Muslim man a garment, then Allah clothes him of Hell the like of it; and whoever stands by a Muslim man a stand of repute, then Allah stands by him a stand of repute on the Day of Resurrection. So we contemplated this hadith, and the best of what came to us in it of his saying: Whoever eats by a man a meal, then Allah feeds him of Hell the like of it, is that upon the man who eats by the man the wealth of people, as the man takes their wealth to fill his poverty, taking it for himself. So he is thereby of the people of the threat mentioned in this hadith. And it is like the meaning of what is said: So-and-so eats by his religion, and so-and-so eats by his knowledge. And the meaning of: whoever clothes by a Muslim man, is like this meaning also. And the meaning of: whoever stands by a Muslim man a stand of repute, i.e. whoever stands for his sake a stand of repute, not for a meaning deserving thereby, but to disgrace him and make heard with him in it, is of the people of the threat mentioned in this hadith. And Allah we ask for success."
"5213","697 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying about the ground mentioned in the Book of Allah for one doing tayammum with it when water lacks, what is it? 4486 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi informed us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was aided by awe, and given comprehensive speech, and the earth was made for me pure and mosques, and I was brought the keys of the treasures of the earth and poured in my hand."
"5214","4487 - al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafii narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was given five no one before me was given: the whole earth was made for me mosque and pure, and I was aided by awe, and spoils were made lawful for me, and I was sent to the red and the white, and I was given intercession. Abu Jafar said: I heard al-Muzani say: al-Shafii said: Then I sat to Sufyan, so he mentioned this hadith. Al-Zuhri said: from Abu Salama or Said, from Abu Hurayra, then he mentioned it."
"5215","4488 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Salim narrated to us, from Hazim ibn Khuzayma of Taym al-Rabab, from Mujahid al-Makki, from Abu Hurayra who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was given five no prophet before me was given: I was sent to people all, their red and their black, while the prophet before me was sent to his household or to his village folk; and I was aided against my enemy by awe a month's journey before me and a month behind me; and spoils and fifths were made lawful for me and were not lawful for a prophet before me - fifths were taken and placed, then fire descended from heaven and burned them; and the earth was made for me mosque and pure, I pray in it where prayer reaches me."
"5216","4489 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzayma and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of the Tabuk expedition stood at night praying. So men of his Companions gathered behind him guarding him. Until when he prayed and turned to them, he said: This night I was given five no one before me was given: I was sent to people generally, while those before me the prophet was only sent to his people; and I was aided against the enemy by awe - had between me and him been a month's journey he is filled with awe of me; and spoils were made lawful for me, while those before me magnified eating them - they burned them; and the earth was made for me mosque and pure - wherever prayer reaches me I wipe and pray; while those before me magnified that - they only prayed in their churches and synagogues; and the fifth, what is it! It was said to me: Ask, for those before you asked. So I delayed my request to the Day of Resurrection; it is for you, and for whoever testified there is no god but Allah. So this hadith was argued by some people that whatever of the earth is mosque is of it pure. And of those who held that: Abu Hanifa. And he was opposed in that, so it was said: His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The earth was made for me mosque and pure, is upon division, and that intended is that part of it is mosque and part of it is pure, and that the pure of it is other than the mosque, and it is dust specifically, not other than it of what is not dust. And of those who opposed him in that: Abu Yusuf. And they narrated in that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5217","4490 - What Fahd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Said son of al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Abu Malik al-Ashjai (h), and what Ahmad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Awana narrated to us, from Abu Malik al-Ashjai, then both joined, so both said: From Rabi ibn Hirash, from Hudhayfa who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We were favored over people with three: the earth was made for us mosque, and its soil was made for us pure, and our rows were made as the rows of angels, and I was given the verses of the end of Surat al-Baqara from under the Throne - none before me was given of it, nor shall anyone after me be given of it. So what in this hadith indicated that the meaning of his saying in what we mentioned before it: The earth was made for me mosque and pure, is upon the division requiring part of it mosque and part pure, not upon the whole requiring all of it mosque and all pure, as Abu Yusuf said. And Allah we ask for success."
"5218","698 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about witr: does it have a known time not prayed but in it, and if not prayed in it not prayed after? Or is the whole time for it a time? 4491 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Lahia narrated to us, Abu Hubayra narrated to us that Abu Tamim Abd Allah ibn Malik al-Jayshani informed him that he heard Amr ibn al-As say: A man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah has added for you a prayer, so pray it between Isha and the morning prayer, witr witr. Indeed he is Abu Basra al-Ghifari. Abu Tamim said: So I and Abu Dharr were sitting, so Abu Dharr took my hand and we went to Abu Basra, finding him at the door next to the house of Amr ibn al-As. So Abu Dharr said: O Abu Basra, did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah added for you a prayer, so pray it between Isha and the rising of dawn, witr witr? So Abu Basra said: Yes. He said: Did you hear him? He said: Yes. He said: Did you hear him? He said: Yes. So we contemplated this hadith, and found in it of what Amr ibn al-As related from the man he related from that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah has added for you a prayer, so pray it between Isha and the morning prayer, witr witr. So his saying: To the morning prayer, may intend thereby the prayer itself - and between prayer and rising of dawn is a period of time - and may be that to the time of the morning prayer which is after the Isha prayer. Then what Abu Dharr addressed Abu Basra with: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah added for you a prayer, so pray it between Isha and the rising of dawn, witr witr? And in that is what uncovered the meaning bearing each of the first two aspects, that it is upon the rising of dawn, not upon the morning prayer itself. Then we looked: do we find this hadith from Abd Allah ibn Hubayra from other than this aspect we brought it from?"
"5219","4492 - So we found Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Said ibn Yazid i.e. Abu Shuja al-Himyari narrated to us, Ibn Hubayra narrated to me, from Abu Tamim al-Jayshani that Amr ibn al-As addressed people on Friday and said: Abu Basra narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah has added for you a prayer, and it is witr, so pray it between the Isha prayer and the Fajr prayer. Abu Tamim said: So Abu Dharr took my hand and walked in the mosque to Abu Basra, and said: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say what Amr said? Abu Basra said: Yes, I heard this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar said: So what in this of his saying between the Isha prayer and the Fajr prayer may intend thereby the prayer itself, or intend thereby the time of prayer. So we looked in that: do we find anything of that in other than this hadith?"
"5220","4493 - So we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, Ibn Lahia and al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd Allah ibn Rashid al-Zawfi, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Murra, from Kharija ibn Hudhafa al-Adawi that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah has commanded you with a prayer better for you than red camels, between the Isha prayer and the rising of dawn, witr witr."
"5221","4494 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sa'd, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. And in this hadith was that by which we resolved the difficulty we faced regarding what the first two hadiths we mentioned could imply. Then we considered: has anything else been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter besides these reports?"
"5222","4495 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, Yahya meaning Ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah al-'Awaqi, he said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri said: They asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the witr, and he said: Perform the witr before the dawn."
"5223","4496 - And we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Hasten to perform the witr before the dawn."
"5224","4497 - And we found Muhammad ibn Ali had narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Asim, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5225","4498 - And we found Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Sulayman ibn Musa informed me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When dawn rises, all night prayer and the witr have gone, so perform the witr before dawn. And this hadith of Ibn Umar was the clearest we found in this chapter regarding the time of the witr in which it is commanded to be prayed."
"5226","4499 - And we found Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahini narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Abu Qatadah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Bakr: When do you perform the witr? He said: At the beginning of the night. And he said to Umar: When do you perform the witr? He said: At the end of the night. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Bakr: You took the cautious course, and he said to Umar: You took the strong course. Then we considered what was reported from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this."
"5227","And we found Fahd ibn Sulayman had narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from al-A'mash, Abu Ishaq narrated to me, from Asim ibn Damrah, that a man said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: I asked Abu Musa about the witr, and he said: When the caller to prayer calls, there is no witr. So Ali said: He strained the bow and went to excess in giving verdict; the witr is between the two prayers. As for the statement of Abu Musa, 'when the caller calls there is no witr,' it may refer to a call they made at night before dawn, and it may refer to a call they made after dawn. As for the statement of Ali, 'the witr is between the two prayers,' in it is affirmation of the witr after dawn and denial that it is after the dawn prayer; and it may mean the witr that has the merit of witr, and it may refer to the witr that is prayed only in that time and not after it."
"5228","And we found Harun ibn Kamil had narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Marzuq informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad ibn Hilal, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: The witr is between the evening prayer and dawn."
"5229","And we found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: he narrated to us - then he mentioned one of the two men, either Abu Ghassan or Ahmad ibn Yunus - Abu Ja'far said: I doubt - he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad ibn Hilal, from Abdullah, the like of it."
"5230","And we found Harun had narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Malik ibn Mighwal informed us, from Abu Husayn, from al-Aswad, from Abdullah, the like of it."
"5231","And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, he said: The prayer was commenced, so we waited for Amr ibn Shurahbil, and he was their imam, and he said: I was performing the witr. Then he said: Abdullah was asked: Is there witr after the call to prayer? He said: Yes, and after the iqamah. So in this is what indicates that the witr, in the view of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, was permissible after the iqamah, and in that is what indicates that it is unrestricted in all time. Then we returned to what the people of knowledge upon whom fatwa turns in the cities say, and we found them upon two sayings: among them are those who say he makes it up throughout all time just as he makes up other missed prayers, and among those who say that are Abu Hanifah and his companions; and among them are sayers who say he prays it between him and the dawn prayer and does not pray it after that, and among those who say that are Malik and al-Shafi'i. And when they differed in that with this difference, we considered what they differed about, and we found the prayers that are made up when missed are of two kinds: one kind for which all time is a time, namely the five prayers, prayed in their times and made up after their times throughout all time except the times in which the like of them are not prayed; and one kind is the Friday prayer, prayed in a specific time of its day, and whoever misses praying it in that time of its day does not pray it after that in the rest of its day nor in later time. And the witr must be one of two faces: either like the five prayers, made up throughout all time as they are made up, or like Friday, prayed only in the time appointed for it and not prayed in near or distant time after it. And since we found it prayed after the passing of the night appointed as its time in what is near to it, we understood thereby that it is in the ruling of the five prayers regarding the time in which it is made up, and that all time is a time for it, so it is prayed in its distant time as it is prayed in its near time. And from Allah we ask success."
"5232","699 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what the people of knowledge differed about regarding qunut in the witr, whether it is before bowing or after it, and what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deciding between them in that. Abu Ja'far said: It has been reported from more than one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his qunut in the witr that it was before bowing. Among those from whom that was reported is Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and even if it turned only upon Aban ibn Abi Ayyash, it was yet borne by more than one of the people of chains, and they included it in this chapter when it was from other than Anas ibn Malik, so we too included it therein for that reason. 4500 - And it is what Yazid ibn Sinan had narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Aban ibn Abi Ayyash informed us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, he said: I spent the night with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to see how he performs qunut in his witr, before bowing or after it, and he performed qunut before bowing. Then I sent Umm Abd, and I said: Spend the night with his wives and see how he performs qunut, and she came to me and informed me that he performed qunut before bowing. And Abdullah ibn Mas'ud was upon that after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5233","As Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, he said: Abdullah would not perform qunut in any of the prayers except in the witr, before bowing."
"5234","And as Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hisham informed us. And as Harun informed us, he said: Nu'aym informed us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Hisham informed us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, that he would perform qunut before bowing, and the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), meaning in the witr. And this meaning has also been reported from Ubayy ibn Ka'b."
"5235","4501 - As Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Bukhari al-Ahwal and others had narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Hanzali al-Razi Abu Hatim narrated to us, Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Mis'ar, from Zubayd, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform witr with three units, not giving salutation in them until he finished; the first unit with Glorify the name of your Lord the Most High, the second with Say O disbelievers, and the third with Say He is Allah the One; and that he performed qunut before bowing, and when he finished his prayer he said: Glory be to the King, the Holy, twice raising his voice, and making the third aloud. And among them is Abdullah ibn Abbas."
"5236","4502 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Mu'alla ibn Mansur al-Razi narrated to us, Ata ibn Muslim informed us, al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I spent the night at my aunt Maymunah's, and I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray eight units, then perform witr; he recited in the first unit the Opening of the Book and Glorify the name of your Lord the Most High, and recited in the second the Opening of the Book and Say O disbelievers, and in the third the Opening of the Book and Say He is Allah the One, then he performed qunut and supplicated, then bowed."
"5237","So a speaker said: Does the hearing of Habib ibn Abi Thabit from Ibn Abbas stand proven? Our answer to him in that was that his hearing from him and from Abdullah ibn Umar is established, and there has been reported in what he heard from him what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Shu'bah informed us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, that he heard Ibn Abbas, and a man asked him and said: I am a man of the Sawad who undertakes tax collection in the village, not intending to wrong, only intending to ward off wrong from myself, then he recited this verse: Fight those who do not believe in Allah nor in the Last Day nor forbid what Allah and His Messenger forbade, up to His saying: while they are humbled. Then he said: He strips humiliation from their necks and puts it upon your neck. Abu Ja'far said: Then we returned to the hadith of Ubayy, and do we find it from other than the hadith of Mis'ar as Hafs reported it from Mis'ar?"
"5238","4503 - And we found Ali ibn Sa'id ibn Bashir al-Razi had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Musa al-Harrani al-Asamm and Ishaq ibn Zurayq at Ra's al-Ayn narrated to us, they said: Makhlad ibn Yazid al-Harrani informed us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Zubayd al-Yami, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform witr with three units, reciting in the first unit Glorify the name of your Lord the Most High, in the second Say O disbelievers, and in the third Say He is Allah the One, and he would perform qunut before bowing; and when he gave salutation and finished, he said at his finishing: Glory be to the King, the Holy, three times, prolonging the last of them."
"5239","4504 - And we found Ali ibn Sa'id had narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Umar ibn Khalid al-Raqqi, known as Ibn al-Aqta', narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform witr with Glorify the name of your Lord the Most High, and Say O disbelievers, and Say He is Allah the One, and he would perform qunut before bowing. And all these reports were upon qunut before bowing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and from those of his Companions whose qunut in the witr we mentioned. And analogy also bore witness to this saying, for we saw qunut as an addition in this prayer over other prayers, and we saw the additions in prayers over other agreed-upon prayers to be the takbir in the two Eids, and we found them not differing that it is before bowing, not after bowing, so analogy upon that was that the qunut added in the witr over other prayers be before bowing in it, not after bowing. Then a speaker among those who deny qunut before bowing said: I found these who perform witr before bowing adding in this prayer a takbirah for which we find no basis, and it is not permissible to add in prayers what has no basis. Our answer to him in that was that those who added this takbirah found for it a basis from two eminent men of the Emigrants among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), namely Ali ibn Abi Talib and Abdullah ibn Mas'ud."
"5240","As Ali ibn Shaybah had narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, Waki' informed us, from Sufyan, from Abd al-A'la meaning al-Tha'labi, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, that Ali said takbir in the qunut when he finished the recitation and when he bowed."
"5241","And as Ali had narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Masruq and al-Aswad and the companions of Abdullah, they said: Abdullah would not perform qunut except in the witr, and he would perform qunut before bowing, saying takbir when he finished his recitation at the time of qunut. So this was among what makes known that Ali and Abdullah did not say it by inference nor by derivation, since the like of it is not said by inference nor by derivation; it is only said by tawqif with which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instructed the people. So that was, in our view, among what must not be abandoned, and among what its sayers must be praised for."
"5242","Then we found from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, what further strengthened this meaning, in his qunut in the dawn prayer before bowing therein, as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah had narrated to us, Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us; and as Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us, then they joined and both said: from Mukhariq, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: I prayed behind Umar the dawn prayer, and when he finished the recitation in the second unit he said takbir, then performed qunut, then said takbir and bowed."
"5243","And as Bakkar narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mukhariq, from Tariq ibn Shihab, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So this Umar too said takbir for qunut before bowing, and that strengthened what we had reported before it from Ali and Abdullah, and this was among what its sayers must be praised for. Then a speaker said: Al-Hasan ibn Ali reported in the hadith of Ibn Abi Fudayk which he reported from Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, from his uncle, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Al-Hasan ibn Ali taught me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he finished the recitation in the third unit of the witr and nothing remained but bowing, said before bowing: O Allah, guide me among those You guided. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. He said: So in that is what indicates that he would not separate between the recitation and the qunut with takbir nor with anything else. Our answer to him in that was that what we mentioned from Ali and Abdullah, strengthened by what we reported after it from Umar, since it was not said by inference nor by derivation, has come under the ruling of what is related from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and whoever relates something preserving it is worthier than whoever falls short of it. And from Allah we ask success."
"5244","700 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding what differs in color of wheat, barley, dates and salt, that there is no harm in it two for one. 4505 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl informed us, my father narrated to us, from Abu Zur'ah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dates for dates, wheat for wheat, barley for barley, salt for salt, like for like; whoever adds or seeks addition, it is usury, except what differs in colors. So we pondered this hadith, and we found the colors mentioned in it to be the kinds of the different genera of these things subject to usury, not anything else, for we found no disagreement among the people of knowledge that black dates and other than black of them are one genus, not sold for the other color except like for like, and we found that reported from Abdullah ibn Umar."
"5245","As Yahya ibn Uthman had narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: What differs in colors of food, there is no harm in it hand to hand: dates for wheat, raisins for barley; and he disliked it on credit. And we found people's speech runs upon this, for we found them saying: So-and-so came to us with colors of food, meaning kinds of food, and saying: So-and-so spoke to us with colors of speech. And this was the worthiest interpretation of the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we mentioned, given what we reported therein from Ibn Umar confirming it, and given what we found used in people's speech indicating it. And from Allah we ask success."
"5246","701 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the measure the grandfather inherited from his son's son. 4506 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya informed us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My son's son has died, so what is mine of his inheritance? He said: Yours is a sixth. When he turned away, he called him and said: Yours is another sixth. When he turned away, he called him and said: The other sixth is a grant. So in this hadith was what needs standing upon the meaning intended by it, for in it the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the grandfather who asked him what was his of his son's son's inheritance: Yours is a sixth. And we knew he restricted him to a sixth only because the rest of the inheritance had another entitled to it besides him, since there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge regarding the grandfather, the father's father, when there is no other, that he deserves all the inheritance of his son's son. Then he said to him: Yours is another sixth, then informed him that that sixth is a grant. So we understood that he granted him only from what has no entitled one by causing him to inherit it from that deceased. And this was, in our view, before Allah the Exalted sent down upon His Prophet regarding the inheritance of the grandfather, the father's father, from his son's son, except the sixth which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) first gave the grandfather who asked him. And what remained of his inheritance was of what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted that grandfather the other sixth from what Allah had not sent down upon His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anything about. So its ruling was in the ruling of property left by one who left it with no entitled one by inheritance from him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the grandfather from it what he gave him as a grant, and deferred the rest of it to see his view in it. And inheritances at the beginning of Islam ran only upon the path of bequests thereof, among it the saying of Allah the Blessed and Exalted: Prescribed for you when death approaches one of you, if he leaves good, is bequest for parents and near relatives. So that indicated that the parents were not entitled of their child's inheritance except what was bequeathed to them thereof. And when that was so, the ruling of that inheritance, when there was no bequest therein for them, was in the ruling of property with no entitled one, whose ruling returns to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to place it where he saw fit. Then Allah the Exalted abrogated that with the inheritances He obligated in the estates of the deceased, and He did not send them down all together, but sent down part after part. So it is possible that what He had sent down thereof at that time was the sixth of the deceased's property for his grandfather, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid that sixth to the grandfather who asked him what was his of his son's son's inheritance, and granted him after that of its remainder what he granted him thereof, and what remained besides that of that inheritance had no ordinance of Allah the Mighty and Majestic therein, and its ruling was the ruling of inheritances that are not for a specific heir. So this is the best we found in interpreting this hadith, and Allah knows best the reality of the matter in it. And there has also been reported in this chapter another hadith, and it is:"
"5247","4507 - What Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Shabbabah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, from Yunus meaning Ibn Abi Ishaq, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Ma'qil ibn Yasar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brought an inheritance case in which was a grandfather, and he gave him a sixth or a third. And this hadith was, in our view, not contradicting the first hadith, for what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the grandfather of that deceased in this hadith is a third or a sixth, and it was worthier for us to make it the sixth which Imran preserved from him, so what he gave him of that sixth was by causing him to inherit it from that deceased, and Ma'qil did not preserve what was from him regarding the rest of that inheritance, and Imran preserved it, so whoever preserved something is worthier with it than whoever fell short of it. And with Allah is success."
"5248","702 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: Had Mut'im ibn Adi been alive and spoken to me regarding these filthy ones - meaning the captives of Badr - I would have released them to him. 4508 - Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had Mut'im ibn Adi been alive and spoken to me regarding these filthy ones, I would have released them to him, meaning the captives of Badr, and he had a favor with the Prophet. So a questioner asked about the meaning of this hadith and said: How is it permissible for him to release to him those of the disbelievers who came into his captivity, whose ruling is the ruling of killing or the ransom that returns to him and his companions, as He the Mighty and Majestic said: When you meet those who disbelieve, striking of necks until when you have inflicted slaughter upon them, then secure the bond, then either favor afterward or ransom. Our answer to him in that was that in this verse he recited to us is what indicates the meaning we were asked about, for Allah the Exalted gave His Prophet therein, after securing the bond, favor or ransom. So He had given him to favor and release whom he favored, or to take from him the ransom by which he ransoms himself from the killing obligatory upon him. And favor is what he said he would have done for al-Mut'im ibn Adi had he asked him regarding them. So that agreed with the hadith of Jubayr we mentioned. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) favored in other than the captives of Badr, namely the captives of Hawazin when they spoke to him regarding them, and he answered them by saying: The most beloved speech to me is the truest, then gave them choice between one of the two parties: either the captives or the property, so they chose the captives, and he released them to them. And we shall mention that in its place later in this book of ours, Allah the Mighty and Majestic willing. And from Allah we ask success."
"5249","703 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what was from him regarding the captives of Hawazin when they asked him to favor them, and that he did not do that except after the Muslims' consent to it. 4509 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: And Urwah claimed that Marwan ibn al-Hakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the delegation of Hawazin came to him as Muslims and asked him to return to them their properties and captives, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: With me are whom you see, and the most beloved speech to me is the truest, and choose one of the two parties: either the captives or the property, and I had waited for them. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had waited for them some ten nights when he returned from Ta'if. When it became clear to them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would return to them only one of the two parties, they said: We choose our captives. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among the Muslims, praised Allah as He deserves, then said: To proceed: these brothers of yours have come repentant, and I see fit to return to them their captives, so whoever of you loves to make that willingly, let him do so, and whoever of you loves to remain upon his right until we give it to him from the first of what Allah bestows upon us, let him do so. So the people said: We have made it willingly for you, O Messenger of Allah, and for them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: I do not know who of you permitted in that among who did not permit, so return until your headmen raise to us your affair. So the people returned, their headmen spoke to them, then they returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him that they had made it willingly and permitted. Then a speaker said regarding this hadith: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not release whom he released of the captives of Hawazin until the Muslims released that regarding them. Yet you reported to us in the chapter before this chapter of this book of yours that the Messenger of Allah said to Jubayr ibn Mut'im when he spoke to him regarding the captives of Badr: An elder, had he come to me - meaning his father - and spoken to me regarding them, I would have released them to him. So in this is his informing Jubayr that had his father spoken to him regarding the captives about whom Jubayr spoke to him, he would have released them to him, with no mention from him of his need for the Muslims' releasing that to him regarding them, and this is severe discrepancy. Our answer to him in that was that what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he addressed Jubayr with regarding the captives of Badr, that was from him regarding captives whose way is killing them, or favoring them, or taking ransom from them and releasing them, and there was not in that any ownership of the Muslims fallen upon any of them; rather the ways regarding them were these faces we mentioned, nothing else. So it was for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to execute regarding them what he saw thereof, with no need for the Muslims' releasing that to him regarding them. And the captives of Hawazin were women upon whom ownerships had fallen, for they in that were unlike the men, since they are not killed while the men are killed, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had divided them among the Muslims, so they owned them, so it was not proper for him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to take them out of their ownerships except with their willing consent to that and their satisfaction with it. And among what was reported indicating his division of them among the Muslims before he asked regarding them what he asked of what we mentioned:"
"5250","4510 - What Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to me, al-Layth narrated to me, Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab and Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned six thousand of the captives of Hawazin, of women, men and children, to Hawazin when they embraced Islam, and he gave choice to women who were with men of Quraysh, among them Abd al-Rahman and Safwan ibn Umayyah, who had taken as concubines the two women with them of Hawazin, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave them choice, and they chose their people. Then this speaker said: This is a disconnected hadith, so do you have regarding the meaning you mentioned a connected hadith? Our answer to him in that was that there has been reported in that of connected hadith:"
"5251","4511 - What Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la had narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Jarir ibn Hazim informed me that Ayyub narrated to him that Nafi' narrated to him that Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to him that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at al-Ji'ranah after returning from Ta'if, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed in the pre-Islamic time to seclude for a day in the Sacred Mosque, so what do you see? He said: Go and seclude for a day. He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had given him a slave girl of the fifth, and when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed the captives of Awtas, Umar ibn al-Khattab heard their voices saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has freed us. So he said: What is this? They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has freed the captives of Awtas. So Umar said: O Abdullah, go to that slave girl and free her path. Abu Ja'far said: And this hadith is regarding the captives of Hawazin even though that was not mentioned in this hadith, for that was at al-Ji'ranah, and al-Ji'ranah was in the eighth year of the Hijrah, and in it was the battle of Hawazin, and it has indicated what we mentioned of this meaning."
"5252","4512 - What Yazid ibn Sinan had narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Abdullah al-Bakka'i narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, a slave girl of the captives of Hawazin, and he gifted her to Abdullah ibn Umar his son. Ibn Ishaq said: Nafi', the freedman of Ibn Umar, narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: I sent her to my maternal uncles of Banu Jumah to prepare her for me until I circumambulate the House, then come to them, intending to have her when I returned to her. So I left the mosque when I finished, and the people were rushing, and I said: What is your affair? They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has returned to us our women and children. I said: That companion of yours is with Banu Jumah, so go and take her. So they went and took her. So this hadith uncovered what we mentioned, and it became clear, praise be to Allah the Exalted, that there is no contradiction in anything we reported in this chapter and in the chapter before it of what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the captives of the people of Badr, and what was from him regarding the captives of Hawazin, and that what was from him regarding the captives of Badr was regarding captives upon whom the ownerships of the Muslims had not fallen, so he had no need for the Muslims' releasing to him regarding them what he wished to do regarding them of favor and other than it, and that what was from him regarding the captives of Hawazin of his seeking from the Muslims willing consent to that for him was from him only because their ownerships had fallen upon them before that, so removing their ownerships from them was not proper except with their willing consent to that, their releasing it, and their permission in it. And with Allah is success."
"5253","704 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what was from him of returning to the sayings of the headmen of the Muslims in what they mentioned to him of what was from the people over whom they are headmen regarding the captives he wished to release to their people. Abu Ja'far said: We mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, of the hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud with which we began mentioning therein, the return of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the captives to what the headmen mentioned to him that it had been therein of the people over whom they are headmen. And there has also been reported in that: 4513 - What Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, Harun ibn Musa al-Farwi informed us, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, he said: Ibn Shihab said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me that Marwan and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the Muslims permitted him in freeing the captives of Hawazin, said: I do not know who of you permitted among who did not permit, so return until your headmen raise to us your affair. So the people returned, their headmen spoke to them, and they returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. So more than one of the people of knowledge inferred from what is in this hadith the acceptance by judges from agents of what they acknowledge upon those who appointed them in what they appointed them with before them, for the headmen in what we mentioned had been established by those over whom they are headmen in their affairs more than the standing of agents in what they were appointed with before the judges who appointed them with what they appointed them with before them. And among those of them who held to that were Abu Hanifah and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, and they said: Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not need, after the headmen informed him, to ask each of their people whether his headman told the truth in what he mentioned of him permitting in what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought permission in, and he sufficed with the word of the headmen from their people's word. And most of the people of knowledge besides them and besides Abu Hanifah, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on him, hold the like of that in the acceptance of acknowledgment by litigation agents before judges in the rights of those who appointed them. And from Allah we ask success."
"5254","705 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding captives, whether it is permissible to kill them or not. Abu Ja'far said: Ata ibn Abi Rabah disliked killing the captive in cold blood, as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that he disliked killing the captive in cold blood, and he recited this verse: Then either favor afterward or ransom. And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that he disliked killing the polytheist in cold blood, and he recited to us: Then secure the bond, to the end of the verse. Ibn Jurayj said: His saying: Then seize them and kill them wherever you find them, abrogated it. Abu Ja'far said: So we pondered what Ata said of what we mentioned from him, and we found Allah mentioned this meaning in two places of His Book: one of them the place mentioned in his hadith, and the other the one mentioned in Surah al-Anfal 67, and it is His saying the Mighty and Majestic: It is not for a prophet to have captives until he inflicts slaughter in the land; you desire the goods of this world, while Allah desires the Hereafter, and Allah is Mighty, Wise, to His saying: a great punishment. So in this verse was Allah's informing His Messenger and the believers that it does not befit a prophet to have captives of the polytheists until he inflicts slaughter among them, and it is understood that killing among them by what is in this verse is worthier than capturing them. And in that is what indicates His permitting them to kill them, and employing what is worthier with them than the capture they are in. And this has indicated the permissibility of killing captives, not the prohibition of killing them. And the verse Ata recited in his hadith was likewise indicating the permissibility of killing them after capturing them, not prohibiting it, for in it is: Then either favor afterward or ransom, and favor and ransom are after capturing. So killing them before capturing is not prohibited by it. We shall mention its ruling here to stand upon it. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding killing captives:"
"5255","4514 - What Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ja'far al-Raqqi narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Ibrahim, he said: Al-Dahhak ibn Qays wished to appoint Masruq, so Umarah ibn Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt said to him: Do you appoint a man of the remnants of Uthman's killers?! So Masruq said to him: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud narrated to us - and he was in our souls not a liar - that your father, when he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he ordered his killing, and he said: Who is for the children, O Muhammad? He said: The Fire. So I am pleased for you with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was pleased for you with."
"5256","4515 - And what al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam all together had narrated to us; al-Rabi' said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, and Muhammad said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth informed us; they both said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent cavalry toward Najd, and it brought a man of Banu Hanifah called Thumamah ibn Uthal, chief of the people of al-Yamamah, and they tied him to a pillar of the mosque's pillars. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to him and said: What do you have, O Thumamah? He said: I have good, O Messenger of Allah; if you kill, you kill one with blood, and if you bestow favor, you bestow upon one grateful, and if you want property, ask and be given of it what you wish. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left him until the morrow, then said: What do you have, O Thumamah? He said: I have what I said to you, then repeated the like of his first speech. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left him until after the morrow, then said: What do you have, O Thumamah? He said: I have what I said to you, then repeated the like of his first speech. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Release Thumamah. So he went to palms near the mosque, washed, then entered the mosque and said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. O Muhammad, there was no face on earth more hateful to me than your face, and your face has become the most beloved of all faces to me; by Allah, there was no religion more hateful to me than your religion, and your religion has become the most beloved religion to me; by Allah, there was no land more hateful to me than your land, and your land has become the most beloved land to me; and your cavalry seized me while I intended umrah, so what do you see? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him glad tidings and ordered him to perform umrah. When he arrived at Makkah, one said to him: Have you become a Sabian, O Thumamah? He said: No, but I embraced Islam with Muhammad the Messenger of Allah, and by Allah no grain of wheat shall come to you from al-Yamamah until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permits therein."
"5257","4516 - And what Yazid ibn Sinan had narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to us that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent cavalry of his; then he mentioned the like of it."
"5258","4517 - And what Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus had narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Zanjawayh, and he is Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik, narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Abdullah and Ubaydullah the sons of Umar informed us, from Sa'id meaning al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, that Thumamah al-Hanafi was captured, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would come to him in the morning and say: What do you have, O Thumamah? And he would say: If you kill, you kill one with blood, and if you favor, you favor one grateful, and if you want property, be given of it what you wish. So the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) loved ransom and said: What do we do with killing this one? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) favored him one day and he embraced Islam one day, so he freed him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent with him to the garden of Abu Talhah and ordered him to wash, so he washed and prayed two units, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your brother's Islam is good. Do you not see the standing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon the saying of Thumamah to him while a captive: If you kill, you kill one with blood? And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not repel him from that, nor say to him that whoever is captured is safe, meaning that he would not kill the captive, while you are a captive."
"5259","4518 - And what Ishaq also had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) captured Thumamah ibn Uthal, and he would pass by him and say: O Thumamah, what do you have? And he would say: If you kill, you kill one with blood, and if you favor, you favor one grateful; then he mentioned the hadith. So in that is what indicates that killing him was permissible for him."
"5260","4519 - And what Yunus had narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik."
"5261","4520 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, Abu al-Walid narrated to us, Malik narrated to us in both their hadiths together, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah in the year of the conquest with a helmet upon his head; when he removed it, a man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, this Ibn Khatal is clinging to the curtains of the Ka'bah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Kill him. Abu Ja'far said: And Ibn Khatal on that day was in the ruling of a captive."
"5262","4521 - And what Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, Ahmad ibn al-Mufaddal al-Hafari narrated to us, Asbat ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Suddi claimed, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, from his father, he said: On the day of the conquest of Makkah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave safety to the people except four men and two women, and said: Kill them even if you find them clinging to the curtains of the Ka'bah: Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl, Abdullah ibn Khatal, Miqyas ibn Subabah, and Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh. As for Abdullah ibn Khatal, he was brought while clinging to the curtains of the Ka'bah, and Sa'id ibn Hurayth and Ammar ibn Yasir raced to him, and Sa'id outran Ammar, and he was the stronger of the two men, and killed him. As for Miqyas ibn Subabah, the people caught him in the market and killed him. As for Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl, he rode the sea, and a violent wind struck them, and the ship's people said to the ship's folk: Be sincere, for your gods avail you nothing here. So Ikrimah said: By Allah, if nothing but sincerity saves me at sea, nothing but it shall save me on land. O Allah, upon me is a covenant to You: if You save me from what I am in, I shall come to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then place my hand in his hand, and I shall find him pardoning, generous. So he was saved and embraced Islam. As for Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh, he hid with Uthman, and when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the people to pledge, he brought him until he stood him upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: O Messenger of Allah, pledge Abdullah. So he raised his head and looked at him three times, each time refusing, then pledged him after three. Then he turned to his Companions and said: Was there not among you a man to stand to this one when he saw me refrain from pledging him and kill him? They said: We did not know, O Messenger of Allah, what was in yourself, so why did you not gesture to us with your eye! So he said: It does not befit a prophet to have treacherous eyes."
"5263","4522 - And what Fahd had narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Ahmad ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in this hadith to his Companions what"
"5264","4523 - And what Yazid ibn Sinan had narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Ayyub al-Sha'iri and Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us. And what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki had narrated to us, Hafs ibn Umar al-Juddi narrated to us; they said: Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Nafi' Abu Ghalib narrated to us, he said: I saw a funeral of many folk, in it Anas ibn Malik. So Anas said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a man of the disbelievers was the severest of people upon the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If Allah enables him over him, he shall strike his neck. Then Allah the Exalted gave the Muslims victory over them, and they would bring them as captives, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pledge them, until that man was brought, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refrained from his pledge so the man might fulfill his vow, and the man disliked to stand and strike his neck before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him doing nothing, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pledged him. So the man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: What do I do, O Messenger of Allah, with my vow? So he said: I refrained from him so you might fulfill your vow, but you did nothing. So he said: O Messenger of Allah, had you but gestured to me! So he said: It is not for a prophet to gesture. And in Yazid's hadith especially, that was in the battle of Hunayn. So in this hadith too is the like of what is in the hadith before it."
"5265","4524 - And what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani had narrated to us, Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us. 4525 - And what Fahd ibn Sulayman had narrated to us, Abu Ghassan narrated to us; they both said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated to me, Abu Wa'il narrated to me, Ibn Mu'ayz al-Sa'di narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Ibn al-Nawwahah and a man with him called Ibn Waththal came, having come with him as two envoys from Musaylimah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Do you bear witness that I am the Messenger of Allah? So they said: Do you bear witness that Musaylimah is the Messenger of Allah? He said: I believe in Allah and His messengers; had I been one to kill an envoy, I would have killed you two. Abu Ja'far said: And they at that time were as two captives. And in what we mentioned of these reports is what indicates the permissibility of killing captives. And from Allah we ask success."
"5266","706 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the ghurrah he decreed for the fetus, and what its measure is of the blood money. 4526 - Ibrahim ibn Ahmad ibn Marwan al-Wasiti narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Abdullah al-Wasiti narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Sa'id meaning Ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Malih, from Hamal ibn Malik ibn al-Nabighah, he said: He had two wives, Mulaykah and Umm Afif, and one of them stoned the other with a stone, striking her front while she was pregnant, and she cast a fetus and died. So that was raised to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed blood money upon the killer's clan, and decreed for the fetus a ghurrah: a slave or slave girl, or a hundred sheep, or ten camels. So her father, or a man of her clan, stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, he neither drank nor ate, nor cried nor raised voice, and the like of that, is his blood wasted? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are in nothing of the rhymed speech of pre-Islamic time. So we pondered this hadith after standing upon the consensus of the people of knowledge regarding the measure of the ghurrah obligatory for the fetus of the blood money, that it is half a tenth of it. So we found in it the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning the ghurrah that it is a slave or slave girl, and in that was informing the people what the ghurrah is. Then he followed that with his saying: or a hundred sheep. So that was not of the ghurrah in anything, but it is the portion that is the measure of the ghurrah of the blood money, of sheep, what it is, for the blood money of sheep, in the saying of whoever makes sheep a kind of the kinds of blood moneys, is two thousand sheep, so a hundred thereof is half its tenth. And among those who made the blood money of sheep this measure were Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. As for Abu Hanifah, he would make blood money only in camels, dirhams and dinars especially. As for Malik, he would make it in camels, dinars and dirhams. As for al-Shafi'i, he would make it in camels especially, besides other than them. And what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that was worthier. And what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his aiming with blood money for the slain Ansari to a hundred camels, nor with his saying regarding mistaken intentional killing therein is a hundred camels, did not repel that blood money be kinds besides camels. Then he said in this hadith: or ten camels. So this was, in our view, an error in transmission, for its departure from all the sayings of the scholars, and for their receiving it with disagreement to it. And this hadith turned only upon Abu al-Malih; so among those who reported it from him is Qatadah as we mentioned, and Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani reported it from him."
"5267","4527 - As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, he said: I heard Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali ibn Usamah, and he had accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - thus al-Rabi' narrated to us in Asad's musnad in a type titled with the musnad of Usamah al-Hudhali, so we understood thereby that the place of 'ibn Usamah' is 'from Usamah,' especially as he said therein: and he had accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while Abu al-Malih did not accompany the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor did we know he saw him; and the one who accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his father, and he is Usamah ibn Umayr - he said: Among us were two women, and one of them struck the other with a pole, killing her and killing what was in her belly. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed for the fetus a ghurrah: a slave or slave girl, or a horse, or ten camels, or so-and-so of sheep. So a man of the killer's kin said: How do we pay blood for one, O Messenger of Allah, who neither ate nor drank, nor cried nor raised voice; is the like of that wasted? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Are you a rhymester? Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed that the inheritance of the slain woman is for her husband and child, and that the blood money is upon the killer's clan. So in this hadith was an addition from Ayyub over Qatadah: mention of the horse. And in it was: or ten camels, as in Qatadah's hadith. And that was, in our view, of Ayyub's hadith as it is of Qatadah's hadith, in the error in the number mentioned in both of camels, for there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge that the measure of blood money of camels is a hundred camels, and half a tenth thereof is only five camels, not ten camels. And among those who reported it from him also is Salamah ibn Tammam, and he is Abu Abdullah al-Shaqari."
"5268","4528 - As Fahd narrated to us, Uthman ibn Sa'id ibn Murrah al-Qurashi narrated to us, al-Minhal ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Tammam, from Abu al-Malih, from his father, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disputing a woman, and said: I married this woman, and her co-wife struck her belly, and she cast a dead fetus. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pay its blood. And with her was a brother of hers called Imran ibn Uwaymir, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, do we pay blood for one who neither ate nor drank, nor cried nor raised voice, and the like of him is wasted? So he, upon him be peace, said: Leave me from the rajaz of the desert, or the rajaz of the Bedouins; therein is a ghurrah: a slave or slave girl, or five hundred dirhams, or a horse, or a hundred and twenty sheep. He said: O Messenger of Allah, by Him who sent you with truth, I own neither dinar nor dirham nor anything. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her brother - and he was that day over the charities of Hudhayl - : Take from under your hand a hundred and twenty sheep. So he did. And in this hadith regarding the ghurrah was that it is a slave or slave girl, or a horse. And in it also was: or five hundred dirhams. So in that is what indicates that dirhams are a genus of the genera of blood money, and that its measure thereof is ten thousand dirhams as the Kufans say in that, unlike what the Hijazis say therein, for the Kufans make the measure of blood money of dirhams ten thousand, and the Hijazis make it thereof twelve thousand dirhams. And they have in that of what was reported agreeing with what they said therein."
"5269","4529 - What Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Awaqi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed with blood money twelve thousand. And regarding that was revealed: And they resented not except that Allah and His Messenger enriched them of His bounty, by their taking the blood money. Then one impugned this hadith and said: There reported it from Amr one more preserving than Muhammad ibn Muslim, namely Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, and he did not mention Ibn Abbas therein."
"5270","4530 - And he mentioned what Bahr ibn Nasr had narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ikrimah, with this hadith, and he did not mention Ibn Abbas in this hadith. So of our proof to them against them in that was that Ibn Uyaynah would sometimes raise this hadith and mention Ibn Abbas therein, and sometimes not mention Ibn Abbas therein. So among those who reported it from him affirming Ibn Abbas therein is Muhammad ibn Maymun al-Makki, who is called al-Khayyat."
"5271","4531 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Maymun informed us, Sufyan informed us, from Amr, from Ikrimah; we heard him once saying: from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed with twelve thousand, meaning in the blood money. So this hadith of Sufyan and Muhammad ibn Muslim returned to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him. And in this hadith was of the measure of blood money of dirhams what is therein, namely twelve thousand dirhams. And in Abu Malih's hadith is what indicates that it is ten thousand. And this is of what is not grasped by inference nor by derivation; rather it is said therein by suspension. And ten thousand we are certain of its obligatoriness, and we are not certain of the obligatoriness of what exceeds it. So the worthiest thing for us was not to decree in blood money of dirhams except with ten thousand. And in this hadith: or a hundred and twenty sheep. And this is of what we know no one of the scholars held to, and in their consensus upon its contrary is what indicates its corruption, and that the worthier in that is what is in Qatadah's hadith by which the blood money of sheep returns to two thousand sheep. Except that some people impugned this hadith of Salamah ibn Tammam, and mentioned that Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak made it from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Malih, from his father, and mentioned in that:"
"5272","4532 - What Yahya ibn Uthman had narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, al-Minhal ibn Khalifah informed us, Salamah ibn Tammam narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Malih al-Hudhali, from his father, and he did not mention after his father anyone, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought two women who were with a man of Hudhayl called Hamal ibn Malik; one of them struck the belly of her fellow with a tent pole, and she cast a dead fetus. So they went with them to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with her was a brother of hers called Imran. So he related to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he decreed therein a ghurrah. So he said: Do we pay blood for one who neither drank nor ate, nor cried nor raised voice; is the like of him wasted? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave me from the rajaz of the desert folk, or from the rajaz of the Bedouins; therein is a ghurrah: a slave or slave girl, or five hundred dirhams - and he did not mention what it is - or a horse, or a hundred and twenty sheep. So he said: O Messenger of Allah, she has sons who are chiefs of the quarter, worthier to pay her blood than I. So he said: You are worthier to pay blood for your sister than her child. So he said: We have nothing to pay blood nor to pay. So he said to Hamal ibn Malik, and he is the husband of the two women and was over the charities of Hudhayl: Take from under your hand, of the charities of Hudhayl, a hundred and twenty sheep. So this hadith of Salamah ibn Tammam returned from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Malih, from his father, and thereby returned disconnected. Except that it is possible that Salamah ibn Tammam took it from Ibn Abi Malih likewise, and Abu Malih narrated it from his father as in the hadith of Uthman ibn Sa'id ibn Murrah, and that is the worthiest it is carried upon, given the eminence of Uthman ibn Sa'id, his precision and memory, especially as Ayyub agreed with him in his reporting this hadith from Ibn Abi Malih, from his father. And Allah knows best the reality of the matter in that, and Him we ask for success."
"5273","707 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he said to Yazid Abu Ma'n regarding his charity which Ma'n took from the man with whom he had placed it: Yours is what you intended, O Yazid, and yours, O Ma'n, is what you took. 4533 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja al-Ghudani narrated to us, Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abu al-Juwayriyah, from Ma'n ibn Yazid, he said: I pledged the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I and my father and grandfather, and he addressed upon me and married me, and I disputed to him. And my father had gone out with dinars to give them as charity, and placed them with a man, and I took them and brought them to him. So he said: By Allah, you I did not intend with them. So I disputed him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Yours is what you intended - to the father - and yours is what you took, O Ma'n. Abu Ja'far said: So we pondered this hadith, and we found in it Ma'n's mention that his father went out with the dinars with which he had gone out to give them as charity, and placed them with the man with whom he had placed them. So that placing of his with that man was - and Allah knows best - for those dinars, to place them in the face for which he took them out, and that man was to him therein an agent. And Yazid had preceded intending with those dinars charity upon other than his son, upon what is in the hadith of what he said to his son Ma'n after his taking his charity from that man. And agencies are only by what the appointers speak and address their agents with, not by what they intend in that and conceal from them in their hearts. And reward is only in what is of the intentions of givers of charity and others of the people of deeds by which nearness to Allah the Exalted is sought, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Deeds are only by intentions, and to a man is only what he intended, of what we shall mention later in this book of ours, Allah the Mighty and Majestic willing. And since the matter in what we mentioned was as we described, and there was from Yazid Abu Ma'n what was from him of the intention he intended in what he had taken out to give as charity, he had the reward of his intention, and what his son Ma'n took thereof he had taken from one appointed therein with permissible agency by what he did therein. So what the agent of his father did for him therein of what he paid to him was permissible for Ma'n, and his father had the reward of what he intended of charity with it upon the stranger whom he had wished it be charity upon. And Muhammad ibn al-Hasan argued with this hadith for his saying regarding one who gives the zakat of his property as charity upon a man he sees as unrelated to him, then knows after that that he is his father or son, that it suffices him. And this is not, in our view, of his saying in anything, nor in this hadith with which he argued is a proof for him therein. And the proof of that - and Allah knows best - is that that charity is not lawful to its taker, for it is the zakat of his father's property, and the zakat of his father's or son's property is not lawful to him. And when it is not lawful to him, it is not sufficing for his father, or for his son who gave it to him. And the like of that is a man paying the zakat of his property to a man upon that he is poor, then knowing after that that he is rich; so it does not suffice him either, for it is unlawful upon the one given it. And when it is unlawful upon him, it is not sufficing for its giver to him. And this is the saying of Abu Yusuf, and it is the worthiest said in this chapter in our view. And Allah knows best, and Him we ask for success."
"5274","708 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding additions in the prices of sold things: do they join the prices upon which those sales were contracted or not? Abu Ja'far said: We mentioned in what preceded of us in this book of ours the hadith of Jabir ibn Abdullah regarding his selling his camel to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his coming to Madinah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordering Bilal, when he arrived at Madinah, to pay him its price and to add him a qirat. So I said: The addition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shall never leave me; it was in my sack until the people of Sham took it on the day of al-Harrah. And its chain with which we mentioned it was: 4534 - that Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Jabir ibn Abdullah. And we repeated mentioning its chain here, for after we mentioned that we had brought it in what preceded of us in this book of ours, we thought we had not brought it, so we repeated its chain by way of precaution. So in the hadith of Jabir we mentioned is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) adding him, after the sale between them, in the price of the camel he had bought from him with it. And in that is proof that the addition joined that price and became of it, and became as one who contracted the sale with it along with other than it of what the sale was contracted with. And it was impossible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made Jabir own what he made him own with a meaning, and he not own it with that meaning, and own it with other than it, as says one who says: the addition in the price is a gift from the one who adds it to the one he adds it to. And among those who said that were Malik and Zufar, for things are owned only whence they were owned, not from other than it. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of addition in other than sales:"
"5275","4535 - What Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Fudayk informed us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man who stipulates with a woman, their companionship is three nights; if they love to separate, they separate, and if they love to add in the term, they add. Salamah said: I do not know, was it for us specially, or for people generally?"
"5276","4536 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man and woman who contract mut'ah, their companionship between them is three nights; if they love to add, they add. And in this hadith, in the time when mut'ah was free and lawful, was that when it is contracted upon a specific time and concluded upon it, then its two contractors love to add in that time a period they love, mentioning its measure, that that addition joins the first period, and that the ruling of the second period therein is the ruling of the first period. So the like of that also is sale: when it falls upon a specific thing with a specific price, then one of its two contractors wishes to add his fellow in what he made him own therein something, and he adds it to him and he accepts it from him, that that addition joins it and enters its ruling. And we reported the hadith of Jabir we reported in this chapter in establishing this meaning."
"5277","4537 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us in the like of it, he said: Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought from him a camel for an ounce of gold, and ordered Bilal, and he weighed for him and added him a qirat."
"5278","And there has been reported from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in their employing additions in sales after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what agrees with this meaning. Of that is what Ubayd ibn Rijal had narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with them, said: We wished that Uthman and Abd al-Rahman would trade until we see which of them is greater in enterprise in trade. So Abd al-Rahman bought from Uthman a horse for land of his, another, for forty thousand dirhams, or about that - Abd al-Razzaq doubted the number - if the bargain reaches it safe. Then he went a little, returned, and said: I add you six thousand if my messenger finds it safe. He said: Yes. So Abd al-Rahman's messenger found it dead. He said: So he exited from it by the other condition. So a man said to al-Zuhri: If he did not stipulate? He said: It is of the seller's property. So in this hadith was what it indicated of what al-Zuhri said at its end: that had the sale proceeded upon the first contract, the mare's death would have been of its buyer's property, namely Abd al-Rahman; then Abd al-Rahman added Uthman in its price an addition by which Uthman added him a condition obliging him that if it died before his messenger's arrival to it, it died of Uthman's property, namely its seller. So in that is what indicates joining additions in contracts. And that was from Uthman and Abd al-Rahman in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who wished they would trade to stand upon which of them is greater in enterprise in trade, and they did not deny what was from them against them, nor oppose them therein. So that indicated their following them upon it."
"5279","And of that is what Yazid ibn Sinan had narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, al-Ajlah al-Kindi informed us, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, he said: I saw Ammar ibn Yasir come out of the palace, buy animal feed for a dirham, and seek addition from its owner of a rope, and dispute him until this took a piece of it and this a piece of it, then carried it upon his shoulder until he entered the palace. Abu Ja'far said: And this was from Ammar while a governor, for he would not dwell the palace the governors dwelled except while a governor, and he verified that."
"5280","What Yahya ibn Uthman had narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Abu Sinan and al-Ajlah, from Ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, he said: I saw Ammar ibn Yasir come out while a governor, buy animal feed, and seek addition of a rope, and this would pull and this would pull. So Abu Sinan said: I do not know which of them overcame? And al-Ajlah said: So they split it in halves. So in this hadith is that Ammar - while a governor for whom gift is not proper, nor accepting a present from anyone proper - sought addition from his seller of that feed. And that would not be from him except because it joins the sale and becomes of it with its share of its price, as it had the sale fallen upon it along with other than it fallen upon. And in that is what indicates the saying we informed of in what preceded of us in this chapter in what was said therein. And these additions, in our view, only join what they were added in after what they were added in is in the state in which resuming sale upon it is permissible. As for if it was entered by what prevents that, like the death of the sold, or like its buyer's freeing it, or like its exit from his ownership to another's ownership, then those additions, if they were, were unlike this meaning, and did not join that contract in which they were added. And He is the Granter of success, and He is the Sought for help."
"5281","709 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding addition in what addition is not permissible in; rather it returns to its adder, or is a gift from him to the one he added it to. 4538 - Salih ibn Shu'ayb ibn Aban al-Basri Abu Shu'ayb narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Mahdi al-Ubulli informed us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Abu Jahm ibn Hudhayfah as a collector, and a man disputed him regarding his charity, seized him and struck him, and Abu Jahm wounded him. So they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Retaliation, O Messenger of Allah. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yours is so-and-so. But they were not pleased. So he said: Yours is so-and-so. And they were pleased. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall address the people this evening and inform them of your pleasure. They said: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed, saying: These Laythis came to me seeking retaliation, and I offered them so-and-so and they were pleased. He said: Are you pleased? They said: No. He said: So the Emigrants were about to attack them, and the Prophet, upon him be peace, ordered them to refrain from them. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called them and added them. So he said: Are you pleased? They said: Yes. He said: So I shall address the people and inform them of your pleasure. They said: Yes. So he addressed the people and said: Are you pleased? They said: Yes. So in this hadith is a subtle meaning of jurisprudence that must be stood upon, and stood by upon that the addition in this meaning is unlike the addition in the two meanings we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. For the addition in the two meanings we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter is an addition in a sale whose two contractors may undo it then contract it anew, and a marriage they may undo or contract anew with what they contract with, so addition therein was permissible. And reconciliation for Abu Jahm ibn Hudhayfah is of what it is not permissible for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and those he reconciled with on his behalf to undo, for had a man wounded a man with a wound, or offended against him with an offense, and reconciled him thereof upon something, or reconciliation was made for him thereof upon something, then the two contractors of that reconciliation wished to undo it between them, they cannot do that, and if they undo it, it is not undone. And what is of this path, addition therein does not join its origin, and it is differed about: a group of the people of knowledge say it is void and returns to its adder, and among those of them who said that were Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf; and a group of them say it is a gift from its adder to the one he added it to, so if he delivers it to him it is permissible for him, and if he withholds it from him he is not compelled to deliver it to him. And this is a meaning Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim mentioned what indicates its fame from Malik's school therein, and it is Zufar's saying therein, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan inclined to it in some of his questions entering this chapter. And we know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pay those people what is not lawful for them to take, and that he pays no one except what is wholesome for him, for of his law in the like of this is forbidding consuming usury and forbidding feeding it. And in his permitting them that is proof of its wholesomeness for them, and that it came to them as a gift from him to them, as said one who said it of those we mentioned from him. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the proof over all people. And with Allah is success."
"5282","710 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding what Allah sent down upon him about the People of the Book when they seek his judgment in their penalties, of judging between them therein, and of turning away from them therein, and whether that was abrogated by His saying: And judge between them by what Allah sent down, or not? 4539 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man al-Saqati narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, Mujalid ibn Sa'id al-Hamdani narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: A man of the people of Fadak committed adultery, and the people of Fadak wrote to some Jews at Madinah: Ask Muhammad about that; if he orders you with flogging, take it from him, and if he orders you with stoning, do not take it from him. So they asked him about that, and he said: Send to me the two most knowledgeable men among you. So they brought him a one-eyed man called Ibn Suriya, and another. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Are you two the most knowledgeable of those before you? So they said: Our people ascribed that to us. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Is not with you the Torah wherein is Allah's judgment? So they said: Yes. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: So I adjure you by Him who split the sea for the Children of Israel, sent down the Torah upon Musa, sent down manna and quails, shaded you with clouds, and saved you from Pharaoh's folk: what do you find in the Torah regarding stoning? So one of them said to the other: I was never adjured with the like of it. Then they said: We find that the look is adultery, embracing is adultery, and the kiss is adultery; and when four bear witness that they saw him entering and returning as the kohl stick enters the kohl container, stoning has become obligatory. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is that. So he ordered him stoned. And was revealed: If they come to you, judge between them or turn away from them; if you turn away from them they shall not harm you a thing, and if you judge, judge between them with justice, the verse. So in this hadith is that Allah the Exalted gave in the recited verse therein to His Prophet choice to judge between the Jews when they come to him, and to turn away from them and not judge between them. Then some people said: This is a firm verse, and what was mentioned in this hadith of the Prophet's stoning that Jew was by his choice to stone him, though he might not stone him, given Allah's saying: Or turn away from them, meaning: so do not judge between them. And others of the people of knowledge opposed them in that, and mentioned that this verse is abrogated by His saying: And judge between them by what Allah sent down and do not follow their desires. And they reported what they said in that from Abdullah ibn Abbas."
"5283","4540 - As Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn al-Harith al-Wasiti al-Baghandi had narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Two verses were abrogated of this surah, meaning Surah al-Ma'idah: If they come to you, judge between them or turn away from them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given choice: if he wished he judged between them, and if he wished he turned away from them and returned them to their judgments. Then was revealed: And judge between them by what Allah sent down and do not follow their desires. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was ordered to judge between them upon our Book. Abu Ja'far said: And this hadith of Ibn Abbas verified the abrogation of this verse by the verse recited in his hadith. And the ruling of those after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein of the rulers was upon the like of what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon thereof in each of these two sayings we mentioned. And the worthier for judgments therein, in our view - and Allah knows best - was judging between them, had the verse not been abrogated, not turning away from them, for when they judge between them, the two parties we mentioned bear them safe and leaving an obligatory upon them therein, for whoever says they judged, and upon them to judge with it, says: They performed the obligatory upon them therein; and the others say: They judged with what they may judge with, and the judges thereby exit, in their view, from leaving an obligatory, if any was upon them therein. And when they turn away from them and leave judging between them, one party says: They left an obligatory upon them, and the other party says: They left what they may leave. And what obliges safety for them with both parties together was worthier for them than what obliges them safety with one party and does not oblige it for them with the other. This had the verse not been abrogated; then when it became obligatory, by the hadith of Ibn Abbas we mentioned, with its connected chain and fine context, that it be abrogated by the verse we recited after it, judging between them was worthier, and holding to it likelier. And we found His saying the Exalted: And judge between them by what Allah sent down, bears that the intent be: And judge between them by what Allah sent down when they seek your judgment, and that it be upon the meaning: And judge between them by what Allah sent down by your standing upon what was between them obliging that judgment upon them, even if they did not seek your judgment. So we considered: has there been reported in that what indicates one of these two possibilities?"
"5284","4541 - And we found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakha'i narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abdullah ibn Murrah, from al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A Jew whose face was blackened and who was beaten was passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) being paraded, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is the affair of this one? So they said: He committed adultery. So he said: What do you find the penalty of adultery in your Book? They said: His face is blackened, he is punished and paraded. So he said: I adjure you by Allah, what do you find its penalty in your Book? So they pointed to a man of them, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked him. So the man said: We find in the Torah stoning, but it became frequent among our nobles, and we disliked to establish the penalty upon our lowly and leave our nobles, so we agreed upon something and placed this. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned him and said: I am the worthiest to revive what they killed of Allah the Exalted's command. And in this hadith was that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned that Jew with no seeking of judgment by the Jews to him therein. So that indicated that the worthier of the two possibilities for the verse we recited, agreeing with this hadith of them, and that the intent of His saying: And judge between them by what Allah sent down and do not follow their desires, is: And judge upon them with what was sent down upon you in the Book sent down upon you, after your knowledge of its obligatoriness upon whom it is judged upon, whether they sought judgment in that before you judge between them therein or did not seek your judgment therein. And from Allah we ask success."
"5285","711 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the penalties of the People of the Book in adultery: are they stoning? And is it remaining among them to the Day of Resurrection? Or has that been abrogated and returned to other than it? Abu Ja'far said: A speaker said: You reported in the chapter before this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his stoning the Jew he stoned for the adultery from him in the two hadiths of Jabir and al-Bara you reported therein, so why did those of the people of knowledge who abandoned them abandon them, among them Abu Hanifah, al-Thawri, Zufar, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, saying: The People of the Book are not stoned in adultery, though they stood upon these two reports and other than them of what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that? And they mentioned: 4542 - What Yunus had narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik ibn Anas informed him, from Nafi', from Abdullah ibn Umar, that he said: The Jews came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned to him that a man of them and a woman committed adultery. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: What do you find in the Torah regarding stoning? So they said: We disgrace them and they are flogged. So Abdullah ibn Salam said: You lied; in it is stoning. So they brought the Torah and spread it, and one of them placed his hand upon the verse of stoning, and recited what was before it and after it. So Abdullah ibn Salam said to him: Lift your hand. So he lifted his hand, and in it was the verse of stoning. They said: He spoke truth, O Muhammad, in it is the verse of stoning. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them stoned. Ibn Umar said: And I saw the man bending over the woman shielding her from the stones. Abu Ja'far said: Our answer to him in that was that for which they abandoned stoning the adulterers of the People of the Book, after standing upon these reports and upon what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein, is that the ruling was, in their view, regarding the Jews, meaning in the adultery penalty in the Torah, for whose sake the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned whom he stoned of the Jews mentioned therein, only of adultery especially, whether chastity was with it or not. And the proof with them of that was the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking the Jews: What do you find the adulterer's penalty in your Book? So they answered with what they answered him with of what was mentioned, meaning in the reports we mentioned in that. And there was not in his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking them mention of a chaste adulterer, nor mention of an unchaste adulterer. Likewise in their answering him about that was no mention of adultery of a chaste one, nor adultery of an unchaste one. So they inferred thereby that the punishment was in adultery in the Torah likewise. Then the punishment after that upon adulterers in Islam was unlike that, namely what Allah the Exalted mentioned in His Book with His saying the Exalted: And those of your women who commit indecency, bring four of you to witness against them; if they witness, confine them in houses until death takes them or Allah makes for them a way. So that was the penalty of adulteresses. Then He the Mighty and Majestic said: And the two of you who commit it, harm them; if they repent and reform, turn away from them. So that was the penalty of men therein, with no flogging nor stoning. Then Allah the Mighty and Majestic abrogated that upon His Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tongue with the way He had preceded in its mention with His saying in His Book: Or Allah makes for them a way. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said what Ubadah ibn al-Samit mentioned from him in that."
"5286","4543 - As Yunus had narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Hittan ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take from me, for Allah has made for them a way: the virgin is flogged and exiled, and the non-virgin is flogged and stoned."
"5287","4544 - And as Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, he said: And Mansur ibn Zadham informed us, from al-Hasan, he said: Hittan ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi narrated to us, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take from me, for Allah has made for them a way: the virgin with the virgin, flogging a hundred and exile a year, and the non-virgin with the non-virgin, flogging a hundred and stoning with stones. So thereby he explained what the way Allah made therein is, and informed of the penalties of each class of virgins and non-virgins. Then Abdullah ibn Umar said, after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and after his knowledge of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoning whom he stoned in adultery of the People of the Book:"
"5288","What Abdullah ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam had narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: Whoever associates with Allah is not chaste. So thereby he informed that the People of the Book are not chaste, and when they are so they are not stoned in adultery. And he, may Allah be pleased with him, is trusted upon what he said. And since the People of the Book exited the chastity obliging stoning, after Allah the Exalted's إطلاق to His Prophet to judge between them by what Allah the Exalted sent down upon him, and not to follow their desires, and all people at first were unchaste until there be from them the causes obliging them chastity, so the adultery punishments become obligatory upon them when from them, namely flogging which is their penalty before being chaste, they were upon that too, not exiting from it until proof stands of their exit from it to what transfers their punishments in their adultery from flogging to stoning. And they agreed that the Muslim man becomes chaste with his Muslim wife after they are free, adult, having had intercourse with her while adult. So thereby became obligatory, by their consensus upon transferring the ruling of whoever was of this path from flogging to stoning when adultery was from him, and leaving other than him upon his first penalty which they agreed was his penalty in adultery until they likewise agree upon transferring him from that penalty to the stoning we mentioned. And in that is what indicates, in the affairs of the People of the Book, what its sayer said of the negation of stoning from them. And Malik ibn Anas entered this meaning, and Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam mentioned from him in his Small Compendium which he composed upon his saying, and we wrote it from one who narrated it to us from him, he said: When the Christian embraces Islam then commits adultery, having married in Christianity, he is not chaste until he has intercourse with his wife in Islam. Likewise the slave freed, having a wife, commits adultery, he is not chaste until he has intercourse with her after freeing, then commits adultery after that, then he is chaste. Likewise the slave girl freed, having a husband, she is not chaste until she commits adultery after her husband has intercourse with her, after freeing. So that indicated that his school was in chastity that what was from the Christian in his Christianity of marriage and intercourse does not make him chaste, for had it made him chaste in his Christianity, Islam occurring upon him would confirm it. And when that was not so, it indicated that of the causes of chastity by which stoning becomes obligatory in adultery is Islam. And in that is what indicates its sayer's adherence therein to what his opponent said thereof of what we mentioned from him. And with Allah is success."
"5289","712 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his stoning whom he stoned of the Jews: was that by the testimony of other than them of the Jews upon them? And what enters in that of accepting the testimony of the People of the Book some upon others, and of rejecting it. So if a speaker says: You reported the hadith of Ibn Umar that the Jews brought the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a man and woman of them who committed adultery, and their bringing them both indicates they brought them by their choice, and the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving asking them to bring four witnesses besides them of the Muslims to testify upon what they accused them both thereof, and in his leaving that is proof of his accepting the testimony of those Jews who brought them to him upon them thereby. Likewise in al-Bara's hadith, when the Jew with blackened head was passed upon him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they informed him of the adultery from him until they did that with him for it, and there is not therein acknowledgment from that Jew of what they mentioned from him thereof, nor his confirming them upon it; rather from them was their informing the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what was from him, and the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying at that: I am the worthiest to revive what they killed of Allah's command, then he stoned him. So in that is what indicates accepting their testimonies upon him thereby. Our answer to him in that was that the matter therein is as they mentioned therein. And this meaning has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clearer than these two hadiths. 4545 - As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Ju'fi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, Mujalid informed us, from Amir, from Jabir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a Jew and Jewess who committed adultery, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Jews: What prevents you from establishing the penalty upon them? So they said: We used to do when sovereignty was ours and among us; as for when our sovereignty is gone, we do not dare upon them. So they sought his judgment upon them. So he ruled upon them with stoning. And this meaning is what Shurayh and others held to, of the rightly guided caliphs; Umar, Uthman and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them."
"5290","As al-Husayn ibn Nasr had narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from a shaykh, from Yahya ibn Waththab, from Shurayh, that he would allow the testimony of the People of the Book some upon others. And among them is Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, with his knowledge, trustworthiness and place in Islam."
"5291","As Yazid ibn Sinan had narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, Amr ibn Maymun informed us that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz allowed the testimony of a Magian upon a Christian, and a Christian upon a Magian. And among them is al-Sha'bi."
"5292","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu al-Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, that he would allow the testimony of the Christian upon the Jew, and the Jew upon the Christian."
"5293","And as al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, that he would allow the testimony of the People of the Book some upon others. And among them is Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri."
"5294","As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: The testimony of the Christian and the Jew, some upon others, is permissible; but the testimony of the Jew upon the Christian, nor the Christian upon the Jew, is not permissible."
"5295","And as Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, Idris ibn Yahya narrated to me, from Bakr ibn Mudar, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, he said: He would say: The testimony of the Christian upon the Christian; and he held the testimony of the Jew upon the Christian, or the Christian upon the Jew, not permissible. And among them is Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari."
"5296","As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me that he heard Yahya ibn Sa'id saying ... then he mentioned the like of his hadith from Ibn Wahb, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, the same. Abu Ja'far said: And al-Layth ibn Sa'd held to that of their saying."
"5297","As Yunus had narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Layth saying that, meaning the like of what he mentioned, from Ibn Shihab and Yahya ibn Sa'id. Abu Ja'far said: And these are the imams of the cities and their jurists."
"5298","And I heard Yunus saying: I heard Ibn Wahb saying: Malik ibn Anas opposed his teachers in rejecting the testimony of Christians some upon others; Ibn Shihab, Yahya ibn Sa'id and Rabi'ah would allow it. Abu Ja'far said: And I heard Ahmad ibn Abi Imran saying: I heard Yahya ibn Aktham saying - mentioning this chapter - : I gathered therein the saying of a hundred jurists of the predecessors regarding accepting the testimonies of the People of the Book some upon others, and I found no disagreement therein from their likes to them in that except from Rabi'ah, for I found from him its acceptance, and found from him its rejection. And a speaker said: How is accepting their testimony permissible with the disbelief they are upon toward Allah the Mighty and Majestic? He said: And when our sinners with what is less than disbelief, their testimony is not accepted, the disbelievers of other than us are likelier that their testimony not be accepted. Our answer to him in that was that the disbelief the People of the Book are upon did not exit them from the state of guardianship of some over others in marrying their daughters, and in guardianship over their minors of those who are their fathers in selling for them and in buying for them, while the like of that is not permissible of our sinners in the like of those we mentioned of their children. And whoever was of our sinners, it is obligatory upon us to oppose him and leave confirming him upon what he is upon of his sin until we remove him from it to the obligatory upon him by the law he is of its folk. And the People of the Book are unlike that, since they are left upon the ruling of their law, not taken with leaving that, nor with removal from it to other than it. And when they in what we mentioned are so, they are unlike the sinners of us, and they are in the rest of what is in their law as we are in what our law obliges. And among those who held to this saying were Abu Hanifah, Ibn Abi Layla, al-Thawri, and the rest of the Kufans besides them, except what they differ in of their creeds when they differ; for Abu Hanifah would not observe that, and his companions followed him upon it, while Ibn Abi Layla and many of them would oppose them therein, and not accept the testimony of the folk of a creed among them upon others. And with Allah is success."
"5299","713 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the intent of Allah the Mighty and Majestic's saying: O you who believe, testimony between you when death approaches one of you, the verse, and in its ruling: is it remaining, or did abrogation reach it? 4546 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Salih ibn Abdullah al-Tirmidhi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Qasim, from Abd al-Malik ibn Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Tamim al-Dari and Adi ibn Badda' would frequent Makkah for trade, and a man of Banu Sahm went out and died in a land with no Muslim therein, and bequeathed to them his estate. So they delivered his estate to his folk, but withheld a silver cup inlaid with gold. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made them swear by Allah: We concealed not nor informed. Then the cup was recognized at Makkah with some folk of Makkah, and they said: We bought it from Adi and Tamim. So two men of the Sahmi's kin stood and swore by Allah: This is the Sahmi's cup, and our testimony is worthier than their testimony, and we transgressed not; indeed then we are of the wrongdoers. So he took the cup, and regarding them this verse was revealed. Abu Ja'far said: And this Muhammad ibn Abi al-Qasim is Kufan, trustworthy, known as al-Shanni, and there reported from him other than Ibn Abi Za'idah, among them Abu Usamah."
"5300","4547 - And al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibari narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn al-Husayn al-Urani narrated to us, Yahya ibn al-Muhallab Abu Kudaynah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it. Then a speaker said: This is a verse Allah sent down in His Book, and there was reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his judging with what He sent down upon him therein what I reported in this chapter. And there was reported from Ibn Abbas, and he is the one who reported this hadith, in his holding to it, and that it is, in his view, of what judging with what is therein stands, untouched by abrogation. And he mentioned:"
"5301","What Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi had narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us, al-Mughirah ibn Salamah al-Makhzumi narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Habib ibn Abi Amrah narrated to us, he said: I heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr saying: Ibn Abbas said regarding His saying the Mighty and Majestic: Or two others of other than you: Of other than the folk of Islam, of the disbelievers, when you find no Muslims."
"5302","And as Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid meaning Ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Habib ibn Abi Amrah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas: Or two others of other than you, he said: Of other than the Muslims, of the People of the Book. Abu Ja'far said: So this indicates that it was, in Ibn Abbas's view, firm, unabrogated. And there was reported from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari therein what indicates that his school therein was as Ibn Abbas's school."
"5303","As Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, al-Mughirah al-Azraq informed us, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi saying: Abu Musa al-Ash'ari judged at Daquqa with this verse: Or two others of other than you."
"5304","And as Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Harawi narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah informed us, from Amir, he said: A man of Banu Khath'am went out and died at Daquqa, and none witnessed his bequest but two Christian men of his folk, so he made them witness upon his bequest. So they came to Kufah, and Abu Musa al-Ash'ari made them swear after the afternoon prayer in Kufah's mosque by Allah, beside whom there is no god, that they betrayed not, substituted not, concealed not, and that it is his bequest. Then he allowed their testimony. Abu Ja'far said: So that indicated that it was, in his view, firm, unabrogated. And we know of no one of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disagreeing with them - meaning Ibn Abbas and Abu Musa - in that, and Allah knows best. Then the Followers therein were most of them upon the like of what they both were upon therein. So he mentioned what:"
"5305","Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Ahmad narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Hisham ibn Hubayrah wrote to Shurayh asking him about the testimony of polytheists upon Muslims? So he wrote to him that the testimony of polytheists upon Muslims is not permissible except in a bequest, and not permissible in a bequest except one be traveling."
"5306","And what al-Harawi had narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Shurayh, he said: The testimony of the Jew nor the Christian is not permissible except in travel, and not permissible in travel except in bequest. He said: So this Shurayh, and he is the judge of the rightly guided caliphs, his school therein too was that it is firm, unabrogated."
"5307","And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab: Or two others of other than you, he said: Of the People of the Book. So that indicated what what was before it indicated."
"5308","And what al-Harawi had narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah, regarding His saying the Mighty and Majestic: You detain them after prayer, he said: It is the afternoon prayer. He said: And Ma'mar said: Qatadah said the like of it."
"5309","And what Ahmad ibn Dawud had narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad, he said: I asked Abidah about it, and he said: Of other than the folk of the creed. So that too indicated the like of what what was before it indicated."
"5310","And what Ibn Abi Maryam had narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Warqa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, regarding His saying: Testimony between you when death approaches one of you, that the Muslim die, and two Muslims or two disbelievers attend his death, and none but two of them attend; then if his heirs are pleased with what they concealed thereof of his estate, so it is, and they swear: they are truthful; then if a stain is found, or confusion, or resemblance, the two nearest of the heirs swear, and deserve, and nullify the oaths of the two witnesses. So that indicated that it was, in Mujahid's view, as we mentioned it, upon what it was upon with others."
"5311","And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim; al-Mughirah said: And one who heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr informed me: Or two others of other than you, they both said: Of other than your religion."
"5312","And what Muhammad had narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated to us, Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, the like of it. So that indicated that it was, with Ibrahim, Sa'id ibn Jubayr and Ibn Sirin, likewise too. So our answer to him in that was that what he mentioned is as he mentioned it, and that the saying therein is the saying in these reports. And of the jurists of the cities, Ibn Abi Layla said it."
"5313","As Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Bishr ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Yusuf saying: And Ibn Abi Layla would say in that, and he mentioned the like of the saying we mentioned from those who preceded in this chapter. And al-Awza'i said it:"
"5314","As Muhammad ibn Sinan permitted to us, from Mahmud ibn Khalid, from Umar ibn Abd al-Wahid, he said: I heard al-Awza'i saying regarding a Muslim man who dies in a village with no Muslims therein, and bequeaths: They wash him and bury him, and their testimony is permissible, meaning upon his bequest. And al-Thawri said it too."
"5315","As al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jazari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Da'laji narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Khidash, from al-Mu'afa ibn Imran, he said: And he was asked - meaning al-Thawri - about the testimony of the protected folk in travel: is it acted upon today? So he mentioned from Sulayman, from Ibrahim, from Shurayh, he said: The testimony of the Jew nor the Christian is not permissible except in a bequest, and not permissible in a bequest except in travel. Sufyan said: Where no Muslim is found. It was said to Sufyan: Is it taken with? Or about that. So he said: Abu Musa acted with it. So if a speaker says: There has been reported from al-Hasan what opposes the sayings of those you mentioned."
"5316","So he mentioned what al-Harawi had narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Rawh narrated to us, Awf informed us, from al-Hasan, regarding His saying: Two just of you or two others of other than you, he said: Of other than the folk of your tribe, all of them of the folk of prayer. Do you not see Him saying: You detain them after prayer? So our answer to him in that was that we do not deny that the people of knowledge differed in that - and how do we deny they differed therein, when Abu Hanifah among his companions, Malik among his companions, and al-Shafi'i among his companions hold that it is unlike what it is upon of those we mentioned? So among them are those who hold that it is abrogated by His saying: And bring two just of you to witness. And this is of what is not decided upon the مخالف by the standing of proof upon him with abrogation of what Allah sent down in His Book, and His Messenger acted with, and those of his Companions who acted with it acted with. And it is not permissible to abrogate what was agreed upon its establishment except by the standing of proof with what obliges that therein. As for what we mentioned of what al-Hasan infers with of Allah's saying: You detain them after prayer, what indicates that they are of the folk of prayer, that is of what we have no proof therein; rather that is, with many of the people of knowledge, upon that he intended thereby the time all the folk of religions magnify, namely after the afternoon prayer, which they observe and fear the descent of punishment upon them at disobedience therein. And we mentioned in that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Three Allah shall not look to them on the Day of Resurrection nor purify them, and for them is painful punishment: a man who swore after the afternoon upon goods that he was given for them so-and-so, lying. And we mentioned that with its chain in what preceded of us in this book of ours. So when this difference in this was as we mentioned, the ruling of the verse remained upon what it was upon until there be the like of what obliges its abrogation. And al-Zuhri and Zayd ibn Aslam would hold that it is of what acting with it was abrogated."
"5317","As Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned in the difference of those he mentioned of the jurists of Madinah therein what he mentioned therein, and said after that: And the testimony of a disbeliever upon any of the Muslims is not permissible in any of properties, in residence nor travel."
"5318","And as Yunus had narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Abdullah ibn Ayyash informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, regarding this verse: Testimony between you when death approaches one of you, the verse, he said: That was in a man who died with none of the folk of Islam with him, and that was at the beginning of Islam when the land was war, and people disbelievers except the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions at Madinah. And people would inherit by bequest, then bequest was abrogated and the shares obligated, and the Muslims acted with them. Abu Ja'far said: And in this to now is nothing obliging the abrogation of this verse. And Allah is the Granter of rightness."
"5319","714 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The adulterer marries none but one flogged like him. 4548 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Habib al-Mu'allim, Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to me, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The adulterer marries none but one flogged like him. Thus Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us this hadith. And that was, in our view - and Allah knows best - upon the flogged in the adulterer persisting after flogging upon the adultery for which he was flogged, not upon abandoning it, nor withdrawing from it; for his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) describing him with flogging was mentioned for him in a state he is therein blameworthy, for flogging in adultery is therein expiation for the flogged, and his blame with that repels that that flogging was expiation for him when persisting upon what obliges upon him its like. Then we considered: has this hadith been reported with other than these wordings?"
"5320","4549 - And we found Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Azhar ibn Marwan al-Raqashi informed us, from Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id, he said: Habib al-Mu'allim informed us, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The adulterer is flogged, thus he said, but it is: The flogged adulterer marries none but his like. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith was the intent in mentioning the marrier and married both with flogging, not with the adultery for which they were flogged. So that was understood that what was intended with what each of them was mentioned with is the adultery for which he was flogged, not the flogging itself for which he was flogged. Then we considered: did other than Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id report this hadith with a meaning differing therein from Abd al-Warith of what we reported from him upon it?"
"5321","4550 - And we found Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Harb had narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ash'ath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam al-Ijli narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Habib al-Mu'allim narrated to us, he said: I said to Amr ibn Shu'ayb: So-and-so says: The adulterer marries none but an adulteress like him. He said: And what pleases you of that? Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The adulterer marries none but an adulteress like him, and the flogged marries none but one flogged like him. And in this hadith was an addition over what is in the first two hadiths, namely: The adulterer marries none but an adulteress. So that was upon the two adulterers persisting upon blameworthy states, meaning that one of them marries his fellow only for the blameworthy states he agrees with him upon. And in it is that the flogged marries none but one flogged upon that meaning. And that was, in our view - and Allah knows best - upon one flogged in adultery he is persisting upon, one flogged in adultery she is persisting upon, not upon two adulterers each flogged in his adultery a flogging Allah the Mighty and Majestic made expiation for him, since he withdrew from that adultery for which he was flogged that flogging and repented to Allah from it. And we found a hadith reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein mention of something which may be what was mentioned in these hadiths is intended for what was mentioned therein to it, and it is:"
"5322","4551 - What Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah had narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to me, Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, from al-Hadrami - Abu Ja'far said: And he is Ibn Lahiq - from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that a woman called Umm Mahzul, and she would be at Ajyad, and stipulate for the man who marries her that she suffice him maintenance, and that a man of the Muslims sought the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permission regarding her, and he recited this verse, or this verse was revealed: And the adulteress none marries but an adulterer or polytheist."
"5323","And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, Hushaym informed us, from al-Taymi, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad - and he did not mention between them al-Hadrami - from Abdullah ibn Umar, and he did not say Ibn Amr, he said: There were known prostitute women; a man would marry a woman of them so she spend upon him, among them Umm Mahzul."
"5324","4552 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn al-Akhnas, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that a man called Marthad ibn Abi Marthad said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Shall I marry Anaq? For a prostitute at Makkah. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was silent to me until this verse was revealed: The adulterer marries none but an adulteress or polytheist woman, and the adulteress none marries but an adulterer or polytheist. So he said: O Marthad. So I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. So he recited to me this verse and said: Do not marry her. So it is possible that what is in the first reports is the one who marries the woman for this meaning which إطلاقs her deed to her, to reach of what she earns of that deed to what conveys to him of spending upon him and sufficing him burden in himself and in her. And whoever was so, was a doer of what becomes a cause for adultery, and blame for him upon that is of no obscurity. Then a speaker said: Is it permissible to be named with what he is named with in the first hadith of the adultery he was named with therein, and that إطلاق upon him, though adultery was not from him? Our answer to him in that was that it is permissible to إطلاق upon him this name when he became a cause for his إطلاقing it to one who does it, and his permitting it to him that, as was reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5325","4553 - Of what Ali ibn Ma'bad had narrated to us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Thabit ibn Umarah narrated to us, he said: I heard Ghunaym ibn Qays saying: I heard Abu Musa al-Ash'ari narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Any woman who perfumes and passes by people for them to find her scent, she is an adulteress, and every eye is adulterous. And in this hadith was the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) إطلاق adultery upon her, though from her was the cause from which adultery is. So the like of that - and Allah knows best - was his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) إطلاق adultery upon whom he إطلاقd it upon in the first reports, for his doing what becomes a cause for the adultery he إطلاقd upon him. So became clear, praise be to Allah and His favor, the meaning we carried the first reports we mentioned in this chapter upon, with this second report we mentioned therein. And Allah knows best."
"5326","715 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the suckling by which inviolability becomes obligatory: does it have a known number or not? 4554 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): One suck nor two sucks of suckling make unlawful."
"5327","4555 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Zur'ah Wahbullah ibn Rashid narrated to us, Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: One suck of suckling nor two sucks make unlawful. Abu Ja'far said: So al-Layth and Wahbullah ibn Rashid differed upon Yunus in the chain of this hadith; each of them said therein from him what we mentioned from him therein. Then we considered the reality of this hadith's chain, how it is?"
"5328","4556 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Ayyub informed me, Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from A'ishah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One suck nor two sucks make unlawful. So thereby we stood upon that this hadith returned to A'ishah, either from Urwah from her, or from Ibn al-Zubayr from her. Then we considered: did other than al-Zuhri report this hadith from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr or not?"
"5329","4557 - And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah informed us, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One suckling nor two sucklings make unlawful."
"5330","4558 - And we found Yahya ibn Uthman had narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Abbad ibn Abbad al-Muhallabi narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"5331","4559 - And we found Yusuf ibn Yazid had narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"5332","4560 - And we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki had narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Ibn al-Zubayr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So this hadith of Hisham ibn Urwah from Urwah was reported from him by many of his companions from whom his knowledge is taken, of what we mentioned from them from him, and they returned it to Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, not to A'ishah. Except that we found of his companions one man who opposed all who reported it from him therein, mentioning from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr from al-Zubayr:"
"5333","4561 - As Yazid ibn Sinan had narrated to us, Abu Kamil Fudayl ibn al-Husayn al-Jahdari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Dinar Abu Bakr al-Ala'i narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: One suck or two sucks, or one sip or two sips, do not make unlawful. Abu Ja'far said: And since this hadith turned only upon Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, either from A'ishah, or from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, then we found it from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, after his standing upon what was with him therein, either from A'ishah, or from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, he said in the suckling that makes unlawful what contradicts what is in these reports."
"5334","As Yunus had narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, that he asked Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab about suckling, and he said: What was in the two years, even a single drop, it makes unlawful; and what was after the two years, it is only food he eats. Ibrahim ibn Uqbah said: Then I asked Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, and he said as Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab said. Abu Ja'far said: So thereby we understood that Urwah, with his eminence and place in knowledge, did not leave what was therein with him from A'ishah, or from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to what contradicts it, except its abrogation was established with him; for had the matter not been so, his probity would fall thereby, and when his probity falls, his narration falls - and far be Allah the Mighty and Majestic from that being so - and that what he said thereof of what he gave Ibrahim ibn Uqbah fatwa with was only with what is worthier with him than what contradicts that of what A'ishah or Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to him, of what abrogates it. So if a speaker says: And what abrogated that? It is said to him: What we reported in other than this place from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Amrah, from A'ishah."
"5335","4561M - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, al-Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Amrah, that A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Of what was sent down of the Quran then fell: that nothing of suckling makes unlawful but ten sucklings, then there came down after: or five sucklings. So it is possible that the falling of that of the Quran is a falling of it of rulings, and Urwah's leaving observing the five is for the establishment of that falling with them of rulings. Then a speaker said: There has been reported from other than A'ishah and Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr in this chapter what agrees with what was reported from them therein, of negating unlawfulness with one sip and two sips."
"5336","4562 - And he mentioned what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Qatadah informed us, from Abu al-Khalil, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Umm al-Fadl, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One sip nor two sips make unlawful."
"5337","4563 - And what Ahmad ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Khalil, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Umm al-Fadl, that a man married a woman while he had another wife, and came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My first wife claims she suckled my other wife. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One sip nor two sips make unlawful."
"5338","4564 - And what Salih ibn Shu'ayb ibn Aban al-Basri had narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"5339","4565 - And what Yahya ibn Uthman had narrated to us, Bakr ibn Khalaf narrated to us, al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Ayyub mentioning from Abu al-Khalil, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Umm al-Fadl, that she said: A Bedouin entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in my house, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I had a wife then married another upon her, and my first wife claims she suckled my other wife one suckling or two. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One sip nor two sips make unlawful. He said: So in this hadith too is as in Urwah ibn al-Zubayr's hadith. Our answer to him in that was that whoever knows something is worthier than whoever falls short of it. So Urwah was of those who stood of this ruling's reality upon what he stood upon of what we mentioned from him, and the reporters of this hadith fell short of that. And what Urwah stood upon thereof is a proof upon this hadith's reporters, obliging leaving what went upon them to what Urwah reported of what we mentioned from him. Then a speaker said: In A'ishah's hadith is what opposed what you reported from al-Qasim, from Amrah, from her."
"5340","4566 - And he mentioned what Yunus had narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah daughter of Abd al-Rahman, from A'ishah, that she said: Of what was sent down of the Quran: ten known sucklings make unlawful, then they were abrogated with: five known sucklings. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while it is of what is recited of the Quran. He said: So in this hadith is what indicates the remaining of the five's ruling. Our answer to him in that was that al-Qasim ibn Muhammad in memory and precision is above Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, especially as Yahya ibn Sa'id agreed with him upon what he reported thereof. And he is above Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr too."
"5341","4567 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from A'ishah, she said: There came down of the Quran: Nothing makes unlawful but ten sucklings, then there came down after that: or five sucklings."
"5342","4568 - And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from A'ishah, that she said: There was sent down in the Quran ten known sucklings, then there was sent down: five known. And al-Qasim and Yahya are worthier in memory than Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, given their high rank in knowledge, and because two are worthier in memory than one, had he equaled one of them - so how when he falls short of each of them - besides that his hadith is impossible! For had what he reported been as he reported, it would have to join the Quran, and be recited with it in prayers as the rest of the Quran is recited therein, and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have left some of the Quran unwritten in their codices - far be Allah from that being so - or some of the Quran besides what the rightly guided ones gathered would remain. And for had that been so, what they wrote might be abrogated, and what they fell short of abrogating, so the obligation of acting would rise - and we seek refuge with Allah from this saying and its sayers. Then the eminent of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were in unlawfulness with little suckling and much thereof upon what we mentioned, among them Ali ibn Abi Talib, Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, Ibn Abbas, and Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them."
"5343","As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Qatadah informed us, he said: He wrote to Ibrahim al-Nakha'i asking him about one suckling and two, and he wrote: Abu al-Sha'tha narrated to them that Ali and Ibn Mas'ud said: Little of suckling and much thereof makes unlawful."
"5344","And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Bazi' informed us, he said: Yazid meaning Ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Sa'id meaning Ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: We wrote to Ibrahim ibn Yazid al-Nakha'i asking him about suckling, and he wrote: Shurayh narrated to him that Ali and Ibn Mas'ud would say: Little of suckling and much thereof makes unlawful."
"5345","And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad informed us, from Qays, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Little of suckling and much thereof makes unlawful."
"5346","And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Ibn Umar was asked about one suck and two, and he said: It is not proper. So it was said to him: Ibn al-Zubayr sees no harm in it. So he said: Allah says: And your sisters of suckling. So Allah's decree is worthier than Ibn al-Zubayr's decree. Then the jurists of the cities too are upon this saying, of Madinah's folk and Kufah's folk, except who exited from them to clinging to these reports. And that is indicated of what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5347","4569 - What Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Kufi had narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, he said: Uqbah ibn al-Harith narrated to me that he married a woman, and she was brought to him, and a black woman entered upon them both and said: I suckled you both. So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I married a woman, and she was brought to me, and a black woman entered upon us and said: I suckled you both. So he turned away from me. So I came to him from the other side and said: O Messenger of Allah, I married a woman, and she was brought to me, and a black woman entered upon us and said: I suckled you both, and she is lying. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How, while she claims she suckled you both! Leave her from you. Abu Ja'far said: Thus Isma'il narrated to us."
"5348","4570 - And al-Rabi' al-Muradi had narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Uqbah ibn al-Harith; Ibn Abi Mulaykah said: I heard the hadith from Uqbah, and a companion of mine narrated it to me from him, and I am of my companion's hadith more preserving. He said: Uqbah said: I married Umm Yahya daughter of Abi Ihab, and a black woman entered upon us and claimed she suckled us all. So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he turned away from me. I said: O Messenger of Allah, she is lying. He said: And what makes you know? How with her when she said what she said - leave her from you. Then we considered in this hadith: can we know the man Ibn Abi Mulaykah mentioned from him from Uqbah or not?"
"5349","4571 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hujr informed us, Isma'il ibn Ulayyah informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, he said: Ubayd ibn Abi Maryam narrated to me, from Uqbah ibn al-Harith, he said: And I heard it from Uqbah, but I am of Ubayd's hadith more preserving. He said: I married a woman, and a black woman came to us; then he mentioned this hadith. So thereby we stood upon that the man who narrated to Ibn Abi Mulaykah this hadith from him, after his hearing it from Uqbah, is Ubayd ibn Abi Maryam. Then we considered: did other than those we mentioned report it from Ibn Abi Mulaykah?"
"5350","4572 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me, Uqbah ibn al-Harith narrated to me, or I heard him narrating to the folk, he said: I married the daughter of Abi Ihab, and a black slave girl came and claimed she suckled me. So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, and he turned away from me - thus Ibrahim dictated to us, but it is: she claimed she suckled us, or she suckled me and her - and he turned away from me. Then I asked him, and he turned away from me. Then he said: How with you when that was said! And he forbade me from her."
"5351","4573 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus informed us, Umar ibn Sa'id ibn Abi al-Husayn narrated to me, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Uqbah ibn al-Harith, that he married the daughter of Abi Ihab, and a freedwoman of his of Makkah's folk came in the morning of her ownership, and said: I suckled you both. So I asked the girl's folk, and they denied that. So I rode to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at Madinah, and mentioned that. So I said: O Messenger of Allah, I asked the girl's folk and they denied that. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How when it was said! So I divorced her and married other than her."
"5352","4574 - And we found Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Umar ibn Abi al-Husayn, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Uqbah ibn al-Harith, that a black woman came and claimed she suckled them both. So they mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he turned away and smiled, and said: And how when it was said! And under him was the wife of Abi Ihab al-Tamimi."
"5353","4575 - And we found Fahd, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Uqbah, with the like of it, except that he added therein that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade him. Abu Ja'far said: And in this hadith was the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving uncovering the number of the suckling that black woman mentioned she suckled Uqbah and the woman he married. And in that is what indicates the equality of its little and much in unlawfulness, for had it been of his law that one suckling and two to the number mentioned in that hadith we reported do not make unlawful, it would be impossible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) order the one who asked him to separate from whom the woman who mentioned to him that she suckled them both suckled him and the woman he married, with a suckling not preventing his marrying her. Rather Uqbah would stand, and he would say to him: Ask her about the number of the suckling she suckled you both - how much is it? To stand thereby upon that it is of the suckling making it unlawful for him to marry her, had it in reality been so, and to be cautious of that had doubt been therein, or that it is of the suckling not making his marrying her unlawful, so he leaves him and that marriage. And in his leaving so is what indicates that there was no difference with him between little suckling and much thereof in unlawfulness. And from Allah we ask success."
"5354","716 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding what Allah loves of pride. 4576 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi of Taym Quraysh, he said: Ibn Jabir ibn Atik narrated to me, from his father, and he was of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Of pride is what Allah the Mighty and Majestic loves, and of it what He hates. As for the pride Allah loves, it is a man's strutting with himself at charity and at fighting. And the pride Allah the Mighty and Majestic hates is in wrongdoing and boasting. Abu Ja'far said: So we pondered this hadith, and we found in it that the pride Allah loves is a man's strutting with himself at charity and at fighting. So his strutting with himself at fighting was understood what is intended by it, and that it is of what frightens thereby his enemy present for his fighting, and of what increases his power over him and little heed of him. And since that was so in pride at fighting, the like of it was pride at charity; for the giver of charity is opposed by Satan, who casts in his heart the decrease of his property with the charity he attempts, and frightens him with poverty if it was from him, as He the Exalted said: Satan promises you poverty and orders you indecency, while Allah promises you forgiveness from Him and bounty. And when he strutted at his charity to show thereby his devil little heed in what he casts in his heart of what prevents him thereby of charity, that becomes of what belittles his devil in himself, and of what its owner's wealth's doer intends with what he does therein of what is neared with to Allah the Mighty and Majestic, overpowering him therein. So that was from him in charity the counterpart of what is from the fighter in the strutting we mentioned therein with him, and his praise upon that is as the praise of the strutter at fighting in his strutting. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"5355","717 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding what the people of knowledge differed in about the slain found among a folk with none known who killed him: is his blood money thereby obligatory upon them or not? 4577 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abu Layla ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, that men of his folk's elders informed him that Abdullah ibn Sahl and Muhayyisah went out to Khaybar of hardship befalling them. So Muhayyisah came and informed that Abdullah ibn Sahl was killed and cast in a well or spring. So he came to the Jews and said: By Allah, you killed him. So they said: By Allah, we killed him not. So he came until he arrived upon his folk and mentioned that to them. Then he came, he and his brother Huwayyisah, and he is older than him, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl. So Muhayyisah went to speak, and he is the one who was at Khaybar. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Muhayyisah: Let the elder, let the elder, meaning age. So Huwayyisah spoke first, then Muhayyisah spoke. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Either they pay your companion's blood, or they announce war. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to them in that, and they wrote: By Allah, we killed him not. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Huwayyisah, Muhayyisah and Abd al-Rahman: Do you swear and deserve your companion's blood? They said: No. He said: Then do the Jews swear for you? They said: They are not Muslims. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid his blood from himself, sending to them a hundred until admitted upon them the house. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar, when they mentioned their finding their companion slain at Khaybar, and it is the Jews' abode: Either they - meaning the Jews - pay your companion's blood, or they announce war, before there was from the Ansar with him a claim upon what they claimed, and this threat is only in those's withholding an obligatory upon them, namely paying the blood money of the Ansari found slain among them to his kin. And this is a chapter of jurisprudence its folk disputed in: a group of them said: Finding the slain among a folk obliges his blood money upon those folk, even if the kin of that slain do not swear upon what they claimed of the side the slain was found among, and of those they mentioned that they know not who killed him of those folk nor of other than them. And among those who held to this saying were many of the Kufans, among them Abu Hanifah, Ibn Abi Layla, and al-Thawri. And a group of them say: Qasamah and the obligatory with it are not obligatory by finding the slain among a folk; rather with them it is obligatory by one of two matters: a man says: My blood is with so-and-so, then dies, or the kin of a man claim upon a man that he killed a man and bring a doubt from his house, even if not decisive upon what they claim. So this with them is what obliges qasamah, and other than that does not oblige it. And among those who held to that of them was Malik ibn Anas. And a group of them say: Qasamah is not obligatory, nor is the blood money of a slain found among a folk obligatory with it, until there be the like of the cause in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed with qasamah, namely that Khaybar was the Jews' abode with none mixing them, and enmity between them and the Ansar was apparent, and Abdullah went out after the afternoon and was found slain before night, so it almost overcomes whoever hears this that none killed him but some Jews. So when the like of this is, making therein qasamah and the blood money's obligatoriness is not far. Likewise enter a group a house in a village or desert alone, or two rows in war, and they separate not but a slain among them, or upright proof of the Muslims comes from directions they did not gather therein, and each separately establishes upon a man that he killed him, and their testimonies agree, none hearing another's testimony, even if not of those declared just, or a just one testifies that he killed him - for every cause of this overcomes the judge's mind that it is as that slain's kin claimed. So the kin may swear upon the one and the group of who could be in their total, and qasamah with him, nor the blood money's obligatoriness with it, is except with what we mentioned. And among those who held to that was al-Shafi'i. And when they differed in that, uncovering it became obligatory, and the obligatory analogy therein with what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in its like. So we considered in that."
"5356","4578 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rafi' narrated to us. 4579 - And we found Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Abd al-Salam had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us. Then they joined, and each of them said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: Qasamah was in pre-Islamic time, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirmed it in the Ansari found slain in the Jews' well. So the Ansar said: The Jews killed our companion. And from Abu Salamah and Sulayman ibn Yasar, from men of the Ansar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Jews, beginning with them: Fifty of you swear, and they refused. So he said to the Ansar: Swear and deserve. So they said: Do we swear upon the unseen, O Messenger of Allah? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made it blood money upon the Jews, and because he was found among them. Then a speaker said: This is a hadith disconnected in chain from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); for what al-Zuhri mentioned from Abu Salamah and Sulayman ibn Yasar is only from men of the Ansar, and they may be of those who accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and may be of those who did not accompany him. And we considered in that."
"5357","4580 - And we found Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, Ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and Sulayman ibn Yasar, freedman of Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from men of the Ansar of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirmed qasamah upon what it was upon in pre-Islamic time."
"5358","4581 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and Sulayman ibn Yasar informed me, from some folk of the Ansar of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that qasamah was in pre-Islamic time qasamah of blood, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirmed it upon what it was upon in pre-Islamic time. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed with it in some folk of the Ansar of Banu Harithah who claimed upon the Jews."
"5359","4582 - And we found Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani had narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to me, al-Zuhri narrated to me, then he mentioned the like of it in its chain the same. So we understood that these Ansar from whom Abu Salamah and Sulayman ibn Yasar took this hadith were of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And we stood upon that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the blood money of the Ansari found slain among the Jews upon the Jews, and that was obligatory by his being found slain among them before his kin swear upon the Jews: they killed him. And of what indicates that too is what was from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after him in their obliging the blood moneys of the slain found among a folk upon the folk among whom the slain were found, by their being found among them likewise, even if there was not in that qasamah of their kin:"
"5360","As Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam had narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that a man was struck near the House, and Umar asked Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Ali said to him: His blood money is of the treasury. Abu Ja'far said: And this is of what there is no qasamah in, which Ali pointed to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, with, and to what Umar saw therein. And in it is what indicates that they did not order, before obliging blood money, that slain's kin to swear until they deserve blood money. And this in the presence of others of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who denied that not upon them, nor opposed them therein. So that indicated their following them upon it."
"5361","And as Fahd had narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Wahb ibn Uqbah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Madhkur al-Hamdani narrated to me that people crowded in the grand mosque in the time of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, and an elder died in the crowding. So Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered his blood money of the treasury."
"5362","And as Fahd had narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, Muslim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Madhkur, the like of it."
"5363","And as Yusuf ibn Yazid had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, Wahb ibn Uqbah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Madhkur, that an elder was crowded in the mosque in Ali's, may Allah be pleased with him, time, and died. So that was raised to Ali, and he paid his blood from the treasury. And Yazid at that time saw Ali and spoke to him. Abu Ja'far said: And of Umar in confirming this meaning was his stipulating it upon the protected folk."
"5364","As Fahd had narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, from Hisham al-Dastuwai, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, stipulated upon the protected folk: If a man of the Muslims is killed in your land, upon you is blood money. Then was from him what agrees with this meaning, and of what he judged therein with qasamah, with his obliging blood money upon those among whom the slain in whom that qasamah was found, with blood money upon the swearers among whom that slain was found."
"5365","As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Uthman ibn Matar narrated to us, from Abu Hariz, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Harith al-Wadi'i, he said: They found a slain between two villages, and wrote in that to Umar ibn al-Khattab. So Umar wrote: Measure between the two villages; whichever was nearer to him, take fifty qasamahs, and they swear by Allah, then make them pay blood money. Al-Harith said: So I was of those who swore, then we paid blood money."
"5366","And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn al-Azma' who said: A slain man was found between Wadi'ah and another clan, and the corpse was closer to Wadi'ah. So Umar said to Wadi'ah: Let fifty men among you swear by Allah: we did not kill him, nor do we know his killer, then pay the blood-money. Al-Harith said to him: We swear and we still pay? He said: Yes. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith from Umar is that he judged with qasamah upon those among whom the slain man was found, then imposed the diyah upon them for his heirs. And in what we reported from him before, which Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, agreed with him upon, is his judging with diyah for a slain man found among people among whom qasamah is not possible, because they are not residents of that place where the slain man was found, but are from different places. So likewise we say regarding a slain man found among a people whose killer is unknown: in it are qasamah and diyah; and regarding a slain man found in a place that has no inhabitants, whose killer is unknown, there is diyah without qasamah. Thus Abu Hanifah and his companions used to say in this matter. Strengthening what they said is what we reported in the hadith of Abu Layla from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar about the Jews: Either they pay the blood-money for your companion, or they declare war, before there was any qasamah from the Ansar in that, and their declaration of war would only be for withholding what was obligatory upon them. An objector said: In the hadith of Abu Salamah and Sulayman, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar: Swear and establish your right. They said: Should we swear about the unseen? Our answer to him in that was that his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): establish your right, may mean: establish your right with evidence you bring against the specific killer of your companion, and we will execute him for you for it. This objector said: In the hadith of Abu Layla, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar: Will you swear and establish your right? In this is what indicates they only establish what they claimed by the oath he called them to. Our answer to him in that was: At the beginning of this hadith is what indicates the obligation of diyah for them upon the Jews by their finding their companion slain among them. If he says: Then what is his saying: Will you swear and establish your right? Our answer to him in that is that this is from what was criticized against Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, and he was opposed in it, and confusion on his part in it was claimed."
"5367","4583 - As Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us: Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Abd Allah al-Bakka'i narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Bujayd ibn Qayzi, brother of Banu Harithah - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim said: By Allah, Sahl was not more knowledgeable than him, but he was older than him - that he said to him: By Allah, the matter was not like this, but Sahl confused what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. He did not say: Swear about what you have no knowledge of. Rather, when the Ansar spoke to him, he wrote to the Jews of Khaybar: A slain man has been found among your dwellings, so pay his blood-money. They wrote to him, swearing by Allah they did not kill him, nor do they know his killer. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid his blood-money from his own funds. Abu Ja'far said: This Abd al-Rahman ibn Bujayd - his standing is the standing Muhammad ibn Ibrahim mentioned of him, and he described him with knowledge by which he surpassed the knowledge of Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah. Eminent scholars narrated from him, among them Zayd ibn Aslam."
"5368","4584 - As Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Bujayd al-Ansari, from his grandmother, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Send back the beggar, even with a burnt hoof."
"5369","4585 - As al-Rabi' al-Muradi and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us; al-Rabi' said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to me, and Muhammad said: My father and Shu'ayb informed us, from al-Layth, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Bujayd, brother of Banu Harithah, that his grandmother, Umm Bujayd - who was among those who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - narrated to him that she said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, the needy man stands at my door, and I find nothing to give him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: If you find nothing to give him except a burnt hoof, then place it in his hand. Abd al-Rahman ibn Bujayd was followed in what we mentioned from him by Amr ibn Shu'ayb."
"5370","4586 - As Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us: Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ziyad narrated to me, he said: Ibn Ishaq said: And Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to me ... like the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn Bujayd, except that he said in his hadith: Pay his blood-money, otherwise declare war. So they wrote back, swearing by Allah they did not kill him, nor do they know his killer. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid his blood-money from his own funds. That is more fitting for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it is the assumption about him that he would not command anyone to swear about what he has no knowledge of. If an objector says: But you said: If a twenty-year-old raised in the East buys a hundred-year-old slave raised in the West, and sells him immediately, and the buyer finds a defect in him, that the seller swears decisively: He sold him to him without this defect, though he has no knowledge of it. What we said is that his knowledge of what we described is sound. Our answer to him in that was that what he mentioned about swearing over a defect, as he mentioned it, is like swearing over what the disagreement we mentioned occurred about; for swearing over a defect is swearing to deny something broad for the one sworn about when his oath is in it, and even if it is decisive, it returns to knowledge by which the contrary of what he swore is not known. Swearing to affirm things is different, for it does not befit a man to swear by Allah: Such-and-such indeed happened, of what he did not know. Allah forbade him that in speech, so how much more in an oath, by His saying, exalted be His grandeur: And do not pursue what you have no knowledge of. Then He informed him of accountability for that by His saying: Indeed the hearing, the sight, and the heart - all of those will be questioned about it. So He informed him, exalted is He, that testimony affirming things is what these three things know, and whoever goes beyond them to other than them, or falls short of them, opposes what Allah commanded him in them. And the one swearing the qasamah mentioned in the hadith of Sahl goes beyond what is in this verse to other than it, of what Allah forbade him. An objector said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Bujayd, even if his standing is what you mentioned, does not compare to Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, due to Sahl's companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Ibn Bujayd falling short of that. Our answer to him in that was: He (the opponent) said regarding the marriage of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Maymunah, after he reported the hadith of Amr ibn Dinar from Jabir ibn Zayd from Ibn Abbas that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her - she being his aunt - while in ihram. He said: The woman's family may know of her affair, though they did not attend it, due to their concern for her, what others who attended her affair do not know. And her freedman - meaning Sulayman ibn Yasar - and her nephew Yazid ibn al-Asamm reported that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her while not in ihram. So he made that a proof for the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) marrying her while not in ihram, and countered with it the hadith of Ibn Abbas, despite the eminence of Ibn Abbas and his companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Allah's pleasure with him, and his being with him in that Hajj. So why is it denied to his opponent that Abd al-Rahman ibn Bujayd, being from Banu Harithah, the people of the slain man, said what he said opposing Sahl in it, and his opponent's countering Sahl with Abd al-Rahman in that? And if he says: He only did that with what he has of what he reported from Uthman that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one in ihram does not marry nor give in marriage - he is countered by being told: Likewise your opponents countered Sahl with Abd al-Rahman ibn Bujayd, due to what the Ansari companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who are from the people of that slain man agreed with them upon of what they said, and due to our accepting what al-Zuhri reported from Abu Salamah and Sulayman ibn Yasar from men of the Ansar among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) being more deserving for us than accepting what Bushayr ibn Yasar reported - who is not like Abu Salamah nor Sulayman ibn Yasar - from Sahl. Especially with what there was from Umar and Ali of what we narrated from them in this chapter, of what necessitates the agreement of the people of this view with their view, and the rest of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving opposition to them in that. And Allah knows best."
"5371","718 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports on how qasamah was, as reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it. 4587 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, that he informed him that Abd Allah ibn Sahl al-Ansari and Muhayyisah ibn Mas'ud went out to Khaybar, and separated for their needs. Abd Allah ibn Sahl was killed. It reached Muhayyisah, so he came with his brother Huwayyisah and Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abd al-Rahman began to speak due to his place as the brother, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let the elder speak, let the elder speak. So Huwayyisah and Muhayyisah spoke, and mentioned the affair of Abd Allah ibn Sahl. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: You swear fifty oaths, and you establish your right to your killer or your companion. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we did not witness nor attend. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will the Jews then exonerate themselves to you with fifty oaths? They said: O Messenger of Allah, how do we accept the oaths of a disbelieving people? Malik said: Yahya ibn Sa'id said: Bushayr claimed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid his blood-money from his own funds. Thus Malik reported this hadith from Bushayr and did not go beyond him to anyone else, while others reported it and went beyond him to Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah."
"5372","4588 - As Yunus narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, who heard Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah who said: Abd Allah ibn Sahl was found slain in a well of the wells of Khaybar. His brother Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl and his uncles Muhayyisah and Huwayyisah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abd al-Rahman began to speak, so the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: The elder, the elder. One of his two uncles spoke, either Huwayyisah or Muhayyisah, the elder of them spoke. He said: O Messenger of Allah, we found Abd Allah ibn Sahl slain in a well of the wells of Khaybar, and he mentioned the enmity of the Jews toward them. He said: Will the Jews then exonerate themselves to you with fifty oaths: that they did not kill him? They said: How do we accept their oaths when they are polytheists? He said: Then fifty among you swear: that they killed him. They said: How do we swear about what we did not see? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid his blood-money from his own funds. In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placing the oaths of exoneration upon the Jews, and this contradicts what is in the hadith of Malik, except that most people reported it in agreement with Malik in it. Among those who reported it likewise is Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal."
"5373","4589 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah who said: Abd Allah ibn Sahl and Muhayyisah ibn Mas'ud ibn Zayd set out to Khaybar, when it was at truce that day. They separated for their needs. Muhayyisah came upon Abd Allah ibn Sahl wallowing in his blood, so he buried him, then came to Madinah. Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl, Huwayyisah and Muhayyisah went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the rest of the hadith of Malik. Some said: Thus is qasamah according to the hadith of Malik and Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal: the heirs of the blood begin in it. Others opposed them in that and said: Rather, those among whom the slain man was found begin in it, according to the hadith of Ibn Uyaynah, and in the hadith of Abu Salamah and Sulayman from men of the Ansar which we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began with the Jews, saying to them: Fifty among you swear. This contradicts the hadith of Bushayr, and it is more deserving than it due to the eminence of the narrators of this hadith over the narrators of the hadith of Yahya ibn Sa'id, besides that we found the hadith of Bushayr reported from him contrary to what Yahya ibn Sa'id reported from him."
"5374","4590 - As Fahd narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Ubayd al-Ta'i narrated to us, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, that a man of the Ansar called Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah informed him that a group of his people set out to Khaybar, and dispersed in it. They found one of them slain. They said to those among whom they found him: You killed our companion? They said: By Allah, we did not kill him, nor do we know his killer. They went to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Prophet of Allah, we went to Khaybar and found one of us slain. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The elder, the elder. He said to them: You bring evidence against who killed him. They said: We have no evidence. He said: Then they swear for you. They said: We do not accept the oaths of the Jews. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked that his blood be wasted, and paid his blood-money with a hundred camels of the charity. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed the oaths in this matter upon the Jews among whom the slain man was found, not upon the heirs of the slain man. This strengthened the hadith of Abu Salamah and Sulayman, upon what we reported of Umar's judgment against al-Harith ibn al-Azma' and his people. This, in our view, is among what opposition to it is not permitted."
"5375","719 - Chapter explaining the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the qasamah he judged against the Jews, placing the diyah upon them: are rulings after them likewise, with the diyah upon the residents of the place where the slain man is found even if they do not own it, or upon its owners? Abu Ja'far said: We reported in the hadith of Sahl that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar: Either they pay the blood-money for your companion, or they declare war. And in the hadith of Abu Salamah and Sulayman, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed the diyah of the slain man upon the Jews, though Khaybar belonged to the Muslims and the Jews were their workers in it. Abu Yusuf said: Thus I say: if a house has residents who do not own it, with owners absent from it, then qasamah and diyah are upon its resident, not upon its owners who do not live in it. Abu Hanifah, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, and many scholars besides them opposed him in that, placing qasamah and diyah in that upon the owners, not upon the non-owning residents. We examined what Abu Yusuf said in that and found he erred in it, for in the hadith of Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal is that it - meaning Khaybar - was at truce that day. This was strengthened by the hadith of Malik from Abu Layla from Sahl, in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Either they pay the blood-money for your companion, or they declare war - and that is only regarding a place belonging to them. Sulayman ibn Bilal agreed with Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, in what was reported that Khaybar was at truce that day. 4591 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, that Abd Allah ibn Sahl and Muhayyisah went out to Khaybar in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when it was at truce and its people were Jews. They separated for their needs. Abd Allah ibn Sahl was killed and found slain in a watering place. His companion buried him, then came to Madinah. The brother of the slain man, Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl, with Huwayyisah and Muhayyisah, came and told the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Abd Allah ibn Sahl and how he was killed. Bushayr ibn Yasar, narrating from those who met the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), claimed he said to them: You swear fifty oaths and establish your right to your slain man or your companion? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we did not witness nor attend. He said: Will the Jews then exonerate themselves to you with fifty oaths? They said: O Messenger of Allah, how do we accept the oaths of a disbelieving people? Bushayr claimed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid his blood-money. Abu Ja'far said: By the hadith of Bishr and Sulayman we understood Abu Yusuf's error in this hadith about the state Khaybar was in when that slain man was found in it - that it did not belong to the Muslims, but belonged to the Jews. And Allah knows best."
"5376","721 - Chapter explaining the problematic report about the diyah with which the Ansari was compensated: was it from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) himself, or from the charity camels, or from the Jews? Abu Ja'far said: We reported in the hadith of Abu Salamah and Sulayman, from men of the Ansar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his diyah upon the Jews, because he was found among them. So in this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged it against the Jews. And in the hadith of Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, other than the hadith of Sa'id ibn Ubayd, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid it from his own funds. It is possible he paid it from his own funds though he had made it obligatory upon another, paying it from where payment was not obligatory upon him - and that does not negate that he had already judged it against whom he judged it. And in the hadith of Sa'id ibn Ubayd, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) compensated the slain man with it from the charity camels. It is possible that the saying of whoever said: he paid it from his own funds, means: from what was under his disposal, though he did not own it, so that these hadiths and the hadith of Sahl do not contradict. And it is possible his payment of that from the charity camels was not as payment on behalf of the Jews, for they are not among the people of charity; rather so that the diyah of the slain man not be voided and his blood not wasted - he paid that from the charity camels for this reason, not that he paid on behalf of the Jews anything dropping what he had judged against them. In that is what indicates that whoever pays a debt owed by a man to its owner has not thereby given the debtor ownership of anything he paid on his behalf. Thus Muhammad ibn al-Hasan used to say in this, to the point he said about a man who marries a woman for a hundred dirhams, and a man pays her that hundred on his behalf, then her husband divorces her before consummation: The half of the dower she must return, she must return to the one who paid her the hundred, not to her husband. Muhammad reported no disagreement between him and any of his companions in that. An objector said: She returns it to the husband. The view with us in that is the first view, for the dirhams first left the ownership of the payer into the ownership of the woman, not into the ownership of the husband. This with us also indicates against what Malik ibn Anas said about one who pays a debt owed by a man, without his order, to its owner - that he reclaims that debt from the debtor, for he owned it by paying it on his behalf. We know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pay what he paid of the charity camels to have its like returned to him. We also mentioned among the proof against this view, in what preceded in this book of ours, what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he would not pray over one who left a debt for which he left no fulfillment, and that when Abu Qatadah guaranteed that on behalf of the deceased over whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not prayed, due to the debt upon him for which he left no fulfillment, he prayed over him. We thus understood that if the payer of a debt reclaimed it by paying it on his behalf, so that he could take the debtor for it, then the debt of that dead man would have returned to Abu Qatadah, he would not have been cleared of the debt, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have prayed over him. In his praying over him is what indicates the debt did not return to Abu Qatadah, nor did he own it. In this is clarification of what we described, and exposition of the ruling with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter. Then we found in this matter another hadith with other than what is in these reports we mentioned in this chapter, namely: 4592 - What Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Bahrani informed us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn al-Akhnas narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that Ibn Muhayyisah the younger was found slain at the gates of Khaybar. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring two witnesses against who killed him, and I will hand him over to you entirely. He said: O Messenger of Allah, whence do I get two witnesses? He was found slain at their gates. He said: Will you then swear fifty qasamah oaths? He said: O Messenger of Allah, how do I swear about what I do not know? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall we then have fifty qasamah oaths sworn among them? He said: O Messenger of Allah, how do we have them swear when they are disbelievers, or polytheists? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apportioned his diyah upon them, and aided them with part of it. In this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apportioned his diyah upon the Jews without any oath in that claim against them. In that is what indicates the diyah bound them by the slain man being found among them. And in it is, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his aiding them with half the diyah of the slain man. That, with us - and Allah knows best - is that it was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a payment on behalf of the Ansar, not on behalf of the Jews, for what he paid in that was from funds not lawful for the Jews. And success is from Allah."
"5377","722 - Chapter explaining the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the saying of Ayyub, the Prophet of Allah, peace be upon him: You know that I would pass by two men disputing, and they would mention Allah, exalted is He, so I would return to my house and make expiation on their behalf, out of dislike that they mention Allah except in truth. 4593 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us, Nafi' ibn Yazid informed me, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Prophet of Allah Ayyub, peace be upon him - his affliction lasted upon him eighteen years. Near and far rejected him, except two men of his brothers, among his closest brothers, who would come to him morning and evening. One of them said to his fellow: Do you know, by Allah, Ayyub has committed a sin no one in the worlds committed. His fellow said to him: What is that? He said: For eighteen years Allah has not had mercy on him to remove what is with him. When they went to him in the evening, the man could not restrain himself until he mentioned that to him. So Ayyub, Allah's blessings be upon him, said: I do not know what you say, except that Allah has seen me: I would pass by two men disputing, and they would mention Allah the Exalted, so I would return to my house and make expiation on their behalf, out of dislike that they mention Allah except in truth. He would go out for his need, and when he finished it, his wife would hold his hand until he reached. One day he was late for her, so Allah the Exalted revealed to Ayyub in his place: Strike with your foot; this is a cool bath and drink. She waited for him and came out looking, and he came to her with Allah, exalted be His grandeur, having removed his affliction, in the best state he had been. When she saw him she said: How blessed you are - have you seen this afflicted Prophet of Allah? By Allah, I never saw anyone resembling him when healthy more than you. He said: I am he. He had two threshing floors: one for wheat and one for barley. Allah the Exalted sent two clouds; when one was over the wheat floor it poured gold wheat into it until it overflowed, and the other poured silver into the barley floor until it overflowed."
"5378","4594 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Nafi' ibn Yazid narrated to us, Uqayl informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like, except that he said in place of disputing: contending with harsh words."
"5379","4595 - And Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, from Nafi', then he mentioned with his isnad the like."
"5380","4596 - Abu Ja'far said: So I asked Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud about this hadith, and said to him: Did anyone besides Nafi' ibn Yazid report it from Uqayl? He said: Yes; Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not mention Anas ibn Malik in it. Abu Ja'far said: We considered what is in this hadith of the saying of Ayyub, peace be upon him, to the man who said to him what he said: By Allah, I do not know what you say, except that I would pass by two men disputing and mentioning Allah, exalted is He, so I would return to my house and make expiation on their behalf, out of dislike that they mention Allah except in truth. It was impossible that what he, peace be upon him, did in that was expiation for an oath of theirs or of one of them, for it is not permitted for one to make expiation for another's oath after his violation of it, nor before his violation while he is alive. Rather it is with us - and Allah knows best - upon expiation for the speech in which Allah, exalted is He, was mentioned, of what was not fit for Him to be mentioned in. Then we returned to expiations for things: what are they? We saw they are covering of what was expiated for. Covering of things may be with annihilation of those things, like what people sow in their land, planting it and covering it with soil they throw upon it - so they are called kuffar for their covering it. From it is Allah's saying: Like rain whose growth pleases the kuffar - meaning the tillers, not the disbelievers in Allah the Exalted. Its growth is only after the perishing of what was sown in its place. And it may be with their remaining and appearing after that, like what was said, meaning: it covered its stars that had appeared. The best that occurred to us in interpreting what Ayyub, Allah's blessings be upon him, said of what is mentioned from him in this hadith: Since the address of those two men was what mixed the mention of Allah with what is not fit for His mention, exalted is He, in it, that was a sin that appeared. And what appears of sins and is not changed, Allah the Exalted punishes the special and the general for it."
"5381","4597 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Amr ibn Abi Razin narrated to us, Sayf ibn Abi Sulayman al-Makki narrated to us, from Adi ibn Adi - Abu Ja'far said: he is Ibn Umayrah - from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted does not destroy the general for the deed of the special, but if they see evil among them and do not change it, Allah the Exalted punishes the general and the special. Abu Ja'far said: Since what was from those two men returned to what the general are taken for, Ayyub remedied it with what repels Allah's punishment: charity that expiates sins and repels punishments, without those two men having expiation in that for themselves. That expiation covered that disobedience with a covering in which is its annihilation, though the two men who earned it did not enter into that. Like that is His saying to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And Allah would not punish them while you are among them, nor would Allah punish them while they seek forgiveness. So He informed him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that He lifts punishment from them, though they deserve it, by their seeking His forgiveness. That seeking forgiveness - and Allah knows best - is of what occurs in hearts that it was not from all of them, but from some of them; so punishment was lifted by it from those whose disobedience it was, and from those whose it was not. This is the best of the meanings that occurred to us which what we mentioned from Ayyub, peace be upon him, may bear. Allah knows best the reality in that. And Allah we ask for success."
"5382","723 - Chapter explaining the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his brothers: are they his companions, or others? 4598 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Salt Abu Ya'la narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ma'n narrated to us, Dawud ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, that Rabi'ah ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Hudayr informed him - and he used to accompany Talhah ibn Ubayd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him - he said: I never heard Talhah narrate from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except one hadith. We said: What is it? He said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey. When we overlooked Harrat Waqim, there were graves. We said: O Messenger of Allah, these are the graves of our brothers. He said: These are the graves of our companions. When he came to the graves of the martyrs, he said: These are the graves of our brothers."
"5383","4599 - And Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to the graveyard and said: Peace be upon you, abode of a believing people. And we, if Allah wills, will join you. I wish I had seen our brothers. They said: O Messenger of Allah, are we not your brothers? He said: Rather you are my companions, and my brothers are those who have not yet come. And I am their predecessor at the Hawd. We considered these two hadiths and found brotherhood is sincere friendship, in which there is no deceit, with no inner conflicting with its outer. From it is Allah's saying: The believers are but brothers - meaning because what is between them and what some of them hold for others, its outer does not conflict with its inner. And from it His saying: Forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith. Then from it is the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he commanded his ummah, saying: Do not envy one another, nor hate one another, nor turn away from one another; be servants of Allah as brothers. Companionship may be with an outer conflicting with the inner that is with its holders, while brotherhood is otherwise: free of this, its outer not conflicting with its inner, nor its inner with its outer. And success and protection are from Allah."
"5384","724 - Chapter explaining the problematic report on the meaning of Allah's saying to the Mothers of the Believers: And do not display yourselves as the display of the former ignorance. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us; and Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us; they said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us - the wording is Yahya ibn Uthman's - from Thawr, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, asked him: Have you considered Allah's saying: And do not display yourselves as the display of the former ignorance - was there more than one ignorance? Ibn Abbas said to him: I never heard of a first except it has a last. Umar said: Bring from Allah's Book what confirms that. Ibn Abbas said: Allah, exalted be His name, says: And strive in Allah as you strove the first time. Umar said: Whom did Allah command us to strive against? Ibn Abbas said: Makhzum and Abd Shams. We considered this hadith and Ibn Abbas's saying to Umar in it: I never heard of a first except it has a last, and Ibn Abbas's recitation to him after that of what he mentioned as from Allah's Book, which Umar did not deny being such, though we do not find it in Allah's Book. We found it reported about it that it was from Allah's Book, then dropped from it among what was dropped. As..."
"5385","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Ibn Abi Maryam informed us, Nafi' - meaning Ibn Umar - informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to me, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with them both: Did we not find in what Allah sent down upon us: Strive as you strove the first time - and we do not find it. He said: It was dropped among what was dropped of the Qur'an. Umar said: Do you fear people will return as disbelievers? He said: What Allah wills. He said: If people return as disbelievers, their rulers will be Banu so-and-so, and their ministers Banu so-and-so."
"5386","And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: Nafi' - meaning Ibn Umar - narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, then he mentioned the like."
"5387","And as Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Sa'd informed us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, he said: A trustworthy Qurashi man informed me, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, with the end of the hadith. He said: Umar said: When that happens, none but Banu Umayyah and Banu Makhzum will have any share of authority."
"5388","And as Yusuf narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, who said: Umar said to Abd al-Rahman, then he mentioned the like of his hadith from Ya'qub ibn Ishaq, from Nafi', from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, except that he said: Their rulers will be Banu Umayyah, and their ministers Banu al-Mughirah. By that we understood that what was recited in these reports as from Allah's Book was from Allah's Book, as recited in it, except that Umar and Ibn Abbas did not know it was dropped from it, until Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf informed them of that. Its dropping from Allah's Book does not prevent it being of eloquent speech that is the ultimate proof in language. By that we established that there may be a first for what has no last. Like that is what scholars said in its like about a man who says: The first slave I own is free - then owns a slave: he is freed for him, even if he owns no other after him until he dies. And their distinction between that and the last, where they make a last only for what had a first. From that is what they said about a man who says: The last slave I own is free - then owns a slave and owns no other slave until he dies: he is not freed, for he is not a last when he was a first. This is the best that occurred to us in interpreting what is in this hadith. It is reported from some early companions and others in interpreting that meaning other than this interpretation."
"5389","As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said about: And do not display yourselves as the display of the former ignorance: We used to say there will be another ignorance."
"5390","And as Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, Warqa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid about: And do not display yourselves as the display of the former ignorance. He said: It is the ignorance between Isa and Muhammad - Allah's blessings be upon them both. As for the linguists, among them al-Farra: we found he said in his book on the meanings of the Qur'an and its difficult grammar about: And do not display yourselves as the display of the former ignorance. He said: That was in the time in which Ibrahim, Allah's blessings be upon him, was born. The woman would wear a coat of pearls unstitched on both sides, and wear garments of wealth not covering her body. So they were commanded not to do that. These are interpretations reported for this meaning, plausible for what was said in them, and Allah knows best His intent in them. A supporter among those agreeing with us argued that there may be a first though it has no last, as from that is Allah's saying: And you have known the first creation - this indicates creation was first, though there is no other creation after it. Our answer to him in that was that that too was only sent down after creation had been, and from it Allah's saying: As He produced you from the offspring of other people. That preceded the sending down of the verse he mentioned as indicating what he said. And Allah is the granter of success."
"5391","725 - Chapter explaining the problematic report on the obligation in what scholars differed about: a man buys goods then becomes bankrupt, or dies owing debts - is its seller more entitled to it than his creditors or not? 4600 - al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad - meaning Ibn Amr ibn Hazm - that he informed him he heard Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz narrating, that he heard Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham narrating, that he heard Abu Hurayrah narrating, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever goes bankrupt with people's wealth, and a man finds his goods intact, he is more entitled to them."
"5392","4601 - And Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man who goes bankrupt, and a man finds his wealth intact, he is more entitled to it than others."
"5393","4602 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir and Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about a man when he goes bankrupt: and a man finds his goods intact, he is more entitled to them. And in Bishr's hadith: than the creditors."
"5394","4603 - And Ibrahim narrated to us: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Hazm, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith. Abu Ja'far said: Thus he said: from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that."
"5395","4604 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid al-Qattan and Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan al-Miqsami informed me - the wording is his - he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Abi Husayn informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm informed him that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to him from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about a man who becomes destitute: when the goods are found with him intact and recognized, they belong to its owner who sold them. Abu Ja'far said: We used to say about this hadith that the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it: and a man finds his wealth intact, may mean deposits, loans for use, and their likes, whose finder's ownership stands in them - not sold things which at that time do not belong to their finder, but are things that were his, whose ownership left him - as Abu Hanifah and his companions say in that. Some people among those holding the view of Malik and his followers in that used to argue against us in that."
"5396","4605 - As Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5397","We did not see that a proof against us in our opposing him which we mentioned, due to this hadith's disconnection, until 4606 - Hamid ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi Abu Ahmad narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Bishr ibn al-Hakam al-Naysaburi - he was among the scholars and trustworthy of Naysabur - he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us: Malik informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). By that this hadith strengthened in our hearts when its isnad became connected for us, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as we mentioned. Before that, some people had argued against us in this chapter with what..."
"5398","4607 - Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Khaba'iri narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Any man who sells goods and finds his goods intact with a man who went bankrupt, having received nothing of its price, they are his. If he had paid him something of its price, what remains, he is equal with the creditors. We did not see that a proof against us due to the corruption of Isma'il ibn Ayyash's narration from non-Syrians. Then we found it from Isma'il's narration from Syrians, about whose narration from them no criticism is made of Isma'il."
"5399","4608 - As Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of his hadith before this, adding in it: And any person who perishes having another's wealth intact with him - whether he took something of it or not - he is equal with the creditors. We could not then oppose this hadith, for whoever it reaches and stands upon it through these acceptable channels, opposing it is not permitted. We returned in these reported meanings in it to what Malik used to say in them. We excused whoever opposed them in opposing them: it was only from him because they did not reach him with this connection. Had they reached him with this connection and stood with him as they stood with us, he would not have opposed them but returned to them and held them - as we saw him do in their likes. As for al-Shafi'i, he used to say: When he goes bankrupt after the seller received part of the price for which he bought those goods, he is equal with the creditors in the share of what he received, and more entitled than them to the rest of them. What is in the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repels that and opposes it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is Allah's proof over all His creation. He also, besides that, equated the ruling of his bankruptcy with the ruling of his death, making the owner of the goods in both more entitled to them than the rest of the creditors. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distinguished between them, making the ruling in them different, upon what we mentioned in the hadith of al-Zubaydi from al-Zuhri, and in the hadith of Abd al-Razzaq from Malik from al-Zuhri. Al-Shafi'i used to argue for his equating bankruptcy and death in that with what..."
"5400","4609 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Fudayk informed us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, who said: Abu al-Mu'tamir ibn Amr ibn Rafi' narrated to me, from Ibn Khaldah al-Zuraqi - he was a judge - that he said: We came to Abu Hurayrah about a companion of ours who went bankrupt. He said: This is what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged in: Any man who dies or goes bankrupt, the owner of the goods is more entitled to his goods."
